HT_DATA.load("detail", {"SxdOUGdseq4":{"wy":"HN treats it as the canonical talk on software complexity, shared constantly and recommended to every programmer regardless of language.","q":{"x":"is a must-watch (even if you never touch Clojure)","i":34050492,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45345688,"author":"Lyngbakr","month":"2025-09","story":"Nine things I learned in ninety years","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A superb discussion of simplicity in the context of software.","quote":"Rich Hickey's Simple Made Easy # is a superb discussion about this in the context of software.","text":"I'm sure most folks here have seen the talk, but Rich Hickey's Simple Made Easy # is a superb discussion about this in the context of software. # [VIDEO]"},{"id":43995610,"author":"bad_haircut72","month":"2025-05","story":"Lua for Elixir","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rich Hickey on simple versus easy and judging technology beyond superficial ease.","quote":"You need to watch this","text":"You need to watch this [VIDEO] We should try not to make technical judgements based in flippant things like how few lines can someone not even familiar with the ecosystem get a hello world working"},{"id":42885418,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2025-01","story":"A better build system for OCaml","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clarifies complex versus merely difficult.","quote":"Every programmer who wants to use that word needs to watch this","text":"Also the point of software engineering is to take the real complexity that actually exists and you can't get rid of and work out how to write simple code to deal with it. Good software architecture does not include complexity because there is complexity in the business. It manages the complexity with simple code. The trouble is when people say \"complex\" you don't really know what they mean, though. They often just mean \"difficult\". Every programmer who wants to use that word needs to watch this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38557902,"author":"alanwreath","month":"2023-12","story":"Simplicity","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk the commenter has loved for ages.","quote":"a talk I’ve loved forever by Rich Hickey","text":"loved the take and re-enforcement of a talk I’ve loved forever by Rich Hickey (famous for being integral to the creation of Clojure among other things) called “Simple Made Easy” [VIDEO] Anyone know of any books that dive deeper into the subject?"},{"id":34050492,"author":"tincholio","month":"2022-12","story":"Most(LY Dead) Influential Programming Languages (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Must-watch talk on simple versus easy, beyond Clojure.","quote":"is a must-watch (even if you never touch Clojure)","text":"\"Simple made easy\" [0] by Rich Hickey is a must-watch (even if you never touch Clojure) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31548601,"author":"idunnoman","month":"2022-05","story":"Learnings from 5 years of tech startup code audits","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shaped the commenter's view that complexity is the worst sin.","quote":"I've come to believe that complexity is the worst sin we commit.","text":"As developers, I've come to believe that complexity is the worst sin we commit. Everything we talk about can be traced back to this issue. This is largely due to paying attention to Rich Hickey and learning Clojure. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48072535,"author":"jeffreygoesto","month":"2026-05","story":"Mythical Man Month","stance":"rec","why":"Classic talk whose first half is the philosophical part.","quote":"Simple made Easy\" is a nice add-on, the first half is the philosophical part and a classic.","text":"\"Simple made Easy\" is a nice add-on, the first half is the philosophical part and a classic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47247364,"author":"skeeter2020","month":"2026-03","story":"Nobody Gets Promoted for Simplicity","stance":"rec","why":"Defines simple versus complex and easy versus hard, with the etymology of simplex.","quote":"I think Rich Hickey's talk about simple is great for defining these terms (literally).","text":"I think Rich Hickey's talk about simple is great for defining these terms (literally). He describes how the roots of \"simplex\" mean single braid, which compares to the twisting & coupling with complexity; an apt visual for software development. He also differentiates simple/complex from easy/hard, which is important. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44444988,"author":"jimbokun","month":"2025-07","story":"I'm dialing back my LLM usage","stance":"rec","why":"Lessons on building simple components that LLM-generated code tends to forget.","quote":"I think LLMs have made a lot of developers forget the lessons in \"Simple Made Easy","text":"I think LLMs have made a lot of developers forget the lessons in \"Simple Made Easy\": [VIDEO] LLMs seem to be really good at reproducing the classic Ball of Mud, that can't really be refactored or understood. There's a lot of power in creating simple components that interact with other simple components to produce complex functionality. While each component is easy to understand and debug and predict its performance. The trick is to figure out how to decompose your complex problem into these simple components and their interactions. I suppose once LLMs get really good at that skill, will be when we really won't need developers any more."},{"id":44150265,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2025-06","story":"Figma Slides Is a Beautiful Disaster","stance":"rec","why":"Exemplary presentation style, revealing one bullet point at a time.","quote":"Simple made easy\" considered by many to be one of the greatest ever","text":"> They just don’t get it. What comprises a good presentation. I take it one could argue between what's a presentation and a talk but to me one of the very best presentation I've seen is Rich Hickey presenting Clojure. Then there's \"Simple made easy\" considered by many to be one of the greatest ever: [VIDEO] There are many bullet points: but he'll go through each them and only show the next bullet point once he's done with the previous one. He even kids about a made up graph on one of the slide. I think that what makes a good presentation depends a lot on what's being presented."},{"id":42098425,"author":"chamomeal","month":"2024-11","story":"Zig's (.{}){} Syntax","stance":"rec","why":"Commonly shared Rich Hickey talk worth seeing.","quote":"I know this gets shared all the time, but in case anybody in this thread hasn’t seen the rich hickey talk","text":"I know this gets shared all the time, but in case anybody in this thread hasn’t seen the rich hickey talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40546103,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2024-06","story":"Show HN: I built a backend so simple that it fits in a YAML file","stance":"rec","why":"Distinguishes simple (how built) from easy (how used).","quote":"They are orthogonal, so a system can be both complex and easy, both simple and hard.","text":"A simple system is built so that not many parts are intertwined and hard to understand how it is built. A easy system might be very complexly built, but presents and easy interface for you to use. If complex it would be hard to understand how it is built and how to modify. Basically simple describes how the system is built, while easy describes how much you need to know to use it. They are orthogonal, so a system can be both complex and easy, both simple and hard. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":36321897,"author":"weatherlight","month":"2023-06","story":"Teach yourself Computer Science functionally","stance":"rec","why":"Dives into simple versus easy and implicit state.","quote":"There’s this really great talk about this idea by rich hickey","text":"Keeping track of all that state in one’s head is hard. I think because of the limitations of FP it’s much easier for new programmers to reason about programs and honestly, learn to program. New developers don’t have to micromanage the computer, the way that the would with an imperative lang. For example, SQL isn’t FP, but think about the number of business analysts whose lives would be more difficult if the had to write imperative programs to fetch data from some sort of data store. I think what in comes down to is simple vs easy. Imperative programming in the very very beginning is easy, buts not simple. Because there’s all this implicit state in your program, as the program grows it becomes very very complex. It’s no longer simple. There’s this really great talk about this idea by rich hickey, the creator of clojure, that dives into this in detail. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34971797,"author":"hinkley","month":"2023-02","story":"Do the Simplest Thing That Can Possibly Work (2004)","stance":"rec","why":"Classic talk on simple versus easy.","quote":"Obligatory Simple Made Easy link","text":"Obligatory Simple Made Easy link: [VIDEO] Simple is a matter of intuition, and that can't be transmitted to others easily, or with a single class or book. At one particular job we got punished by the business for calling things 'easy' when what we mean is that we understand the problem and all of the steps are (mostly) known. Our boss coached the hell out of us to say 'straightforward' when we meant 'understood', instead of using 'easy' as an antonym for 'quagmire' or 'scary'."},{"id":45660661,"author":"zahlman","month":"2025-10","story":"Solving the Wrong Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey explains how things become complex when you complect them.","quote":"things become complex because you complect them","text":"I would argue that nowadays the libraries and frameworks are more to blame than the languages. We aren't creating the right elegant DSLs. But more importantly we're still expecting people to inherit from library classes — perhaps multiple levels deep — to swap functionality in and out. We could just use functions as first-class objects but instead we have to understand this unnatural intermingling of code and data that's based on \"modeling\" a supposed concept in the problem domain (rather than the solution domain) that barely makes any sense in and of itself. Like Rich Hickey explained ( [VIDEO] ), things become complex because you complect them. You can get a lot of APL / LISP feeling out of carefully written Python or JavaScript. Not the metaprogramming stuff, sure. But a lot of that in LISP depends on homoiconicity, which is one of the biggest things making the language \"not usable by average developers\"."},{"id":44317156,"author":"pramodbiligiri","month":"2025-06","story":"The Grug Brained Developer (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's talk on not intertwining things that can be kept separate.","quote":"For those wondering what this refers to, here is his talk","text":"Haha. But I thought Rich Hickey was making the simple point that don't intertwine things than can be kept separate! P.S: For those wondering what this refers to, here is his talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43994609,"author":"TexanFeller","month":"2025-05","story":"Working on complex systems: What I learned working at Google","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey on easy versus simple and essential complexity.","quote":"Rich Hickey is famous for talking about easy vs. simple/complex and essential vs. incidental complexity.","text":"Rich Hickey is famous for talking about easy vs. simple/complex and essential vs. incidental complexity. “Simple Made Easy”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43179217,"author":"Jach","month":"2025-02","story":"Clean Code vs. A Philosophy Of Software Design","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the complecting concept.","quote":"You might say you are \"complecting\" things together","text":"Complexity: things twisted together. You can count the things, and count the twists. When a set of things has fewer twists (or even knots) than another set of things, it's simpler. When you pull on something, if it's attached to other things by twists, you are dealing with complexity. When you intentionally entwine things, you are creating complexity. You might say you are \"complecting\" things together, and once done they are \"complected\" together. This is relevant from the smallest details of programming like state (being a more complex twist of value and time, compared to simpler immutable values that are timeless) to the largest issues of modularity (being a property of systems composed of smaller things; when you can disconnect such things without needing to untwist them from each other, you have achieved a simpler design). This is separate from being easy or hard, though one could assert that a simpler system will tend to be easier to change, because you don't necessarily have to deal with as many things twisted together at the same time. But this isn't a given, because we programmers learn and get better at complex things such that they can feel quite easy, and we also love making tools to try and wrangle sources of complexity, either those inherent to a problem domain, or those we unnecessarily inflict on ourselves, and it can be quite easy to make changes to really complex systems once you've learned some of these tools. Complex things can also be very helpful from ti"},{"id":40353385,"author":"kaliszad","month":"2024-05","story":"Using ARG in a Dockerfile – beware the gotcha","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the distinction when easy beats simple.","quote":"Another time when easy won over simple.","text":"That is what you get when people reinvent the wheel and lifetimes/ scopes are implicit. Docker could've used something like JSON5 [0] for their configuration format to make the lifetimes explicit. Another time when easy won over simple. [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35961425,"author":"lbayes","month":"2023-05","story":"Digital Audio Workstation Front End Development Struggles","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the braided, complected notion of complexity.","quote":"","text":"Building user experiences is inherently some of the most difficult software engineering there is. It's not difficult because the existing tools are poorly made (though most of them are), it's difficult because the core problem is extremely complex (in the Rich Hickey [1] sense of the word, e.g., braided, intertwined, complected, etc.). In order to build a user experience, we start with some information that can often (though certainly not always) appear both simple and easy (i.e., unbraided and comprehensible). But then we must create a projection of that information onto a 2D (or 3D) plane and manage it's changing state over time. This is where everything falls apart. We're usually trying to manage a very wide, very deep, loosely defined tree of state and we're trying to reflect an arbitrary number of user interactions and mutations instantly, and over time. Exactly zero of our tools (programming languages, UI toolkits, Operating Systems, etc.) have strong support for managing change-over-time in a way that makes this type of problem fast, stable or enjoyable to present, use, verify or mutate. I love that the OP showed the triangle of Performance, Adaptability and Velocity. I've been framing a similar concept with teams since 2011 which I refer to as, \"Fast, Stable and Delightful.\" It's my thesis that we need to balance these three requirements in both our activities and in the output that we produce. This balance is incredibly difficult to achieve and harder still to mainta"}],"r":[["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["NcUJnmBqHTY",1],["o_AIw9bGogo",1],["h47zZrqjgLc",1],["mbEfzuCLoAQ",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["NH1Tta7purM",1],["-I_jE0l7sYQ",1],["aSEQfqNYNAc",1],["PQmSUHhP3ug",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["_YWMGuh15nE",1],["fwcl17Q0bpk",1],["aokNwKx7gM8",1],["AnZ0uTOerUI",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["EK32jo7i5LQ",1],["gSKTfG1GXYQ",1],["O61WihMRdjM",1],["EZ05e7EMOLM",1]]},"5f9R9MtkpqM":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly say it changed how they hear political speech and recommend it for understanding why opponents are not simply stupid.","q":{"x":"Changed how I listen - to anyone - forever.","i":26361333,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41932946,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite YouTube video, ever?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Instant understanding of political language and identity politics.","quote":"Instantaneous understanding of political language and identity politics.","text":"Russ Olsen, author of Getting Closure, Eloquent Ruby and Design Patterns with Ruby, telling the story of the moon landing as a conference talk. Inspired me beyond measure and brought tears to my eyes. [LINK] —- George Lakoff’s lecture on Moral Politics. Instantaneous understanding of political language and identity politics. Incredibly important, especially today in time of extreme polarization. [VIDEO] —- Sir Ken Robinson: Do Schools Kill Creativity [LINK]"},{"id":30964884,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2022-04","story":"The true purpose of propaganda","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Addresses nonsensical speech, metaphors and framing in political media.","quote":"Changed how I listen to mass media and political speech.","text":"In this lecture on Moral Politics, George Lakoff addresses the “nonsensical” speech, metaphors, framing and more. Changed how I listen to mass media and political speech. [VIDEO] Also highly recommend his Truth Samndwich methodology to defanging propaganda messages in the media. [LINK]"},{"id":26361333,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2021-03","story":"Framing 101","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on framing and metaphors in political discourse.","quote":"Changed how I listen - to anyone - forever.","text":"I highly recommend Lakoff’s lecture on framing and metaphors in political speech and discourse. Changed how I listen - to anyone - forever. [VIDEO] His framelab podcast is also excellent."},{"id":21476613,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2019-11","story":"You can fool some of the people, all of the time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changes how you hear political speech targeted at other orientations.","quote":"It will change how you listen to political speech","text":"May I recommend this talk on the neuroscience of language and thought by George Lakoff? [LINK] And also this one on political language, morals and metaphors? It will change how you listen to political speech, and especially political speech targeting people with different political orientation -- which can often sound nonsensical, but is actually carefully constructed to activate their metaphors. [VIDEO] Basically, the ideas of enlightenment (we are rational beings) are not true. A couple of slides from the first lecture: Myth: Reason is conscious, you know what you think. Science: Reason is mostly unconscious, estimates are about 98% unconscious. Conscious thinking is linear. Brain circuitry is massively parallel. Myth: Ideas are made meaningful via their direct connections to the external world. Meaning is a matter of truth conditions. Science: Ideas are made meaningful via the brain's connections to the body and our embodied experience. Myth: Emotion gets in the way of rationality. Science: Emotion is necessary for rationality."},{"id":21161031,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: What are your arguments in favor of end-to-end encryption?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exposes leverage hooks politicians use to manipulate audiences.","quote":"You will never listen/read political speech the same.","text":"His framelab podcasts are informal and easily digestable. [LINK] I highly recommend * truth sandwich time (if you only listen to one make it this one) * how republicans really think (if you only listen to one make it this one, too) * how trump uses twitter to control the media (very eye opening) * guns over people (on how to frame the gun debate) Also, this lecture on political language exposes the leverage hooks politicians and propagandists use to hook and manipulate us. You will never listen/read political speech the same. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13871705,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2017-03","story":"Why Are the Highly Educated So Liberal? (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Linguistic analysis of conservative versus liberal speech and metaphors.","quote":"I found it extremely valuable in understanding the driving and defining forces behind the two movements.","text":"George Lakoff did an excellent linguistic analysis of conservative vs. liberal political speech, metaphors and framing in his lecture Moral Politics. I found it extremely valuable in understanding the driving and defining forces behind the two movements. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11982169,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2016-06","story":"Reasons not to use (i.e., be used by) Facebook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains think-tank framing with metaphors to influence politics.","quote":"Changed how I process political speech and pretty much any media-supported narrative.","text":"I think you'll enjoy this lecture by linguistics professor George Lakoff: [VIDEO] Among other things he speaks of the think-tank-driven concerted effort to influence political support by using language invoking specific metaphors to frame important issues. Like \"Tax Relief\" for dramatically reducing taxes for the rich etc. Changed how I process political speech and pretty much any media-supported narrative."},{"id":10878642,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2016-01","story":"The “Other Side” Is Not Dumb","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how metaphors from upbringing shape political worldviews.","quote":"This talk changed my view on political speech and narrative dramatically.","text":"I highly recommend the Moral Politics talk by Linguistics professor George Lakoff: [VIDEO] In short, the two sides of the debate speak different language representing different world views rooted in upbringing and triggered / exploited by metaphors. This talk changed my view on political speech and narrative dramatically."},{"id":18983826,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-01","story":"Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez Is Absolutely Right About Racist Algorithms","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of how language establishes social hierarchy.","quote":"For a good explanation of how this works, George Lakoff's lecture","text":"The right[2] looks at intent because that's what matters in a hierarchical view of society. This division is from a fundamental difference in what societies are. This can be observed din how they use language; from one of the most enlightening articles[1] I've ever read: > One of the biggest problems of the entire Culture Wars is that people like us [the left[2]] use language impart information. We usually are not aware that a nice big chunk of population does not use language in that way at all. Their use of language is that of Phatic Language [...] In a hierarchical society [the right[2]], language is [often] not used for exchange of information [...] It is used to establish social hierarchy. For a good explanation of how this works, George Lakoff's lecture[3] \"Moral Politics\". [1] [LINK] [2] The \"left\"/\"right\" labels are being use in a general psychological sense, which doesn't always match the political groups with the same names. [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17102807,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2018-05","story":"Congress wants to extend the copyright on some sound recordings to 144 years","stance":"rec","why":"Reveals how political language uses metaphors and framing to win debates.","quote":"For more on framing, see George Lakoff’s lecture on moral politics","text":"This would be a great way to prevent polititians from framing the bills. For more on framing, see George Lakoff’s lecture on moral politics — reveals the way political language uses metaphors and How framing a debate ensures its outcome. Repeating the frame reinforces the intended meaning. Merely Using the name of the bill defines its outcome. “Tax Relief” - no one wants to fight a “relief” effort. Tax cuts for the rich would be the correct name. “Clear sky initiative” is the name of a clear cutting bill. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12767597,"author":"TheSpiceIsLife","month":"2016-10","story":"It’s official: Venezuela is a dictatorship","stance":"rec","why":"Treats Left and Right as having different moral experiences.","quote":"George Lakoff give's this a fairly good treatment in this","text":"You've probably seen this, but for those who haven't George Lakoff give's this a fairly good treatment in this[1] on 'Moral Politics'. By 'this' I mean the idea that the Left and the Right have different experiences of what is moral. My issue with politics is that I think we need to extricate it from morality. Like we have separation of Church and State a la Secular Society, we need amoral politics. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12252268,"author":"TheSpiceIsLife","month":"2016-08","story":"How to Give Rural America Broadband? Look to the Early 1900s","stance":"rec","why":"Opponents have different morals and rationales, not stupidity.","quote":"people who have different political opinions to you aren't necessarily stupid or greedy or bad actors","text":"On that note, I found this talk on Moral Politics by George Lakoff[1] elsewhere on HN. The basic idea is that people who have different political opinions to you aren't necessarily stupid or greedy or bad actors, they just have different morals and therefore a different set of rationales. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11982200,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2016-06","story":"Reasons not to use (i.e., be used by) Facebook","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why language is important and influential.","quote":"You may want to take a look at this lecture to see why language is so important and influential.","text":"He refuses to use the commonly established language which distorts reality. It is a radical choice (given how well established the manipulative language is). I can see how some may find this irritating. You may want to take a look at this lecture to see why language is so important and influential. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14577909,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-06","story":"Antarctica Is Melting, and Giant Ice Cracks Are Just the Start","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue learning people's language is needed to persuade.","quote":"first you have to open a real dialog","text":"TL;DR - skip straight to [1] > I'm not sure it is possible to convince someone It is possible, but first you have to open a real dialog. That isn't going to happen without first learning to speak their language , because this is \"More than just Resistance to Science\"[1]. > Only new facts will do the trick Arguments based on facts are useful when trying to persuade people fluent in the language of science and logic. Unfortunately I suspect only a minority of the population has sufficient understanding of data-focused language. Most people instead see language as phatic expression [2][3] first. A good example of this from the other side of the language divide that still retains mutual intelligibility are Trump's repeated - and often self-contradictory - promises that he would \"build the wall\". People that primarily use language to convey facts often claims his rhetoric sounds ignorant, useless, or xenophobic. Trump's intended audience, on the other hand, hears the language of emotion that promises protection. Trump isn't trying to convey facts , so self-contradiction and other ignorant claims are not important. To reach people on the other side of this divide, it is probably necessary to first learn their language of social hierarchy, emotion, etc. Skipping that step and continuing to throw facts at them is about as useful as sending them a textbook written in ancient Egyptian hieroglyphs. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] While I'm discussing this as a language problem, some people ma"},{"id":12230164,"author":"TheSpiceIsLife","month":"2016-08","story":"To Cut Down Poverty, Cut Down the Cost of Living","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for progressive versus conservative moral divide.","quote":"which side of the argument a person lands on probably depends on their moral politics","text":"I tend to lean toward agreeing with you, by the way, but the other side of this argument is: We already know that giving money and food to poor people doesn't resolve poverty, when the aid / social welfare stops the poverty returns because aid / welfare prevents people from innovating your way out of poverty. Of course, which side of the argument a person lands on probably depends on their moral politics, see[1] - basically I'm referring to the progressive vs. conservative divide. Fortunately for me I live in Australia where we have social welfare to the tune of $527.60 a fortnight for a single adult[2], and $952.80 for a couple. But of course we still have bunches of poverty, but I've never been to the US so I can't really say how it compares. Also, I think the example of the Nordic countries is a poor comparison. The 'Nordic Countries* as a whole have a population of 26.6 million, comparable to Australia. Sweden has a population of about 10 million, the others are close to half that or much less. In a country that has a population the size of Melbourne (4.5 million) or Sydney (4.9 million) I would imagine it would be a lot easier to want to care for other people. I think that matters a lot. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"PUv66718DII":{"wy":"It is HN's go-to recommendation for the case that programming environments should give instant feedback, and it appears in nearly every thread on live coding, visual programming or developer tools.","q":{"x":"My mind was blown when I saw this video 2 years ago","i":7835383,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":38840760,"author":"pedrovhb","month":"2024-01","story":"What I've learned about flow fields so far","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Real-time feedback on parameters reveals hidden behavior.","quote":"an absolute must watch, if anyone hasn't yet","text":"Nice! Playing around with the widget under the section \"Experimenting with lines\" I'm reminded of Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle talk [0] (an absolute must watch, if anyone hasn't yet). In particular, changing the smoothness reveals a sort of scaling effect that I'd probably never know about if not playing around with sliders and having it update in real time rather than setting individual values. Very interesting and beautiful! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31784835,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Better than most product pitches for dev tools.","quote":"Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle is even better","text":"Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle is even better (albeit it isn't only a product pitch): [VIDEO]"},{"id":16483447,"author":"rpeden","month":"2018-02","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspires rethinking development tools and approaches.","quote":"Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle talk[1] is one of the best I've seen.","text":"I think most here have already seen it, but I find that Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle talk[1] is one of the best I've seen. I re-watch it every so often to help prevent my thinking from falling into a rut. When developing software as a career, it's so easy to just fall into a pattern where you use the same tools and techniques over and over again. Watching this talk this talk inspires me to investigate alternate approaches to developing and delivering software. It's easy to think of software development as something that always need to involve code, and nothing but code. But watching this talk inspires me to go and try things like Node Red and Unreal Blueprints. In both cases, code is usually still involved, but they both offer a visual way of connecting components. I find it helpful to be able to visualize software this way so I can immediate understand how data flows through the application. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8106148,"author":"productionQA","month":"2014-07","story":"Ask HN: Favorite talks [video] on software development?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Top inspirational talk about software development.","quote":"it doesn't get much better than this","text":"Probably not exactly what you need, but as far as inspiration is concerned, it doesn't get much better than this: [VIDEO] Check out Bret Victor's talks in general (worrydream.com). He is quite interesting."},{"id":7835383,"author":"bigsassy","month":"2014-06","story":"Introducing Swift","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing talk on live programming feedback.","quote":"My mind was blown when I saw this video 2 years ago","text":"Is this related to Bret Victor's stuff? My mind was blown when I saw this video 2 years ago, and I can't help but see similarities here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36798677,"author":"anon35","month":"2023-07","story":"MDN Playground","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates the potential of immediate feedback in programming.","quote":"Their potential is beautifully illustrated in Brett Victor's famous 'Inventing on Principle' talk","text":"Great to see. More generally: I feel like playgrounds are grossly underused in our industry. It's possible the issue is in the name -- it immediately connotes that whatever you're doing isn't real work, it's just a place to, well, \"play\". But really these kind of playgrounds are at least a cousin, if not a direct descendant, of the immediate feedback principle that underlies everything from a REPL to the kind of systems (Symbolics, etc) that allow you to edit running code. Their potential is beautifully illustrated in Brett Victor's famous 'Inventing on Principle' talk: [VIDEO] Three areas that I'd love to see playgrounds mature beyond teaching beginners CSS: 1) Integrated directly into developer documentation. You can see some of that in systems that are documenting services -- for example, the Google Sheets API documentation has a 'Try this Method' box that lets you post a request to the service on the same page that describes it, but what I really want is something like that when I'm reading the man page of a destructive disk command, or a complex database query, or a low-level C graphics library. That'll require a playground wired to something as big as a VM, maybe. 2) Integrated into textbooks: for the last couple years the astonishing posts from Bartosz Ciechanowski ([Sound]( [LINK] ), [Bicyle]( [LINK] )) have demonstrated just how much better a textbook could be if you could interactively play with the concepts you're reading about. Unlike dev docs, the thing you're in"},{"id":26070876,"author":"ryanmentor","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: How do you generate higher-quality ideas?","stance":"rec","why":"On working from principles to reach new ideas.","quote":"","text":"Time and Time and Space and Space are necessary for high quality creativity : [LINK] and working with solid principles, from first principles, is how you reach durable new understandings. IMO. The Seven Habits of Highly Effective People has some great material about the use of principles in being personally creative. Inventing on Principle: [VIDEO] I serve as a creativity coach to builders, innovators, and founders. Sometimes what a thinker needs more than anything else is a BREAK to BATHE! and then... EUREKA! [LINK]"},{"id":23725844,"author":"bhntr3","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: Did you take any fresh MOOCs as of 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Mind-expanding content for programming and design.","quote":"Bret Victor ( ) or Rich Hickey ( ) for programming and design.","text":"In terms of mind-expanding or intellectually stimulating, I find it's easier to get that from Youtube. I recommend: 3blue1brown for math ( [LINK] ) Everyday Astronaut if you are casually interested in rockets ( [LINK] ) Bret Victor ( [VIDEO] ) or Rich Hickey ( [LINK] ) for programming and design. Doing a full college course is such a time investment and it usually takes a few weeks to start proving the interesting stuff. I find it more fun to look to shorter content for inspiration. I might realize I don't understand the fundamentals of the topic and then seek out a way to study it."},{"id":23160478,"author":"gervwyk","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Are React-like UI frameworks a fad or a genuine step forward?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of the excellent talk by bret victor","text":"Bringing two concepts closer, especially visually will result in a better developer experience - Thus merging html and js into a single file, component, class / object and sequential logic was really the turning point to build more complex web apps. This reminds me of the excellent talk by bret victor [VIDEO]"},{"id":23255489,"author":"fossuser","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Why isn’t visual programming a bigger thing?","stance":"rec","why":"Good examples of valuable visual programming tools.","quote":"You’d probably like Bret Victor’s inventing on principle talk","text":"You’d probably like Bret Victor’s inventing on principle talk: [VIDEO] He demonstrates some good examples. I’m not quite sure it’s what you mean by visual, but it seems obviously valuable and tools that enabled some of what he shows would be really useful. I’m not sure why we don’t have these things - I think Alan Kay would say it’s because we stopped doing real research to improve what our tools can be. We’re just relying on old paradigms (I just watched this earlier today: [LINK] )"},{"id":19679563,"author":"bch","month":"2019-04","story":"Panic’s Next Editor","stance":"rec","why":"Victor urges targeting what is important with intention.","quote":"while Victor is explicitly saying make it count by way of targeting with intention what’s important to you.","text":"Nice, but I think I can raise you. That talk mostly seemed pretty predictable (though I incorrectly guessed gfx via sixels[0], not a(nother) terminal emulator). If your interest was piqued with that parent video though, prepare to have your mind blown with this[1], who’s thesis maybe similar in the sense of “stop _just_ simply working”. Bernhardt seems to be saying don’t be afraid to take time and think deeply and “go deep”, while Victor is explicitly saying make it count by way of targeting with intention what’s important to you. A sort of “fight the good fight.” [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10644002,"author":"DanielRibeiro","month":"2015-11","story":"The Utopian UI Architect","stance":"rec","why":"Innovative ideas on programming and design.","quote":"Bret does have some pretty innovate ideas on Programming and Design (and more).","text":"You may not consider him a genius. But Bret does have some pretty innovate ideas on Programming and Design (and more). Good examples: Inventing on Principle: [VIDEO] The Humane Representation of Thought: [LINK]"},{"id":43236394,"author":"sitkack","month":"2025-03","story":"How Flash games shaped the video game industry (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Having never used the authoring tool, can you point me towards something to model it after? I think a Bret Victor, \"Inventing on Principal\" style editor would be perfect, combined with some sort of scratch like Python IDE where each element is defined in terms of its reactive behaviors with other elements on a timeline. I didn't say easy, I said not too difficult. Order of magnitude difference. :) [VIDEO] With some [LINK]"},{"id":43382860,"author":"kaycebasques","month":"2025-03","story":"Our interfaces have lost their senses","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks Inventing On Principle: [VIDEO] A couple other Bret Victor resources I gleaned from other comments: Humane Representation Of Thought: [LINK] Dynamicland: [LINK]"},{"id":31472553,"author":"mredigonda","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: What developer tools would you like to see?","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of live variable values while writing functions.","quote":"Basically as you are writing a function, you can write sample input for it","text":"This video [0] is from February 2012, but as far as I know this tool is not yet implemented (or at least it is not widely used). Basically as you are writing a function, you can write sample input for it, and it tells you the intermediate value of each variable inside this function, which lets you reason about the code, makes it trivial to know you've done something wrong, and would make developing a much more interactive experience overall. In my experience, this is done by hand when writing a function, but a tool like this would make it automatic. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18914627,"author":"golangMonkey","month":"2019-01","story":"Ask HN: Why are we still using text editors to write code?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"i like the idea from \"inventing on principle","text":"i guess voice is not an alternative but i thinking of visualising program flow could work. i like the idea from \"inventing on principle\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":16117668,"author":"borplk","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What would your ideal programming language be like?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think the next game-changing evolution in programming languages is moving beyond plaintext. Our basic building blocks of programming has not changed essentially since the beginning of programming. We still treat programs as nothing but a bunch of files and folders edited with the same tools that writers use to write romance novels (yeah yeah \"intellisense\" is not so intelli at the end of the day). We don't create beautiful images by writing RGB values in a text editor. We shouldn't be creating highly complex software systems with them either. We need model based editing environments that will allow us to have a much richer set of software building blocks. We have to allow software to be more than just characters in files. Software \"source\" is not a book. It's the blueprint for a system that has metadata and constraints attached to it. It can't comfortably and forever exist in a flat plaintext structure for human consumption. If you are interested go dig up my old comments I have written more elaborate rants about it before. And yes I'm ready for the inevitable and customary \"NO IT AINT GON WORK WE TRIED BEFORE AND IT AINT WORTH TRYIN EVER EVER AGAIN\" replies. Also relevant: [LINK] (I actually somewhat accidentally and indirectly caused the creation of the subreddit as a result of a discussion here on HN before) [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15244098,"author":"hchasestevens","month":"2017-09","story":"Artificial Intelligence Uses Videogame Footage to Recreate Game Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Hand-animated interactive authoring demo.","quote":"","text":"Imagine this paired with a system like the one Bret Victor demos in \"Inventing on Principle\" [0] or in \"Stop Drawing Dead Fish\" [1], in which the user hand-animates what they want the game movement/overall gameplay to look like, adjusting as necessary. The system described in this paper could get you a decent way from that hand-animated mockup to a working game that looks and feels first and foremost like you intended, rather than working from a playable-but-bad-feeling prototype game engine and having to endlessly adjust to get the game feel \"right\". [0]. [VIDEO] [1]. [LINK]"},{"id":13631545,"author":"DonaldFisk","month":"2017-02","story":"Show HN: DeepUI Programming Studio – A different approach to programming","stance":"reference","why":"Tight coupling between program and its output.","quote":"It's similar to some of Bret Victor's ideas","text":"No, they explain that LabVIEW and other visual languages are just different ways of representing the code. The idea here, I think, is that there's far greater coupling between the output of the program and the program itself. It's similar to some of Bret Victor's ideas: [VIDEO] I'd like to incorporate this coupling idea into my own visual dataflow language ( [LINK] ), but haven't yet decided how to implement it. My approach has been to design the language from the bottom-up, so that simple programs can be simply drawn, and there are higher level programming constructs which simplify more complex code, avoiding the complexity problem (the Deutsch limit) you've seen with LabVIEW."},{"id":6164849,"author":"joeblau","month":"2013-08","story":"Webflow (YC S13) Lets You Design Responsive Websites Visually, Publish Instantly","stance":"reference","why":"Bret Victor's real-time feedback editor idea.","quote":"An editor like the one Bret Victor talked about in his \"Inventing on Principle\" talk[1].","text":"I love the design and the concept of the site as well as others in this space like jetstrap, easel, and divshot. To be honest, I've tried a few of these offerings and as a web developer this is not what I want, I'm looking for two things: 1. An editor like the one Bret Victor talked about in his \"Inventing on Principle\" talk[1]. I want to edit HTML and CSS and see updates reflected in real time[2]. 2. A way to hook this into a DVCS (Or better yet, a desktop application/desktop plugin). My development workflow relies heavily on Git and I don't think I'm going to download a zip payload and extract it every time I need to do a commit. [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK]"}],"r":[["bmSAYlu0NcY",2],["oLiheMQayNE",1],["HQYsFshbkYw",1],["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["-pKR212H5vQ",1],["swQbA4zub20",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["3qln2u1Vr2E",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1],["nqzpAbK9qFk",1],["7R4xhwy-1oI",1],["wUwZZaI5u0c",1],["uBh2LxTW0s0",1]]},"jB0vBmiTr6o":{"wy":"Held up as the landmark demoscene production and a counterpoint to software bloat.","q":{"x":"It demonstrated that 4k was more than sufficient for impressive effects and music","i":22882595,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":44059915,"author":"onename","month":"2025-05","story":"Inigo Quilez: computer graphics, mathematics, shaders, fractals, demoscene","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Showcase of what a 4 kB intro can do, winner of Breakpoint 2009.","quote":"It’s truly amazing what can be done in just 4 kilobyte!","text":"When I want to show people what an intro is and tell them a bit about the demoscene, I usually show them the intro Elevated, which won the PC 4k compo at Breakpoint 2009. For me it really shows the talent of Iq and the other people who created it. It’s truly amazing what can be done in just 4 kilobyte! Elevated by Rgba & TBC: [VIDEO] Iq's slides on the Elevated intro: [LINK] Sourcecode: [LINK]"},{"id":22882595,"author":"duskwuff","month":"2020-04","story":"Finland adds the demoscene as a UNESCO intangible world cultural heritage","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Landmark 4k demo that reshaped the demoscene.","quote":"It demonstrated that 4k was more than sufficient for impressive effects and music","text":"The 4k category was blown wide open in 2009 by rgba/tbc with Elevated [1] (live reactions: [2]). It demonstrated that 4k was more than sufficient for impressive effects and music -- suddenly, 64k was no longer an interesting constraint. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":21643150,"author":"twic","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: Who are your programming rockstars, and why?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Four-kilobyte demo by a standout demoscene programmer.","quote":"Inigo Quilez, a demoscene programmer who does amazing work","text":"Inigo Quilez, a demoscene programmer who does amazing work - of a very high technical and artistic standard, often in painfully tiny sizes - and then writes lucidly about it: [LINK] Here's 'Elevated' a four-kilobyte demo he did (along with others): [VIDEO] And here's the writeup: [LINK]"},{"id":14397219,"author":"userbinator","month":"2017-05","story":"How a 64k intro is made","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing 4KB demo by authors of distance function pages.","quote":"one of the authors of this amazing 4k","text":"Here is another page on distance functions... [LINK] ...written by one of the authors of this amazing 4k ( four kilobytes! ) demo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13056925,"author":"M4v3R","month":"2016-11","story":"Making graphics in 4 kilobytes (2008) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"4KB animated 3D demo with music.","quote":"achieving something like this in 4k is just mindblowing.","text":"Obligatory link to Elevated - a 4 kilobytes animated 3D demo with music by rgba (authors of PDF in the OP) & TBC: [VIDEO] For me, achieving something like this in 4k is just mindblowing."},{"id":11848122,"author":"jsheard","month":"2016-06","story":"Source code for 4kb demoscene production “Elevated” released","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Everything is procedurally generated by a 4096-byte executable.","quote":"It still blows my mind 7 years after release...","text":"Release info: [LINK] Binary: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO] Everything you see and hear is procedurally generated by the 4096 byte executable, in real time. It still blows my mind 7 years after release..."},{"id":9398012,"author":"userbinator","month":"2015-04","story":"Code Inflation [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Realtime demo from a 4KB executable contrasts with bloat.","quote":"are recommended to watch this demo generated in realtime by a 4KB executable","text":"Those who don't see anything unusual or wasteful about an 8KB+ executable whose only action is to exit and return a value are recommended to watch this demo generated in realtime by a 4KB executable: [VIDEO] There's more here: [LINK] The demoscene is basically the exact opposite of mainstream software culture, and although it's focused on multimedia shows, I think some of their techniques and underlying motivation could be applied more generally... I wonder if /bin/true and /bin/false in their most simplest forms even meet the minimum \"requirement for creativity\" to be copyrightable, and if the reason why it has bloated significantly is so that it could be."},{"id":18778536,"author":"userbinator","month":"2018-12","story":"“Modern” C++ Lamentations","stance":"rec","why":"Realtime audiovisual demo in a 4096-byte executable.","quote":"everything you see and hear is generated in realtime by a 4096-byte executable","text":"The size comparisons are also very useful, if anything to show that even the simple \"non-Modern\" C++ program already has quite a bit of bloat. Compilation takes 0.064 seconds, produces 8480 byte executable I am experienced with Asm so I can come up with an instinctive order-of-magnitude estimate, but even those who aren't should try to roughly estimate how many machine instructions are required to implement that code, and come to the conclusion that over 8000 bytes for three nested loops with not much in them, a comparison, some arithmetic, and a handful of library function calls seems rather excessive. Even 800 would be on the high side --- somewhere around 100-200 bytes is my estimate. Also, watch this 4k demo (everything you see and hear is generated in realtime by a 4096-byte executable): [VIDEO]"},{"id":15773902,"author":"userbinator","month":"2017-11","story":"Terrain rendering in fewer than 20 lines of code","stance":"rec","why":"One of the best 4K demos from 2009.","quote":"not very many 64Ks have reached the amazingness","text":"IMHO not very many 64Ks have reached the amazingness of this 11 minute one from 2000: [LINK] ...and for 4K, this one from 2009: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9578569,"author":"david-given","month":"2015-05","story":"Voxel Quest Fluid Dynamics and New Rendering Method","stance":"rec","why":"Iq's 4kB demo Elevated.","quote":"responsible for a number of amazing demos","text":"The maths isn't hard, but you do need a decent grasp of vectors and (optionally) 3D transformation matrices, and it's pretty opaque until you figure out the terminology. I haven't done GPU raymarching myself but I've used the same techniques on the CPU side in order to develop a whole-planet procedural renderer (in Ada, of course); the raymarching was relatively straightforward but I got horribly bogged down trying to make a volumetric atmosphere work. One resource I found really useful was Iñigo Quilez' website; in particular, his article on basic terrain raymarching explains most of the concepts: [LINK] (Also known as iq, he's responsible for a number of amazing demos, including the 4kB demo _Elevated_: [VIDEO] He also appears to have written ShaderToy. Search for iq to see his stuff omfg ShaderToy lets you write shaders to produce _music_ now? [LINK] )"},{"id":29817581,"author":"pezezin","month":"2022-01","story":"Sonic 3D: Director's Cut (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"A 4 kB demo generating an impressive mountain landscape.","quote":"It is actually 4 kB, even more impressive","text":"> 64Kb demo generating really good mountain landscape. Do you mean Elevated? It is actually 4 kB, even more impressive: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":15059471,"author":"userbinator","month":"2017-08","story":"IOCCC Flight Simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Comparable 4 kilobyte demo for flight sim discussion.","quote":"","text":"I am reminded of this 4 kilobyte demo: [VIDEO] (Source code and discussion at [LINK] ; explanation at [LINK] ) although the flight sim is a little more interesting from a technical perspective, since it's interactive and also not using the GPU to do most of the computation. Also, despite the source code being obfuscated, observe that that \"external symbols\" which are still visible, e.g. XDrawLine, XSetForeground, etc. already give a pretty good overview of how it does what it does --- and in general, when reverse-engineering or analysing software, inspecting where the \"black box\" interacts with the rest of the world is an important part of understanding it."},{"id":14618060,"author":"userbinator","month":"2017-06","story":"Reducing the size of iOS app binaries","stance":"reference","why":"Example of generating content at runtime in under 4096 bytes.","quote":"","text":"Switching out of the \"staticy\" background for a plain gray reduced the payload size to ~20M, a savings of ~30Mb. I know this might sound somewhat obvious in retrospect, but you could've retained something similar to the original background and reduced the size significantly by generating it at runtime --- the code to create a random-looking background may only be not more than a few dozen bytes, and can be the same for multiple resolutions. For more examples of this technique, see demoscene productions --- the following video was generated by a binary of less than 4096 bytes : [VIDEO] Source code: [LINK] Explanation: [LINK]"},{"id":13002848,"author":"userbinator","month":"2016-11","story":"Ken's Academy Award: Noise and Turbulence","stance":"reference","why":"Demo featuring Perlin noise and procedural techniques.","quote":"","text":"Perlin Noise and other procedural generation techniques also make frequent appearances in demoscene productions. [LINK] as featured in [VIDEO]"},{"id":5325115,"author":"zxcdw","month":"2013-03","story":"Real pixel coding (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Demo smaller than a basic icon.","quote":"contains less bytes than a basic 32-bit 32x32 pixel icon","text":"This is a great example of the point the guy is making: [LINK] That's 128 bytes. You could encode that as a 43 pixel RGB888 image. That's less than 7x7 pixel icon! Now as Jare mentioned in another comment, this video [VIDEO] contains less bytes than a basic 32-bit 32x32 pixel icon. Some of the whole essence of this demoscene stuff is to work within these constraints(256 bytes, 1024 bytes, 4096 bytes, 64 kbytes, ...) to produce this kind of art . Custom virtual machines, on-fly decompression algorithms and methods, carefully crafted data and ingenious ways of data re-use are the key. It's like black magic."}],"r":[["_YWMGuh15nE",1],["TQB0KK2rxcw",1],["Y3n3c_8Nn2Y",1],["yHXx3orN35Y",1],["5eo8nz_niiM",1],["gWVmPtr9O0g",1],["V2P8mjWRqpk",1],["7ttG90raCNo",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1],["uHQ4WCU1WQc",1],["QspuCt1FM9M",1],["hzGaO4Kdz2Y",1],["Nnuq9PXbywA",1],["aHun58mz3vI",1]]},"8--5LwHRhjk":{"wy":"HN repeatedly calls it a mind-blowing and accessible introduction to SDFs and procedural art from a master explainer.","q":{"x":"he's a master of this stuff and a great explainer.","i":34075707,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35674544,"author":"dgellow","month":"2023-04","story":"Simple Pathtracing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing, packed with educational content on creating art with maths","quote":"is mind-blowing and packed with SO MUCH educative content","text":"I see a Inigo Quilez link, I upvote! He's one of those incredible artist-engineer out there always pushing boundaries further and sharing his ideas and techniques publicly. If you have some time today, watch a few of his videos on his youtube channel, it's incredible to see his creation process in action: [LINK] For example, \"Painting a character with maths\" is mind-blowing and packed with SO MUCH educative content: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34075707,"author":"nighthawk454","month":"2022-12","story":"Signed distance functions in 46 lines of Python","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Master explainer of SDFs painting a character with maths","quote":"he's a master of this stuff and a great explainer.","text":"Great write up. If anyone wants to explore SDFs more, check out Inigo Quilez - he's a master of this stuff and a great explainer. Painting a Character with Maths: [VIDEO] And plenty more on his website in article form: [LINK] Finally, Shadertoy, a graphics shader playground where you can see (and try!) a lot of this kind of stuff: [LINK]"},{"id":28169892,"author":"gregsadetsky","month":"2021-08","story":"Fishdraw: Procedurally Generated Fish Drawings","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing painting of a girl with maths","quote":"Check out his incredible videos! Such as this mind-blowing one...","text":"Agreed. Check out his incredible videos! Such as this mind-blowing one... [VIDEO] \"Painting a Selfie Girl, with Maths\""},{"id":25689173,"author":"kn8a","month":"2021-01","story":"2020 Mathematical Art Exhibition","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing math art with live coding","quote":"This was a mind blowing example of math and art from last year","text":"This was a mind blowing example of math and art from last year - perhaps the live coding of the method was even more impressive than the outcome: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25946777,"author":"sxp","month":"2021-01","story":"Neural Geometric Level of Detail: Real-Time Rendering with Implicit 3D Surfaces","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing demo of signed distance fields","quote":"is an amazing demo of their power.","text":"If you don't have an understanding of signed distance fields, [VIDEO] is an amazing demo of their power. There are more videos about the fundamentals of SDFs at [LINK] The interesting thing about this paper is that they replace the mathematical SDF with a neural net that computes SDF_NN(x,y,z) => -1/1 and use this as a compression system for mesh based inputs. This feels like a graphical version of the [LINK] for NN-based text compression."},{"id":46024129,"author":"Jare","month":"2025-11","story":"Shaders: How to draw high fidelity graphics with just x and y coordinates","stance":"rec","why":"A painting that is a mathematical formula","quote":"this painting is a mathematical formula, a big function on the x and y coordinates of each pixel","text":"Obligatory \"this painting is a mathematical formula, a big function on the x and y coordinates of each pixel\" video from Iñigo Quilez. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44059112,"author":"emigre","month":"2025-05","story":"Inigo Quilez: computer graphics, mathematics, shaders, fractals, demoscene","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting video by Quilez","quote":"it's a very interesting video.","text":"Check out 'Painting a Character with Maths' [1] (2020) by him, it's a very interesting video. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37530172,"author":"antegamisou","month":"2023-09","story":"Painting with Math: A Gentle Study of Raymarching","stance":"rec","why":"Video alternative to the verbose article","quote":"be sure to check out, as the author cites, Inigo Quilez's videos","text":"Good to see high quality articles gaining traction. If the article looks too verbose, be sure to check out, as the author cites, Inigo Quilez's videos Painting a Landscape with Maths - [LINK] Painting a Character with Maths - [VIDEO] Bonus: Painting with Maths in Google Sheets - [LINK]"},{"id":35338774,"author":"pdpi","month":"2023-03","story":"Procedural 3D mesh generation in a 64kB intro","stance":"rec","why":"Video by a Shadertoy creator","quote":"this video by one of its creators is probably worth watching","text":"And, if we're suggesting Shadertoy, this video by one of its creators is probably worth watching: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31235637,"author":"lifthrasiir","month":"2022-05","story":"Making graphics in 4 kilobytes (2008) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth, accessible videos by Quilez","quote":"also has made in-depth videos [1] [2] which might be more accessible to who aren't familiar with shader languages","text":"Inigo Quilez, the author of this slide, also has made in-depth videos [1] [2] which might be more accessible to who aren't familiar with shader languages. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":31375095,"author":"brundolf","month":"2022-05","story":"Sliderland – Creative coding with 64 HTML sliders","stance":"rec","why":"Crazy things you can do with equations","quote":"Check out this video for some crazy stuff you can do with equations","text":"It's very similar to writing shaders. Check out this video for some crazy stuff you can do with equations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26710736,"author":"dominicjj","month":"2021-04","story":"Condition: A 64KB WebGL Intro","stance":"rec","why":"Creation of a selfie from scratch using formulas","quote":"create a selfie of a girl from scratch using formulas here","text":"Demos are almost always generated and displayed at runtime. The music is composed of samples generated at runtime by additive synthesis which is just adding waveforms of differing frequencies together when you get down to it. It doesn't take many lines of code to produce CD quality instruments that can be arranged as a soundtrack. Today's 64k intros offload their drawing onto the GPU using shaders and a technique called raymarching which is a form of fixed-step raytracing that can be done in real time. Essentially the objects you see are painted with math: instead of thousands of vertices for a 3d fractal or a complex object, a formula is fed in to the ray marcher and it returns a shape with colors and lighting. Combining shapes using unions and intersections is then done and often the shape formula will take time as a parameter as well. To play with this concept yourself go to [LINK] and see how shader programs are rendered into incredible scenes with astonishingly few lines of code. For a wonderful look at the creation process, see the grandfather of raymarching Inigo Quilez create a selfie of a girl from scratch using formulas here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25627023,"author":"lligallo","month":"2021-01","story":"Graphtoy","stance":"rec","why":"Builds a 3D scene from a formula step by step","quote":"It makes a full 3d complex scene out of a math formula, with step by step explanation","text":"Checkout this video by the author. It makes a full 3d complex scene out of a math formula, with step by step explanation [VIDEO]"},{"id":44932954,"author":"rozab","month":"2025-08","story":"LL3M: Large Language 3D Modelers","stance":"reference","why":"Interesting but unscalable SDF renderings","quote":"It's certainly not scalable, but it sure is interesting","text":"This is of course true, but have you ever seen Inigo Quilez's SDF renderings? It's certainly not scalable, but it sure is interesting [VIDEO]"},{"id":36378981,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2023-06","story":"Infinite Photorealistic Worlds Using Procedural Generation","stance":"reference","why":"Example of art generated without traditional assets","quote":"","text":"Could you define \"assets\", please? What would you consider the assets in these videos by Inigo Quillez, for example? [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35197553,"author":"gcr","month":"2023-03","story":"The model for the coins in Super Mario Odyssey is 65% simpler than in SM Galaxy","stance":"reference","why":"Incredible SDF work without textures","quote":"Inigo Quilez does some incredible stuff with signed distance fields!","text":"Inigo Quilez does some incredible stuff with signed distance fields! Those don't use textures in the traditional sense, but see [VIDEO]"},{"id":33414364,"author":"CyborgCabbage","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: What piece of code/codebase blew your mind when you saw it?","stance":"reference","why":"Quilez's YouTube channel","quote":"He has a YouTube channel as well","text":"He has a YouTube channel as well: [VIDEO] Also you may like Marble Marcher made by Code Parade, it's a game that uses a similar approach to rendering: [LINK]"},{"id":32811745,"author":"silviot","month":"2022-09","story":"Bauble: A playground for making art with Lisp and math","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how far the technique can go","quote":"This video [1] linked there shows how far you can get with this technique.","text":"Previous discussion: [LINK] (36 comments - sep 6th 2022). This video [1] linked there shows how far you can get with this technique. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30284122,"author":"t8y","month":"2022-02","story":"The performative quality of computer-generated art","stance":"reference","why":"Making-of video for a shader artwork","quote":"people appreciate that it's a shader, even people that are not into programming","text":"I don't think that people only value the output. Take \"Selfie Girl\"[0] by Inigo Quilez people appreciate that it's a shader, even people that are not into programming (though they'd need to see the making of video[1]). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["BFld4EBO2RE",2],["lGEMwh32soc",1],["SSH5EY-W5oM",1],["Ilg3gGewQ5U",1],["z6yL0_sDnX0",1],["XetplHcM7aQ",1],["_3loq22TxSc",1],["C7JQ7Rpwn2k",1],["RzWB5jL5RX0",1],["MQufJBVvAtY",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["4KtotxNAwME",1],["8fi7uSYlOdc",1],["agZ0xISi40E",1]]},"d0nERTFo-Sk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a funny, accurate and easy primer on Keynesian versus Austrian views of the business cycle.","q":{"x":"the Keynes vs. Hayek rap is incredibly well informed about both positions, and it's pretty easy to follow and entertaining","i":2012785,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":25887144,"author":"wonder_er","month":"2021-01","story":"No one knows how much the government can borrow","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious, sharp rap-battle comparison of Keynes and Hayek","quote":"For a hilarious and sharp rap-battle comparison of Keynes vs. Hayek","text":"For a hilarious and sharp rap-battle comparison of Keynes vs. Hayek (which... is basically the framework for all competing conversations on this topic) check out: Just a reminder - this is a rap-battle music video about economic policy. This is why the internet is amazing: Fear the Boom and Bust: Keynes vs. Hayek - The Original Economics Rap Battle!(Youtube)[0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2852894,"author":"ramanujan","month":"2011-08","story":"United States loses AAA credit rating from S&P ","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the Hayekian view well","quote":"Well worth watching if you haven't seen them.","text":"You take the Keynesian view. It's difficult to discuss this in more than a soundbite, but the Hayekian view is well explained by these two videos: [VIDEO] [LINK] Well worth watching if you haven't seen them. In short, the opposite/Hayekian view contends that taxation is seizure of resources from profitable/efficient entities and redistribution towards unprofitable/inefficient entities. We saw this in extremis in the bank bailout, and more controversially in microcosm with individual redistribution. If you accept this -- that \"profitable\" correlates with \"efficient\", and conversely, though the correlation is not perfect -- then taxation is not a Robin Hood sort of thing that increases well being, but rather a move that ties the legs of the efficient sectors of the economy -- those that can post a profit even in tough times -- to those that are already underwater."},{"id":1419133,"author":"Artifex","month":"2010-06","story":"Hayek's Road to Serfdom #1 on Amazon Today","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Educational, hilarious, well-done Hayek vs Keynes rap","quote":"it's educational, hilarious and very well done","text":"At least Glenn Beck is attempting to educate himself/his audience. Although, he'd probably do better to show everyone this (it's educational, hilarious and very well done): [VIDEO]"},{"id":39675667,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2024-03","story":"Rethinking my economics","stance":"rec","why":"Easily consumed primer on Keynes versus Hayek","quote":"this throwback to the 2008 crisis is an easily consumed primer on Keynes vs Hayek","text":"If anyone wants a fun quick summary of Hayek this throwback to the 2008 crisis is an easily consumed primer on Keynes vs Hayek: Keynes vs Hayek rap battle 1: [VIDEO] Keynes vs Hayek rap battle 2: [LINK]"},{"id":23687304,"author":"miles","month":"2020-06","story":"Friedrich Hayek on Evolution and Spontaneous Order (1983) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining overview of Hayek for newcomers","quote":"For those new to Hayek, here is an entertaining overview","text":"There have been a number of Hayek posts, though sadly few of them gained much traction: [LINK] For those new to Hayek, here is an entertaining overview: Fear the Boom and Bust: Keynes vs. Hayek [VIDEO] Fight of the Century: Keynes vs. Hayek [LINK]"},{"id":12565777,"author":"marmot777","month":"2016-09","story":"Ask HN: What are the must-read books about economics/finance?","stance":"rec","why":"Decent starting point before reading Keynes and Hayek","quote":"This hip hop battle is a decent start","text":"These are important books but obviously not a comprehensive list. * John Locke's Two Treatises of Government - It's political philosophy but it's hard to understand Classical Liberalism without having read some Locke. * Adam Smith's Wealth of Nations - He and Locke are the two main guys to read for a solid start on Classical Liberalism, which is completely different than modern political liberalism. It's like having two features in an app with nearly the same name. Confusing as fuck. * E. F. Schumacher's Small Is Beautiful: Economics as if People Mattered - This book will shift your perspective, useful to avoid becoming an a mindless advocate for one school of thought or another. * Marx is a tough one as Capital is massive and unreadable and The Communist Manifesto is a propaganda pamphlet but I think you need to at least find some articles that summarize the basics. * Keynes and Hayek - This hip hop battle is a decent start: [VIDEO] then read Keynes' The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money and Hayek's The Road to Serfdom. * Milton Friedman's - Yes, read Capitalism and Freedom. I hesitated to include it as the guy's so good at making the case that it can turn you into a market advocate bot. Please resist that. Can someone help me on this, is there a book balance Hayek and a book to balance Friedman? I'm sorry but Keynes doesn't do it for me. Look at the difference in titles between Hayek and Keynes. It's hard to motivate to read the Keynes book but nobody ever ha"},{"id":10437924,"author":"afsina","month":"2015-10","story":"Decline in the labor share of income in the U.S.","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite take on Keynes versus Austrian school","quote":"But i like the rap battle the most :)","text":"What you describe is part of the Keynesian economics. There is a great deal of resources about the criticism of Keynesian economics especially from Austrian School. But i like the rap battle the most :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":2012785,"author":"lionhearted","month":"2010-12","story":"Internet start-ups: Another bubble?","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous but well-informed, easy to follow comparison of both positions","quote":"the Keynes vs. Hayek rap is incredibly well informed about both positions, and it's pretty easy to follow and entertaining","text":"> The single most destructive force in a crash is deflationary pressure which can quickly feed on its self and spiral out of control. Well, that's part of the Keynesian view yes. I'm personally of the belief that currency-fuckery causes crashes in the first place, and that more currency-fuckery isn't the answer [1]. But let's just say Keynes is right for now. That still doesn't explain why the healthy Clinton/Bush economies had crazy-low interest rates, which was the main cause of this mess. [1] While it has a humorous spin, the Keynes vs. Hayek rap is incredibly well informed about both positions, and it's pretty easy to follow and entertaining - [VIDEO]"},{"id":1849935,"author":"jswinghammer","month":"2010-10","story":"Things Economists Agree On","stance":"rec","why":"Fun and accurate way to learn two views of the business cycle","quote":"this video is a fun and very accurate way to learn about two different views of the business cycle","text":"This is a Keynesian and Monetarist view of budgets. The idea is that IF you balance the budget (they often don't think this is ever a great idea anymore) then it should be balanced over the ups and downs of the business cycle. So let's say a business cycle starts up again and somehow the government doesn't spend all that extra money before it comes in and there is surplus. Now booms are followed by an inevitable bust no matter what anyone tells you when the next one starts up. Keynes argued that you should spend during busts to keep aggregate demand up and let the economy recover. So you run a deficit during this time. If you consider a balanced budget then they might argue that you should balance it with consideration to short term gains and losses. If you're interested this video is a fun and very accurate way to learn about two different views of the business cycle: [VIDEO] For the record I'm with Hayek in this video :)"},{"id":1090807,"author":"kiba","month":"2010-01","story":"Paul Volcker: How to Reform Our Financial System","stance":"rec","why":"Economics rap battle between Hayek and Keynes","quote":"I think an economic hip-hop video is relevant here","text":"I think an economic hip-hop video is relevant here: \"Fear the Boom and Bust\" - a Hayek vs. Keynes Rap Anthem [VIDEO]"},{"id":37513128,"author":"passion__desire","month":"2023-09","story":"Intermediate sci knowledge associated with overconfidence and negative attitudes","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as relevant to a Sokal-style hoax in economics","quote":"","text":"Well is it possible that an Alan Sokal kind of trick be pulled of in economics if it is a social science. Relevant : [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":37525538,"author":"Empact","month":"2023-09","story":"Repeat after me: building any new homes reduces housing costs for all","stance":"reference","why":"Lyrics illustrate Austrian view of credit-fueled booms","quote":"The boom plants the seeds for its future destruction.","text":"The boom gets started with an expansion of credit. The fed sets rates low, are you starting to get it? That new money is confused for real loanable funds, but it’s just inflation that’s driving the ones who invest in new projects, like housing construction. The boom plants the seeds for its future destruction. The savings aren’t real, consumption’s up too! And the grasping for resources reveals there’s too few. [VIDEO] The claim being that to back a loan with new “printed” money is necessarily inflationary. Loans backed with saved/invested funds are non-inflationary, because the saver gives up their ability to consume with those funds, in proportion with the consumption the borrower takes on."},{"id":33691070,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: What sort of systemic risk to crypto is Paul Graham referring to?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not if you're invested in it. Not if you want to see blockchain technology used to replace the DTCC. Not if you think there are potentially extreme differences between crime (laws enforced by power) and justice (what is ethically right or wrong). The people who invest in crypto are rent-seeking parasites hoping to extract value while incidentally providing liquidity to the Chinese miners blowing non trivial amounts of co2 in the air. The people who transact in crypto are actually building it and working towards the crypto dream of alternative currency. It's important to understand that when a company sells a stock, they get money that they can then use to grow themselves. So there is generally some of the principal \"retained\" in a unit of stock, particularly because the unit of stock also creates a legal relationship with the company. By definition all crypto starts from a miner, so when you buy a unit of crypto, you are transferring money directly to the miner. So the unit of crypto retains none of the principal investment. That is an absolute terrifying property. I am not quite sure what I think of ETH yet, since it's a little different, but BTC is straight bad for the environment. There are also arguments to be made that alternative currencies not controlled by a centralized government are an important endeavor. If governments have a monopoly on management of currency, then it can be expected that they abuse monopoly power. The distance between policy and reality can be me"},{"id":25563373,"author":"triangleman","month":"2020-12","story":"How Complex Systems Fail (1998)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I found a decent analogy for what I'm talking about: [LINK] With a bubble in Beanie Babies there was accompanying fraud and other criminal activity, with most consumers not wittingly participating, but they were affected by it. So with the asset bubble of the 2000's, this kind of behavior affected everyone, as everyone needs a house. In another thread I have said I don't blame the masses who may have made bad decisions in hindsight, as they did not have access to all the relevant information. So I do blame the fraudsters, but in the end I really only blame the Fed for keeping interest rates too low, and of course that can circle back around to society as well if you like. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22149170,"author":"miles","month":"2020-01","story":"Failure to recognize J. M. Keynes's work guarantees further crises","stance":"reference","why":"Used to argue Keynesianism perpetuates boom and bust","quote":"it is our slavish devotion to Keynesianism that promises a continued future of boom and bust cycles","text":"On the contrary; it is our slavish devotion to Keynesianism that promises a continued future of boom and bust cycles: Keynes vs. Hayek, Round 1 & 2: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14553168,"author":"EGreg","month":"2017-06","story":"The Hidden Cost of Privatization","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Look, I actually argue for social safety nets. I believe government's role can be defined simply as \"ensure that the minimum expectations of society are met\", with the full understanding that these expectations (running potable water, medical advances, etc.) increase over time and vary from place to place. I am totally fine with decentralizing government functions to the point of neighborhoods – many of you know that I'm in favor of more decentralization in general. However, even someone like me can say this article falls far short of supporting its audacious title. It's mostly a paean to Keynes and his concepts. In addition, it contains some fallacies: But, if the government covers the costs of the service, why not provide it itself? Why spend the time and money forming the partnership, and monitoring its outcomes, if it is going to pay for what is done anyway. Because when providers compete, quality goes up and costs go down. (On the other hand, when buyers compete, costs go up, which is why single payer systems lower costs.) The answers to these questions center on the greater efficiency of private enterprise and the consequent cost savings of public-private partnerships. Yet, studies of their use in infrastructure investment provide little support for this presumed cost saving Compare the VA to Medicare. Yet, many besides the users of a road benefit from it – the businesses on its route to name just a few. These would be hurt along with its users if its tolls reduced traf"},{"id":1616005,"author":"ramanujan","month":"2010-08","story":"Is Greece entering a Death Spiral?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Hayekian leaning of entrepreneurs","quote":"","text":"He's talking about the satellite photo story from a while back in which Greeks had many more (taxable) private pools than were reported to the government. [LINK] The question though is whether \"more efficient tax collection\" is really the cure for Greece's ailments. It really boils down to whether you think a centralized government can better allocate your dollars to your benefit, or whether you can. You could certainly make an argument that people would free ride and/or greatly underestimate the benefit that spending 10 cents of each dollar on (say) the military or roads would provide, and this furthermore justifies taking that 10 cents by force as part of the price of membership in a community. However, I personally find it less persuasive to argue that centralized allocation programs will produce greater wealth than distributed ones. A remote government simply doesn't have all the information and can't know your preferences. One thing I find interesting is that many people can come to fairly good agreement on what the problem is (bad economy, low unemployment, etc.) but disagree very dramatically on what the solution should be. frossie is likely a Keynesian and rick888 is probably a Hayekian; in my experience, Keynesianism is in the water supply and the \"default belief\" for most college graduates, but most entrepreneurs tend to end up Hayekian: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1646969,"author":"macromicro","month":"2010-08","story":"Spreading Hayek, Spurning Keynes","stance":"reference","why":"Speculates viral rap battle drove Road to Serfdom sales","quote":"Does anyone else think that the surge in sales of the Road to Serfdom stemmed from the viral youtube rap battle?","text":"Does anyone else think that the surge in sales of the Road to Serfdom stemmed from the viral youtube rap battle? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GTQnarzmTOc",3],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["9WcHsT97suU",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["9tocssf3w80",1],["Eghl19elKk8",1],["N1CBDTgGtOU",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1]]},"atvtBE6t48M":{"wy":"Repeatedly called the best beginner course on audio synthesis, since the concepts have not changed since it was made.","q":{"x":"It's the best educational video I've seen on YT","i":28507905,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28507905,"author":"aasasd","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: Have you found it difficult to get quality technical content on YouTube?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Three-hour in-depth explanation of each synth control.","quote":"It's the best educational video I've seen on YT","text":"My problem with informational videos on YouTube is that the majority of what I see in search results is ten minutes or less, often like two-three minutes. So if I want somewhat more information than ‘I wonder what is that thing anyway’, I pretty much can't get that on YouTube. Like, I'd expect thirty minutes to an hour to be a reasonable length for explaining a topic superficially but in more depth than a dictionary entry. Compare that to the three-part introduction to sound synthesis from the 80s where the dude twiddles knobs for three hours and explains what each of them is doing. Just dialing in one sound takes him ten minutes. It's the best educational video I've seen on YT: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25547651,"author":"aasasd","month":"2020-12","story":"1937 film explains how a car differential works","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Slowly twiddles knobs so you hear what each parameter does.","quote":"this three-hour ‘Intro to synthesis’ series is one of my favorite explanations on musicmaking","text":"That's exactly why this three-hour ‘Intro to synthesis’ series is one of my favorite explanations on musicmaking: [VIDEO] Instead of running through all the parameters in ten minutes and one second, the dude keeps twiddling the knobs and lets you hear what they do. He spends ten minutes on dialing just one sound. As a bonus, since the vid is from the early eighties, you can occupy yourself by guessing exactly how high the man is."},{"id":19770083,"author":"superzadeh","month":"2019-04","story":"Subtractive synths explained (2011)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Old-school but complete, digestible course on audio synthesis.","quote":"This has been my favourite course to learn about Audio Synthesis","text":"This has been my favourite course to learn about Audio Synthesis; it's old school but has all you need to know, in a digestible format: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16071247,"author":"kowdermeister","month":"2018-01","story":"Open source modular synth software lets you create 70s & 80s electronic music","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Timeless synthesis fundamentals shared by all synths.","quote":"I highly encourage you to watch this and P2 on synthesis. The concepts haven't changed since then.","text":"I highly encourage you to watch this and P2 on synthesis. The concepts haven't changed since then. You will find the same things on all synths. [VIDEO] Also, go ahead and try Propellerhead Reason (free to play with, you can save, but can't open saved files). It's the best one on the UX side. However I have to admit I'm using it for 10+ years now."},{"id":32016440,"author":"aasasd","month":"2022-07","story":"Ed is the standard text editor (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Unhurried early-80s tutorial showing what each knob does.","quote":"one of my favorite music-making tutorials is the three-part, three-hour series","text":"Yeah, I'm kinda getting into these vids from the seventies and eighties. “Introducing The Amazing Compact Disc, 1982” [LINK] — “You can pick it up and move around, even shake, and nothing happens”, yeah sure bud. “Introduction to Microsoft Excel, 1992” [LINK] “Susan Kare explains Macintosh UI ergonomics on the Computer Chronicles, 1984” [LINK] — the control panel is just astounding by modern standards, especially seeing as for me the control panel is a litmus test of an OS interface. Also one of my favorite music-making tutorials is the three-part, three-hour series ‘Intro to Synthesis’ from sometime in the early 80s: [VIDEO] — instead of going through all the terminology in ten minutes and one second, the guy takes his time twiddling knobs and showing what each one does."},{"id":20586506,"author":"aasasd","month":"2019-08","story":"Ask HN: Your favourite tutorial for total beginners?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the controls you constantly adjust on a synthesizer.","quote":"It explains the things that you'll constantly fiddle with on a synthesizer","text":"More of an explanation than a tutorial, and dunno how it is for total beginners, but — Intro to Synthesis: The Building Blocks of Sound & Synthesis, by Dean Friedman: [VIDEO] It explains the things that you'll constantly fiddle with on a synthesizer (and sometimes even in a DAW with samples: envelopes are likely to appear there). The series benefit greatly from being three hours long in total instead of ten or even thirty minutes. The first vid is followed by: - Intro to Synthesis Part 2 - Types of Synthesis & Programming Examples [LINK] - Intro to Synthesis Part 3 - Additional Synth Features, Performance Controls & Wrap Up [LINK] Also, since the vids are from sometime in early 80s, you'll have fun trying to figure out exactly how high the guy is."},{"id":20794658,"author":"ydnaclementine","month":"2019-08","story":"Ask HN: What are you learning in 2019?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a resource for understanding synths.","quote":"","text":"Music producing with LMMS, mostly wanting to make jungle dnb sounding stuff I've installed FL studio a few times in the past but didn't get really far. This time I've decided, let's actually gain some understanding of theory and tools (like synths: [VIDEO] ) and make a little something just for fun. I'm using LMMS because now I'm on linux and don't want to deal with wine and FL studio. LMMS is actually pretty good, and has a good selection of tutorials on youtube"}],"r":[["x_q50tvbQm4",1],["8CAafjodkyE",1],["i9MXYZh1jcs",1],["b6URa-PTqfA",1],["8PwwRR8deHk",1],["TQMbvJNRpLE",1]]},"zwAD6dRSVyI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the go-to intuitive visualization of high-dimensional geometry, relevant to machine learning, optimization and sphere packing.","q":{"x":"There's a great 3blue1brown video that demonstrates this unintuitive result in a relatively intuitive way","i":44502339,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44502339,"author":"kibwen","month":"2025-07","story":"New sphere-packing record stems from an unexpected source","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intuitive demonstration of unintuitive high-dimensional result","quote":"There's a great 3blue1brown video that demonstrates this unintuitive result in a relatively intuitive way","text":"There's a great 3blue1brown video that demonstrates this unintuitive result in a relatively intuitive way: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17507128,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2018-07","story":"Random Points on a Sphere","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizing higher dimensions","quote":"incredible video about visualizing higher dimensions[1]","text":"Don't forget 3Blue1Brown's incredible video about visualizing higher dimensions[1]. He's getting to xkcd levels of \"Of course there's a relevant video\" [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15410663,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2017-10","story":"What Is a Neural Network?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Trick for visualizing higher dimensions","quote":"A trick for visualizing higher dimensions","text":"A trick for visualizing higher dimensions: [VIDEO] Only 383k subscribers. Hmm. Remember to subscribe, comment, and smash that like button. Real Engineering is another great channel. And of course Veritasium and Numberphile."},{"id":40367217,"author":"NeoTar","month":"2024-05","story":"Strangely Curved Shapes Break 50-Year-Old Geometry Conjecture","stance":"rec","why":"Approach to using higher-dimensional spaces without visualizing them","quote":"has a decent video which perhaps helps get a handle on how one can use higher-dimensional spaces","text":"Three Blue, One Brown has a decent video which perhaps helps get a handle on how one can use higher-dimensional spaces, without needing to wrap you head around trying to envision a four-dimensional cube, or similar. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30013004,"author":"jerf","month":"2022-01","story":"Geometry from Another Universe","stance":"rec","why":"Explains high-dimensional sphere geometry and surface concentration","quote":"This 3Blue1Brown video addresses enough related stuff that the rest should become reasonably comprehensible","text":"As you increase the dimensionality of a sphere, an ever-increasing proportion of the sphere is within epsilon of the surface. This 3Blue1Brown video addresses enough related stuff that the rest should become reasonably comprehensible: [VIDEO] This particular result is tossed in as a side note around 23:25, so it's not addressed directly, but it'll help."},{"id":16524119,"author":"gilbetron","month":"2018-03","story":"Counterintuitive Properties of High Dimensional Space","stance":"rec","why":"Complements intuition about high-dimensional geometry for optimization","quote":"is a great complement to your article","text":"Great article! I've been thinking about this on and off recently, as I wonder if we might be having intuitive issues when it comes to gradient descent optimization. 3 Blue,1 Brown had a video recently that kicked off my head scratching and is a great complement to your article: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40291311,"author":"euW3EeBe","month":"2024-05","story":"Gradient descent visualization","stance":"reference","why":"Background on unintuitive high-dimensional geometry","quote":"Thinking outside the 10-dimensional box - 3blue1brown","text":"Not an expert in this field, but I'm guessing this is related to unintuitive nature of geometry in high-dimensional spaces. One rough example I can think of is the fact that the number of ways to move away from the origin increases exponentially (or even faster?) as the dimensionality goes up. There is _way_ more volume away from the origin than near it, I've seen this explained as something like \"most of the volume of a high-dimensional orange is in the peel\". One result of this is the fact that samples of a standard gaussian end up forming a \"shell\" as opposed to a \"ball\" that you would expect (this phenomenon is called the concentration of measure in general). Also, very roughly, high-dimensional objects have lots of corners, and these corners are also very sharp. I would guess that gradient descent would get stuck in these corners and have a hard time getting out. Some links related to this: - Spikey Spheres: [LINK] - Thinking outside the 10-dimensional box - 3blue1brown: [VIDEO] - This is a fairly long talk about HMC, but it does talk about some problems that come up when sampling high-dimensional distributions: [LINK]"},{"id":17736779,"author":"wruza","month":"2018-08","story":"A Visual, Intuitive Guide to Imaginary Numbers (2007)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah, I see now. “times i” is discrete 90 degree rotation itself, not just ‘i’, nor ‘b’. Thanks everyone for making that clear. This though shows that explanations via analogies or non-strict wording may confuse one rather than enlighten. I’m not good at math, but once understood to not search analogies or geometry in things. Instead it is better to “shut up and calculate”. Not sure if imagining something is required to manage it. It’s only our brain’s faulty quirk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15627681,"author":"darkmighty","month":"2017-11","story":"What is backpropagation and what is it doing? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I would reiterate that simply because there are problems with naive visualization, we shouldn't discredit visual thinking. There are several key elements to effective visual thinking. The primary importance is to keep it grounded in proofs and theorems, so you know exactly what are your limitations. Often you can use a geometric argument on top of a few theorems and you get a very strong result intuitively, and then use this intuition with a tiny amount of algebra to prove it (which might take you forever to arrive from a purely algebraic perspective). Another key is that there are several ways of visualizing things. You can almost always transform a problem into an equivalent one that is easy to visualize (just need a little bit of care with the transformation, etc). --- For example, you can show functions form a vector space, visualizing some interesting algebraic properties about them, even if it constitutes an infinite-dimensional space. You can show several operators (such as d/dx) are linear, you can give it a norm, internal product, etc. This trick lets you use visual tools (and linear algebra tools) with arbitrary functions. You can visualize projection of a function into a subspace, or into some non-canonical basis -- yielding useful applications -- such as Fourier analysis. Fourier analysis itself is a fertile ground for visual thinking. You'll be finding trivial arguments for seemingly difficult decisions such as \"Does this linear system have a bounded output for a"}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"ZWQ0591PAxM":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever discussion turns to how old games were so small, as a clear tour of 8-bit optimization and creative work under hardware limits.","q":{"x":"Here is my favorite video about 8-bit era optimisations. Makes you appreciate games from this era even more.","i":38433792,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38433792,"author":"arnaudsm","month":"2023-11","story":"Street Fighter II, subtile accurate animation (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"8-bit era optimisation tricks that deepen appreciation of old games","quote":"Here is my favorite video about 8-bit era optimisations. Makes you appreciate games from this era even more.","text":"Here is my favorite video about 8-bit era optimisations. Makes you appreciate games from this era even more. This arcane magic survived in the demoscene to this day. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29127298,"author":"splch","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How were video games from the 90s so efficient?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Morphcat explains compression methods and thought process behind a 40KB NES game","quote":"I definitely recommend checking this out as they go over interesting compression methods and more general thought processes.","text":"I love this video: [VIDEO] As part of a Kickerstarter campaign, Morphcat Games made this video explaining how they eked out a really incredible game with only 40 Kb (a lot like Super Mario Bros 2). I definitely recommend checking this out as they go over interesting compression methods and more general thought processes."},{"id":41268241,"author":"throwawayk7h","month":"2024-08","story":"Crafting a 13KB Game: The Story of Space Huggers","stance":"rec","why":"Video about the 40KB NES game Micro Mages","quote":"there's a great video for this 40 kb nes game, micro mages","text":"Incredible. I love these stories of kb games. For those interested, there's a great video for this 40 kb nes game, micro mages: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38388236,"author":"MrFoof","month":"2023-11","story":"Yoyozo (or, how I made a Playdate game in 39KB)","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of fitting Micro Mages into a 40KB ROM","quote":"The goal with Micro Mages was to get a full-featured game into the same footprint of the original","text":"A similarly interesting story is regarding the NES homebrew game, Micro Mages. The goal with Micro Mages was to get a full-featured game into the same footprint of the original, \"Black Box\" NES releases for the North American launch, where a fair number of games fit in 40KB of ROM or less. Video, but it's a good summary: [VIDEO] Morphcat has done other great NES games as well, such as Space Gulls and Bobl."},{"id":24765148,"author":"Timpy","month":"2020-10","story":"Top games from the JS13K 2020 competition","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates how small old games really are","quote":"is an interesting illustration of just how small these games are.","text":"I'm absolutely blown away by some of the games they managed to make in under 13kb. This video \"How we fit an NES game into 40 Kilobytes\" is an interesting illustration of just how small these games are. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21862490,"author":"alecco","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among best talks of 2019","quote":"How we fit a NES game in 40KiB","text":"ZFS for newbies [LINK] main ideas behind ZFS and why it blows out the competition in many scenarios Making C Less Dangerous in the Linux Kernel [LINK] NUMA optimizations in the FreeBSD stack (Netflix) [LINK] How we fit a NES game in 40KiB [VIDEO]"},{"id":21214339,"author":"MrFoof","month":"2019-10","story":"Super Mario Bros. game was just 31 Kilobytes. How's that possible?","stance":"rec","why":"How Micro Mages crammed a full NES game into 40KB","quote":"take a look at a how a recently released NES game (Micro Mages) managed it.","text":"Very much agree with the parent. If you want a better idea on how you can cram everything into 40KB — using every trick in the book, including ones not considered 30+ years ago — take a look at a how a recently released NES game (Micro Mages) managed it. Their primary design constrain was that they restricted themselves to 40KB, despite wanting to do far more than the games that were done in 40KB back in the 80s. [VIDEO]"},{"id":49062443,"author":"hollow-moe","month":"2026-07","story":"Htmx 4.0, the first JavaScript library to release exclusively on the Game Boy","stance":"reference","why":"Micro Mages as new software on ancient hardware","quote":"Love to see new stuff on ancient platforms.","text":"Love to see new stuff on ancient platforms. Surprised I couldn't find a post about Micro Mages on HN, only comments. [VIDEO] Spoiler, when the time will come you'll never see something like this on the play station 4."},{"id":36187730,"author":"latexr","month":"2023-06","story":"A new line drawing method for the cycle savvy","stance":"reference","why":"Example of creativity under hardware limits","quote":"there are games created in the 2000s which work on the NES.","text":"Let’s take pixel art on console gaming as an example. In the early days, games used pixel graphics due to technical constraints. Artists were limited in what they could achieve and had to get creative within the limitations to push the software and hardware to its limits in pursuit of a specific vision. Everyone had the same set of restrictions but thanks to creative technical decisions some rose above as exceptional. There is a particular kind of enjoyment in creating a lot with very little. If your digital art can be hundreds of GBs in size, your 3D rendition of the Sistine Chapel will be judged by its faithfulness; but if you’re only allowed a handful of KBs and everyone believes any kind of 3D graphics are impossible at that size, your dinky little rotating cube becomes an impressive technological feat. Context matters. A tractor pulling a plane is not impressive; a man pulling a plane is. Most people could do the former but not the latter. Furthermore, not all limitations are equally interesting. Nostalgia and popularity also play a role. Today, pixel graphics are an aesthetic choice. Developers and artist can go beyond what was possible when they were a necessity and create something entirely new. Yet some still derive enjoyment and pride from following the limitations of yore to the letter: there are games created in the 2000s which work on the NES. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24207163,"author":"nineteen999","month":"2020-08","story":"Architecture of the Nintendo DS","stance":"reference","why":"Compression tricks for sprites and map layouts in 40K","quote":"I remember seeing this video on that about the clever compression tricks used to cram the sprites and map layouts into 40K.","text":"I remember seeing this video on that about the clever compression tricks used to cram the sprites and map layouts into 40K. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["hpgK51w6uhk",1],["Z7Wl2FW2TcA",1],["u5um8QWWRvo",1],["kLiwvnr4L80",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1]]},"z6yL0_sDnX0":{"wy":"HN commenters strongly recommend it for its account of how the Jacquard loom and punch cards led to computers.","q":{"x":"Every computer engineer, computer scientist and software developer should learn about the Jacquard loom","i":33269771,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45807748,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2025-11","story":"Robert Hooke's \"Cyberpunk” Letter to Gottfried Leibniz","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Connections episode featuring Robert Hooke.","quote":"though the entire series is worth watching all the same","text":"I believe Robert Hooke is mentioned in James Burke’s Connections , a fantastic program that just got a new season on Curiosity Stream; I haven’t seen the new episodes yet but I have thoroughly enjoyed everything else Burke has ever done, so I expect the new series to be great also. [LINK] The episodes Faith in Numbers (1978 original series, S01E04[0]) and Thunder in the Skies (S01E06)[1] feature Robert Hooke from what I can tell from a little searching, though it’s been a while since I saw the series, so I apologize if I’m wrong on that point, though the entire series is worth watching all the same. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":41053033,"author":"sxp","month":"2024-07","story":"How an 1803 Jacquard Loom Led to Computer Technology [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth Connections episode on the loom-to-computer chain of events.","quote":"the amazing documentary, Connections.","text":"If you want a more in depth view about this series of events, I suggest [VIDEO] which is an episode of the amazing documentary, Connections. (The show also has one of the best timed shots in history: [LINK] )"},{"id":33269771,"author":"Maursault","month":"2022-10","story":"Part of the long-lost star catalogue of Hipparchus found in medieval parchment","stance":"strong_rec","why":"James Burke's elegant account of the Jacquard loom's significance.","quote":"Every computer engineer, computer scientist and software developer should learn about the Jacquard loom","text":"Every computer engineer, computer scientist and software developer should learn about the Jacquard loom and its predecessors and its significance as elegantly described by James Burke.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25180400,"author":"sxp","month":"2020-11","story":"We have no idea what happens next","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Connections episode on proto-computers arising from the Black Death.","quote":"My favorite is this one about proto-computers that came about due to the Black Death","text":"I completely agree. For those who haven't experienced Connections-style history, four episodes are on YouTube: [LINK] My favorite is this one about proto-computers that came about due to the Black Death: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44514246,"author":"ssalazar","month":"2025-07","story":"Phrase origin: Why do we \"call\" functions?","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of progression from Jacquard loom to census tabulator to computer.","quote":"the whole video is worth watching","text":"For those who aren't already familiar, James Burke in Connections has a great summary/rundown of this technological progression from Jacquard loom to census tabulator to computer punchcard, starting around the 36 minute mark here (though the whole video is worth watching). [VIDEO]"},{"id":47390833,"author":"staplung","month":"2026-03","story":"IBM, sonic delay lines, and the history of the 80×24 display","stance":"reference","why":"Burke demonstrates old census tabulators.","quote":"The old BBC Connections series has a segment with James Burke using the old census tabulators.","text":"And the reason they were modeled after the dollar bill size is because there were already many types of systems for storing and organizing them. That came in handy for the census. The old BBC Connections series has a segment with James Burke using the old census tabulators. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XetplHcM7aQ",2],["8--5LwHRhjk",1],["Ilg3gGewQ5U",1],["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["V-vFtiDYiIw",1],["gdK4y6O-llE",1],["nO5vjmDFZaI",1]]},"9Q6sLbz37gk":{"wy":"Whenever notebooks get criticized, HN commenters point to it as the standard rebuttal from a prominent practitioner.","q":{"x":"I strongly recommend Jeremy Howard's (founder of fast.ai) recent talk","i":25524388,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":41587074,"author":"3eb7988a1663","month":"2024-09","story":"Bento: Jupyter Notebooks at Meta","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Howard's rebuttal to criticism of notebooks","quote":"That video cannot be seen without watching Jeremy Howard's rebuttal","text":"That video cannot be seen without watching Jeremy Howard's rebuttal: I Like Notebooks. I also believe this was the video that got him kicked out of a conference(?) because it was too confrontational? Which was just ugly for a guy who clearly loves being an educator. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25524388,"author":"armanboyaci","month":"2020-12","story":"Interactive C++ for Data Science","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Addresses common critiques of Jupyter notebooks","quote":"I strongly recommend Jeremy Howard's (founder of fast.ai) recent talk","text":"I strongly recommend Jeremy Howard's (founder of fast.ai) recent talk - \"I Like Notebooks\" ( [VIDEO] ). He addresses some of the concerns/critiques about jupyter notebooks."},{"id":24926400,"author":"tosh","month":"2020-10","story":"I Violated a Code of Conduct","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jeremy Howard's defense of notebooks as a development medium","quote":"I think both talks are very worth watching","text":"If you haven't yet: I think both talks are very worth watching: I don't like notebooks ( [LINK] ) by Joel Grus I like notebooks ( [VIDEO] ) by Jeremy Howard notebooks are a very interesting medium and they have both shortcomings as well as things they are great at and neither were totally obvious to me."},{"id":32271871,"author":"jph00","month":"2022-07","story":"Nbdev: Create delightful software with Jupyter Notebooks","stance":"rec","why":"Persuades notebook skeptics before they judge nbdev","quote":"please watch this first","text":"A from-scratch rewrite of nbdev was released today -- details on what's new are in the launch post: [LINK] Let me know if you have any questions or comments -- I'm the lead dev on the project. (If you're in the \"I don't like notebooks\" camp, please watch this first: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":25164285,"author":"jph00","month":"2020-11","story":"Nbdev: A literate programming environment that democratizes best practices","stance":"rec","why":"Shows differences between line-oriented REPL and notebook working styles","quote":"I have a video where I show the difference between these styles of working in some detail","text":"No that's not the reason - or at least, not for everyone. I created nbdev. I've been coding for over 30 years, and spent over 10 years using R and S-PLUS. I've used Delphi, Visual Studio, Emacs, vim, vscode, and many other editors and IDEs, including many with integrated line-oriented REPLs. There's a big difference between a line-oriented plain text REPL, and a Mathematica/Jupyter-style notebook REPL, especially when you want to mix and match your charts, image outputs, rich table outputs, interactive JS outputs, and so forth. Also, for experimentation, where you want to go back and change things to see what happens (e.g. very common in data science) I find it much easier and more understandable in a notebook. I have a video where I show the difference between these styles of working in some detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46183503,"author":"zupatol","month":"2025-12","story":"What is “literate programming”? (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Howard explains why he does everything in notebooks","quote":"Here's Jeremy Howard explaining why he loves doing everything in notebooks","text":"Another successful example of literate programming is fastHTML, and probably most of the code written at fast.ai and answer.ai. [LINK] Here's Jeremy Howard explaining why he loves doing everything in notebooks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24926896,"author":"tosh","month":"2020-10","story":"I Violated a Code of Conduct","stance":"reference","why":"Re-recorded version of the talk after removal from the conference","quote":"Jeremy recorded a new version","text":"afaiu the talk was removed from or not put on the website of the conference, Jeremy recorded a new version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24932573,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2020-10","story":"I Violated a Code of Conduct","stance":"reference","why":"Contains the section judged unkind, which the commenter found respectful","quote":"which was done quite respectfully from what I watched","text":"[VIDEO] is the specific section that seems to have been judged as \"unkind\". To me, that seems like a massive over-reaction or massively uncharitable interpretation of the talk focused on a single point of disagreement, which was done quite respectfully from what I watched."}],"r":[["Iq9DzN6mvYA",1],["5Dnw46eC-0o",1],["rI8tNMsozo0",1],["udlMSe5-zP8",1],["SrKj4hYic5A",1],["cPNkH-7PRTk",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["7jiPeIFXb6U",1]]},"JxBg4sMusIg":{"wy":"HN commenters call it an internet classic that makes you a more proficient Excel user, and note it is still relevant despite predating newer functions like XLOOKUP.","q":{"x":"will turn you into a more proficient user of Excel.","i":40685446,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":49287856,"author":"Arainach","month":"2026-08","story":"My Rules for Using Spreadsheets","stance":"rec","why":"Still-relevant Excel talk","quote":"is more than a decade old but still relevant","text":"> They force name and number everything which is exactly like using single letter variables, they are only allowed for simple things. This isn't true, named ranges have existed for longer than many (most?) HN readers have been alive. As far as I'm aware they were in the first version in 1985. Joel Spolsky's \"you suck at Excel\" is more than a decade old but still relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48724025,"author":"imurray","month":"2026-06","story":"Alternatives to Nested If Function","stance":"rec","why":"Classic Excel video, though predating xlookup","quote":"That video was before the introduction of xlookup","text":"I think you're thinking of \"You Suck at Excel\" by Joel Spolsky. [VIDEO] Lots of past HN discussion... [LINK] That video was before the introduction of xlookup, which I believe is now worth considering over index and match."},{"id":43605657,"author":"aleph_minus_one","month":"2025-04","story":"Lessons from open source in the Mexican government","stance":"rec","why":"Internet classic on how to really use Excel","quote":"here some internet classic on this topic: You Suck at Excel - Joel Spolsky","text":"> Big tools like MSOffice have the ability to be put in hiring contracts: Everyone is assumed to know how to use it, so the company is allowed to deny the no training excuse without spending money. I would claim that if you haven't worked in the finance or insurance industry (or some related industry) for quite some time, you very likely don't know how to use Excel (I have a feeling that a similar points holds for Word and Powerpoint with respect to some industries, but I think for these applications this phenomenon is a little bit less pronounced). Indeed, I'd claim that most books about how to use Excel are simply crap. To just give you a glimpse how to use Excel, here some internet classic on this topic: You Suck at Excel - Joel Spolsky [VIDEO] Really understanding Excel is life task, similar to really understanding modern C++."},{"id":41806911,"author":"egeozcan","month":"2024-10","story":"Show HN: Tenno – Markdown and JavaScript = a hybrid of Word and Excel","stance":"rec","why":"Light introduction to Excel's data table power","quote":"I really like the super light introduction here from Joel Spolsky","text":"As a well seasoned web developer, when I'm doing front-end, there are always 2 big problems: Data tables and loading indicators (fun way of saying it's hard to tackle lazy loading without a request waterfall). The second problem has had good attempts in the recent years, but no single web application can beat excel in terms of data tables (while not 100% relevant anymore, I really like the super light introduction here from Joel Spolsky: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":40685446,"author":"surfingdino","month":"2024-06","story":"The Microsoft Excel superstars throw down in Vegas","stance":"rec","why":"Makes you a more proficient Excel user","quote":"will turn you into a more proficient user of Excel.","text":"[VIDEO] will turn you into a more proficient user of Excel. However, make sure you know the limits of Excel... [LINK]"},{"id":40284877,"author":"tichiian","month":"2024-05","story":"Pyspread – Pythonic Spreadsheet","stance":"rec","why":"Excel tips video recommended for better usage","quote":"His Excel-lence also recommends this","text":"That A1 mode is Excel \"Baby-Mode\", you can switch to R1C1 mode in settings. Things are far easier then, and more sane. His Excel-lence also recommends this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40385201,"author":"mbforbes","month":"2024-05","story":"Improvements to data analysis in ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Fun video to improve spreadsheet skills","quote":"There's a fun video called You Suck at Excel by Joel Spolsky","text":"Nothing capability-wise! Just speed and ease of manipulation. Maybe an analogy is doing image processing. One could write filters and other manipulations in code, or use something like Photoshop. It turns out Photoshop is a great interface for many of those tasks. I think the same thing is true for more data analysis than I'd realized because of my poor Excel skills. Still want to improve my spreadsheet abilities though. There's a fun video called You Suck at Excel by Joel Spolsky I'm halfway through [1]. Would love to know of more like this. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47072703,"author":"tpoacher","month":"2026-02","story":"Use Microsoft Office Shortcuts in Libre Office","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fair enough. My personal anecdote (and obviously your mileage may vary) is that, I've switched to Libreoffice a long time ago, and now my frustrations mirror yours except in reverse; i.e., I'm very happy and comfortable with the Libreoffice suite, and whenever I am 'forced' to work on MS Office, I find it super-unintuitive, buggy, and frustrating. So perhaps some of this is 'habit / comfort-zone' disguised as preference of functionality. Sometimes it's just simple stuff, like, being unable to resize the comment column, but even small stuff like that adds up in the end. Having said that, I'm not a heavy Excel user, yet I'm already aware of some differences between it and Calc which are probably not a simple matter of reassigning a keyboard shortcut. Switching from AB mode to RC mode is one example; in Calc you need to go to Tools::Options::Calc::Formula::Syntax to switch. But so far I've had no problems working in Calc in general. I was able to follow Joel Spolsky's infamous \"You suck at Excel\" video [0] with no problems, for example. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["T0CpOYZZZW4",1],["quLa6kzzra0",1],["QAr-uHd8h8o",1],["-gGLvg0n-uY",1]]},"jh8supIUj6c":{"wy":"Recommended as a forceful statement of the privacy case, though some commenters found its tone elitist and politically charged.","q":{"x":"All in all, this is a great talk, I highly recommend you watch from start.","i":16699012,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16699012,"author":"mashedvikings","month":"2018-03","story":"It’s Time to Make Our Privacy Tools Easier to Find","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Critique of privacy on Facebook as charade.","quote":"All in all, this is a great talk, I highly recommend you watch from start.","text":"Aral Balkan has excellent things to say about charades such as \"privacy on facebook\": [VIDEO] All in all, this is a great talk, I highly recommend you watch from start."},{"id":9528692,"author":"rashkov","month":"2015-05","story":"So Long, and Thanks for All the Fish","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Overview of internet state from a pro-privacy, human rights perspective.","quote":"Really one of the best talks I've seen on the topic.","text":"Aral Balkan gave a superlative talk a few days ago at re:publica 2015, called Beyond The Camera Panopticon: [VIDEO] It's a broad overview the current state of the internet as seen through a cyberpunk, pro- freedom, human rights, and privacy perspective. Aral is a dynamic speaker and delivers it with a delightfully dark and sardonic voice. Really one of the best talks I've seen on the topic."},{"id":22358024,"author":"maqp","month":"2020-02","story":"Privacy-focused messenger Signal is ready to go mainstream and take on WhatsApp","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why data-collecting companies are not trustworthy.","quote":"Aral Balkan explains this better than I ever could","text":"\"Facebook doesn't control it.\" Unfortunately it's controlled by the Mark Zuckerberg of Russia who still has access to his data brokers. None of the companies with access to our private data seem problematic at first. Google was a gift from the heavens with it's insane 1000MB gmail accounts back in 2003, now it's a private intelligence agency. Facebook was about free fun and connectivity, now it's a private intelligence agency. But Telegram's going to be different and money comes from magic and ICOs even after it reaches critical mass. Aral Balkan explains this better than I ever could [VIDEO] Also, the security model of Telegram is exactly the same as Facebook Messenger: A bunch of promises about respecting your privacy, coupled with opt-in end-to-end-encryption that doesn't work cross-device, and the use of which reveals the intention to hide messages from server (which is extremely valuable metadata: these messages I want to hide from service provider and governments!)"},{"id":10208351,"author":"yuvadam","month":"2015-09","story":"Is it futile to un-Google?","stance":"rec","why":"Ethical case for avoiding data monopolies.","quote":"","text":"Approaching the \"to google or not to google\" question is impossible without stating your casus belli . If you care about opsec, there are many reasons why you would want to use google services: e.g. a hostile actor will have an extremely hard time stealing your emails from Gmail while there's a might higher chance you fucked up a config on your self-hosted setup. Usually, as always, it comes down to smart compartmentalization and using the right tool for the job. Personally, the ethical perspective is a much stronger reason why to stray away from data behemoths like Google and Facebook. First of all I believe the \"usefullness\" of many of these services is over-exaggerated: Facebook noawadays is more of a brainwashing service to expose you to \"content\" that does nothing more than dull your mind and keep you mindlessly scrolling and endless stream of irrelevant advertisements. But even if this isn't the case for all online services, some come at a price I am personally not willing to pay. We are entering an era of digital slavery where our entire lives are managed by data monopolies that are bound by no rules other than those they create for themselves. I don't care how convenient these commercial services are, if the price is giving up the sovereignty of my data, and the core liberties of my human existence. I make a conscious choice to not be part of this system, and we will all have to face this choice sooner or later. For more on this topic, I highly suggest Aral Balkan's t"},{"id":10230678,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2015-09","story":"Google has most of my email because it has all of yours (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Further discussion of the problem beyond Gmail.","quote":"has an excellent further discussion of this problem beyond just Gmail","text":"re:publica 2015 - Aral Balkan: Beyond The Camera Panopticon has an excellent further discussion of this problem beyond just Gmail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9529516,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2015-05","story":"So Long, and Thanks for All the Fish","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The talk is terrible. It is politically charged when it really shouldn't be.","text":"[VIDEO] No one but the White man can bring the Internet to you. Dude, I'm not even white and I find it disparaging. The talk is terrible. It is politically charged when it really shouldn't be. The issue is simple: Privacy concerns are not being respected by Facebook or Google. I don't know why he keeps bringing up \"White People\" conquering nations, or \"The White Dude who lives in a Cloud and watches over Us\" , or any of the other disparaging remarks. What the hell? How many times does he call Mark Zuckerberg white ? Why have any emphasis there at all? If it were a black man in charge of Facebook violating our privacy, it'd be just as bad. Why did he have to bring Mark Zuckerberg's race into this? Its one thing to be against the system, or to expose the system's flaws to the world. But disparaging the audience is not the answer. His harmful speech is going to hurt the cause in the long term."},{"id":9532013,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2015-05","story":"So Long, and Thanks for All the Fish","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he's so disparaging and elitist it is very hard to actually recommend this video to anyone I know","text":"[VIDEO] > A lot of us already subscribe to the mass psychosis of a all seeing White Man in the cloud watching over us. Erm... really? He wants to bring Religion into this? This isn't the only time he references Religion either, but its the most noticeable. [VIDEO] > Like these _beautiful_ Nexus phones Rather disparaging to owners of Nexus phones. He's pretty much calling you a dumb-fuck at this point for buying into Android. On the contrary, if you wish to \"Red Pill\" a Nexus user, you don't start off the argument by calling them a dumb fuck. In fact, you need to be sure to NEVER call them a dumb fuck throughout the entire presentation. And yes, he is calling members of the audience who disagrees with him, dumb fucks. [VIDEO] > Dumb Fucks. And maybe we deserve it. Lets be frank here, his point is \"If you disagree with me, you're a Dumb Fuck\". That is about as disparaging as you can get. Imagine if you will, you were a normal person who didn't care about this privacy stuff. None of the above techniques would win you over. In fact, I'm probably going to have to apologize on behalf of privacy advocates for this guy's disparaging remarks. Again, I fully agree with what he's _trying_ to do. But he's so disparaging and elitist it is very hard to actually recommend this video to anyone I know. Tell me, would you really have your Grandmother watch this guy, to try and win her vote over to the privacy conscious? These disparaging remarks are fine for rallying up a crowd or preaching to"}],"r":[["UawS3_iuHoA",2],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["HXAW2GseICs",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1],["AItTqnTsVjA",1],["6h3RJhoqgK8",1],["wlR5gYd6um0",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["VnvGwFegbC8",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1]]},"oqu5DjzOBF8":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible, nuanced explanation of the mechanics of global warming, including the saturation puzzle.","q":{"x":"this video explains the mechanics of global warming in a way anybody can understand.","i":36004476,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":45515385,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2025-10","story":"Eliminating contrails from flying could be cheap","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting, weird explanation of how the greenhouse effect works","quote":"it's interesting and weird","text":"Related context: Sabine Hossenfelder's explanation of how the greenhouse effect works -- it's interesting and weird: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43165764,"author":"jeffbee","month":"2025-02","story":"The Upper Atmosphere Is Cooling, Prompting New Climate Concerns","stance":"rec","why":"Explains stratospheric cooling as a climate change outcome","quote":"This Sabine Hossenfelder video helped me understand stratospheric cooling as a climate change outcome.","text":"This Sabine Hossenfelder video helped me understand stratospheric cooling as a climate change outcome. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39510440,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2024-02","story":"Xkcd: Greenhouse Effect","stance":"rec","why":"Explains greenhouse effect beyond infrared trapping","quote":"here's an explanation of how the greenhouse effect works","text":"For anyone who's curious, here's an explanation of how the greenhouse effect works -- it's not just that some infrared radiation escapes from earth to space, while some other infrared radiation gets trapped. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36004476,"author":"jandrese","month":"2023-05","story":"The Upper Atmosphere Is Cooling, Prompting New Climate Concerns","stance":"rec","why":"Explains global warming mechanics accessibly","quote":"this video explains the mechanics of global warming in a way anybody can understand.","text":"If you are confused as to why this is a bad thing this video explains the mechanics of global warming in a way anybody can understand. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36081139,"author":"orwin","month":"2023-05","story":"Emissions are no longer following the worst case scenario","stance":"rec","why":"How the greenhouse effect really works","quote":"Here is how the earth 'greenhouse effect' really works","text":"This is good news, somehow. Here is how the earth 'greenhouse effect' really works: [VIDEO] This is why Co2 do not have any effect until it reaches the high troposphere. That takes 20 years. The average climate we have right now is caused by emissions from 2003. I'll repeat that in the comments of every climate article I read by the way, sorry for the repetition. M"},{"id":35675856,"author":"smonn_","month":"2023-04","story":"Methane may not warm the Earth quite as much as previously thought","stance":"rec","why":"Nuanced story behind climate change","quote":"I'd recommend watching this video if you'd like to know the more nuanced story behind climate change","text":"I'd recommend watching this video if you'd like to know the more nuanced story behind climate change: [VIDEO] I am not qualified at all to really comment on it but this did open my eyes to how confusing the entire thing is."},{"id":40374360,"author":"mapt","month":"2024-05","story":"How CO2 warms Earth and why CO2 is not 'saturated' in Earth's atmosphere","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See also","text":"See also [VIDEO] And note that if you think \"Maybe we shouldn't be worried until we understand this\" In the past ~14 months things have taken off in ways that nobody who studies this stuff can fully explain yet (leading theory: cleaner fuel -> less sulfur dioxide forcing). We're 4 to 5 standard deviations from the 30-year mean in sea surface temperature right now. [LINK] The half of the human population that lives in South, East, and Southeast Asia have been under oppressive heatwaves in the past few days that have taken a sizable death toll. [LINK] The USDA just adjusted its planting map using 2022 data and indicated that average winter low temperatures in the US have risen 2.5 degrees over the past 10 years, and continue to rise. It's looking like extreme wet bulb events and the peripheral consequences of extreme wet bulb events are going to be perhaps the biggest consequence of failing to control CO2 emissions, as the estimate of survivable wet bulb temperatures have been revised down, their importance has been revised up, and the number of outlier heatwaves have ticked up (Vancouver? Really?!). Less sea level rise, less crop failures from weather pattern changes, less hurricanes. Billions of people who come to recognize that an unluckily sunny August week without a breeze can kill a majority of the local population, are going to want to mitigate that risk. Heat pump air conditioning & insulated housing for every single temperate & tropical human residence would work as an"}],"r":[["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["l70iRcSxqzo",1],["niKblgZupOc",1]]},"3-son3EJTrU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a short, clear explanation of why people avoid saying things explicitly and how common knowledge works socially and politically.","q":{"x":"this wonderful RSA Animate of Steve Pinker's discussion of how we use language","i":9693090,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9693090,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2015-06","story":"Taarof","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pinker on why we use ambiguous language, relevant to polite ritual like taarof.","quote":"this wonderful RSA Animate of Steve Pinker's discussion of how we use language","text":"It sounds like the ambiguity of it all is a feature, which makes me think of this wonderful RSA Animate of Steve Pinker's discussion of how we use language: [VIDEO] I have a feeling a lot of the insights of this talk also hint at why something ta'arof can be useful to a human society."},{"id":14966763,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-08","story":"The Kolmogorov option","stance":"rec","why":"Short Pinker video explaining mutual knowledge via the emperor's new clothes.","quote":"This short Stephen Pinker video[1] explains it really well","text":"> the vast majority of people might actually wish for rebellion, but none of them actually acts out on that wish. You're talking about Mutual Knowledge[0]. Basically, the Emperor's New Clothes scenario: I know the emperor is naked, but does everyone else know and do I want to risk myself finding out? This short Stephen Pinker video[1] explains it really well: [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11705090,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2016-05","story":"Can Multilingualism Survive?: How cities preserve and abandon languages","stance":"rec","why":"Pinker on why people avoid saying things explicitly and how language excludes outsiders.","quote":"this[0] RSA Animate by Steve Pinker about why we don't just say things explicitly most of the time","text":"You make a very reasonable but demonstrably false assumption about language: that it is all about A trying to communicate an idea to B, as clearly and as efficiently as possible, and all involved are cooperative. Reality is often much more complicated. Take the simple example of A talking to B with listener C. There's the scenario where a A wants to talk to B but without being understood by C. Or the reverse where A wants C to understand something but not B, whilst talking to B. And we can go on like this - see for example, this[0] RSA Animate by Steve Pinker about why we don't just say things explicitly most of the time. As a result of all this we get stuff like jargon, coded slang, read-between-the-lines expressions, and yes: foreign languages. Because if you apply that insight to human groups, it becomes obvious that with human \"tribes\", the use of language is as much a way of excluding outsiders as it is about clear communication. In this light, stuff like youth slang always evolving into something unintelligible to adults makes perfect sense. As long as there is a desire for selecting who doesn't understand you (which I think will always be the case), language diversification will be a thing. If you have access to New Scientist articles, this one[1] sums it up nicely [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":2843558,"author":"dbuxton","month":"2011-08","story":"Randi Zuckerberg Runs in the Wrong Direction on Pseudonymity Online","stance":"rec","why":"Pinker explains why shared communication matters for free societies and assembly.","quote":"talks about why this type of communication is essential to free societies and why freedom of assembly is protected in all free societies","text":"I was talking about something similar earlier today with a friend from high school. We were talking about how it was totally un-cool to disagree with the \"consensus\" about which girls were hot and which were not. We were imagining what it might have been like if we'd felt able to speak our minds. I'm not a huge believer in the whole \"social media toppled authoritarian regimes\" stories that have been going around after Tunisia/Egypt but I do think that to the extent real anonymity is possible it has the potential to help people living in places where dissent is both stigmatised and punished by allowing them to realise that others feel the same way. This Steven Pinker talk [VIDEO] (I think; I don't have Flash so can't check it's the right one) talks about why this type of communication is essential to free societies and why freedom of assembly is protected in all free societies. On the same general topic researchers in public health give people some wiggle room on truth telling by introducing random noise into their responses in order to improve the aggregate quality of information you get from asking them questions about controversial topics (drug use, sexuality): [LINK] tl;dr: anything that helps people tell the truth rather than timidly conforming to social norms is a good thing"},{"id":30069179,"author":"js8","month":"2022-01","story":"Are popular toxicity models simply profanity detectors?","stance":"reference","why":"Pinker on why humans use ambiguity and double-entendres in language.","quote":"Humans are inventing double-entendres to create the ambiguity.","text":"> Has anyone realised yet that almost all of this folly? This guy did: [VIDEO] Humans are inventing double-entendres to create the ambiguity."},{"id":20730151,"author":"chewbacha","month":"2019-08","story":"Censorship in China Allows Govt Criticism but Silences Collective Expression [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Pinker relates the emperor's new clothes story to mutual knowledge and tyranny.","quote":"Stephen Pinker [1] gave a talk and related this to the story of the emperors new cloths.","text":"This is why the first amendment of the US constitution had the notion freedom of assembly. Collective or mutual knowledge is important for a social body to protect itself from tyranny. Stephen Pinker [1] gave a talk and related this to the story of the emperors new cloths. The gist is that independently, everyone knew that the emperor was naked, but it wasn’t until it was publicly exclaimed by a child that the independent knowledge became mutual, thus making it socially OK for the population at large to say what they already knew. Dictatorships and authoritarian regimes understand this and remove the ability to congregate openly on how the system works. They disappear dissidents and control the media. They essentially gaslight the citizenry in the hopes that independent suffering stays independent and never is discussed openly where it could incite rebellion. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19414556,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2019-03","story":"What the Hell Is Going On? Effects of Information Abundance","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It discounts without comment the possibility that the pre-internet style of governance had substantive problems which the working class didn't like (e.g. globalization leading to a race to the bottom in manufacturing wages). It's there between the lines: the author mentions the power of the big companies in the 20th century to shape the narrative through mass media and a lack of proper information among the public, and how that power is now crumbling and how the narrative is being democratized. Basically, it boils down to mutual knowledge, and large shifts in that causes power-shifts in society. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16658813,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2018-03","story":"Review of Steven Pinker’s Enlightenment Now","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> As I read Pinker, I sometimes imagined a book published in 1923 about the astonishing improvements in the condition of Europe’s Jews following their emancipation. Such a book might argue: look, obviously past results don’t guarantee future returns; all this progress could be wiped out by some freak future event. But for that to happen, an insane number of things would need to go wrong simultaneously: not just one European country but pretty much all of them would need to be taken over by antisemitic lunatics who were somehow also hyper-competent, and who wouldn’t just harass a few Jews here and there until the lunatics lost power, but would systematically hunt down and exterminate all of them with an efficiency the world had never before seen. Also, for some reason the Jews would need to be unable to escape to Palestine or the US or anywhere else. So the sane, sober prediction is that things will just continue to improve, of course with occasional hiccups (but problems are solvable). > Or I thought back to just a few years ago, to the wise people who explained that, sure, for the United States to fall under the control of a racist megalomaniac like Trump would be a catastrophe beyond imagining. Were such a comic-book absurdity realized, there’d be no point even discussing “how to get democracy back on track”; it would already have suffered its extinction-level event. But the good news is that it will never happen, because the voters won’t allow it: a white nationalist autho"},{"id":10231744,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-09","story":"'Dissent,' a New Type of Security Tool, Could Improve Online Anonymity","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, anonymity by itself won't save you, though can sharply decreases the odds of getting caught if you are careful. What anonymity does instead is probably the most important part of starting any movement that is mutually exclusive with \"acceptable\" political views. When a political situation starts to work against the interested of the common person, most people tend to notice, at least in some small way. If nothing else, they notice some of the harmful effects that are starting to affect them. As they (greatly) outnumber the people \"in charge\", they only need to use their strength in numbers (either politely with the vote/etc or violently with the pitchfork and molotov. Unfortunately, this can be prevented by creating a situation where people fear retribution and fear that any action they take will not be successful. When you believe that not enough people would join you or that there is no way you to achieve that critical mass of people, learned helplessness sets in. It becomes \"obvious\" that \"fighting corruption is futile\", when it really is as simple as everybody simply trying . This is why the 1st Amendment has a right to assembly , and why \"free speech zones\" and other obvious abridgements of that right were important to fight. It's also why a lot of people in power got very, very scared at the \"Occupy\" movement. People assembling so they can see first-hand that they are not alone in their views is one of the only ways to undo the learned helplessness. It is hard to ar"},{"id":5839682,"author":"jimworm","month":"2013-06","story":"Obama: No warrantless wiretaps if you elect me (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If the sole effect of voting is to choose a winner, then you're probably right. But I think that's not the only effect that comes out of counting the votes. It's the one place where voters actually lay their cards on the table, or rather cast their votes in the ballot box. Votes are not a limitless resource to voters, unlike words or even opinion polls, and it is an important form of communication to other voters in the next election. This is where gaming the voting system hurts democracy. Simply voting for the biggest bloc that is not your vision of pure evil is short-sighted, and causes the voters lose their independence - important for a voting system to arrive at an optimum result [1], and it also destroys the only credible method of communicating the lack of confidence in the system to each other [2]. Everybody knowing that everybody else knows that everybody have lost faith in the main blocs is not sufficient to create real change, but it certainly is necessary. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2618102,"author":"Joakal","month":"2011-06","story":"Behavioral reprogramming (overcoming social anxiety)","stance":"reference","why":"Animated Pinker talk on how people use indirect language in social situations.","quote":"","text":"OP's condition said it was social anxiety than social awkwardness to which my post was asking for a more overall solution to social issues. To answer your question, I think his sessions were pretty beneficial but I think missed one thing that I believe therapists should be aware, some socially awkward people are oblivious. There's body language [0], there's ways of talking about or asking for things (don't do it directly sometimes) [1], etc. Some people can't magically overcome that when meeting people but I believe they can learn (at risk of exacerbating their self-consciousness). [0] How Do You Use Body Language to Read Minds?; [LINK] (It's said to be inaccurate, I'm not sure either) [1] RSA Animate - Language as a Window into Human Nature; [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"NcOb3Dilzjc":{"wy":"Recommended whenever outages and cascading failures come up, as a classic on interdependent systems.","q":{"x":"You may enjoy the first episode of James Burke's Connections (\"The Trigger Effect\"), if you've not seen it.","i":36794134,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48094279,"author":"HocusLocus","month":"2026-05","story":"The Greatest Shot in Television: James Burke Had One Chance to Nail This Scene","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic Burke episode opening in the World Trade Center elevator","quote":"I was one of the finest hours ever produced for television.","text":"The \"Trigger Effect\" episode ( [VIDEO] ) that set off Connections:I was one of the finest hours ever produced for television. It influenced my family and I in the years to come. Hope for the best, while preparing for the worst. It begins at South Tower of the World Trade Center, and as Burke enters an express elevator (Car 6 or 7 if you must know) he says in effect, \"Do I worry about ascending to the top in a steel box? Of course I don't! We take going up in the world like that for granted.\" When the elevator goes dark he says. \"We expect this to happen sometimes so what do we do? We strike a light and look around in the dark for an emergency button to push and is we find one, we press it.\" In this discussion of single takes, note what the temperature of the butane lighter must have been by the time he finishes his speech. Ouch! Also interesting to note in the realm of happenstance: the airliner portrayed in trouble over darkened New York was flight 911."},{"id":41009160,"author":"01100011","month":"2024-07","story":"CrowdStrike Update: Windows Bluescreen and Boot Loops","stance":"rec","why":"Interconnected systems can fail spectacularly","quote":"This might be a good time for folks to go back and watch the first episode of James Burke's Connections","text":"This might be a good time for folks to go back and watch the first episode of James Burke's Connections: The Trigger Effect [VIDEO] Interconnected systems can fail spectacularly in unforeseen ways. Strange that something so obvious is so often dismissed or overlooked."},{"id":36794134,"author":"bamfly","month":"2023-07","story":"Cold restart whole system after total outage","stance":"rec","why":"Connections episode on system dependence","quote":"You may enjoy the first episode of James Burke's Connections (\"The Trigger Effect\"), if you've not seen it.","text":"You may enjoy the first episode of James Burke's Connections (\"The Trigger Effect\"), if you've not seen it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33716163,"author":"aequitas","month":"2022-11","story":"How long would society last during a total grid collapse?","stance":"rec","why":"Burke series on grid collapse and interconnected systems","quote":"It's an old series, but well worth the watch.","text":"This resonates very much with the Connections[0] series by James Burke. It's an old series, but well worth the watch. Back then it already discussed what would happen if the grid would collapse, and how everything is interconnected. Now with the internet it has gotten even worse. I think the questions asked back then in the series are still very relevant and maybe even more so nowadays. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35693799,"author":"yamtaddle","month":"2023-04","story":"The Geomagnetic Storm Continues","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on how society breaks down within days of power loss","quote":"TL;DW shit gets very, very dicey in a matter of days if the power goes out","text":"[VIDEO] Connections , episode 1, \"The Trigger Effect\". TL;DW shit gets very, very dicey in a matter of days if the power goes out—and most of our ways of recovering with any speed rely on power so if it goes out for most or all of the world at once, that's extremely bad . If it hit the wrong time of year, I could easily see a global power outage killing millions in the very first day. Loss of air conditioning and heating could push it into the millions all on their own."}],"r":[["UJ7Qc3bsxjI",1],["PdO3IP0Pro8",1],["Gksxu-yeWcU",1],["OMi6xgdSbMA",1]]},"Obt-vMVdM8s":{"wy":"The canonical recommendation for understanding the GIL, praised both for its clarity and for how well it explains a deep technical topic.","q":{"x":"My favorite example is Beazley's GIL talk","i":7092622,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7092622,"author":"famousactress","month":"2014-01","story":"Code is not Literature","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deeply understood code explained to audience","quote":"My favorite example is Beazley's GIL talk","text":"\"The point of such a presentation is to take a piece of code that the presenter has understood deeply and for them to help the audience understand the core ideas...\" I do get lots of value out of that. My favorite example is Beazley's GIL talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32419870,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2022-08","story":"How to choose the right Python concurrency API","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates GIL thrashing and degradation with more cores","quote":"David Beazley does a much better illustrating the nuances than I ever could","text":"I am not expert, and this might be dated information, but historically there is a \"thrashing\" introduced between the GIL and OS scheduled threads which has surprising performance implications. David Beazley does a much better illustrating the nuances than I ever could: [VIDEO] Including a wild phenomenon where a threaded Python program's performance can degrade by increasing number of CPU cores available to a system. Coroutines avoid this by not requiring individual thread scheduling by the OS. So they effectively release the GIL like threads on IO but avoid OS level thread switching."},{"id":17648774,"author":"devxpy","month":"2018-07","story":"Code with Mu: a simple Python editor for beginner programmers","stance":"rec","why":"Famous GIL talk, mind-bending","quote":"here's one of the famous ones on GIL.","text":"David probably isnt the best guy for that. But here's one of the famous ones on GIL. [VIDEO] Beware though, his talks are known to be devastating to the programmer, resulting in serious head injuries, trauma, headaches and other fun stuff."},{"id":5818920,"author":"Luyt","month":"2013-06","story":"Python's Hardest Problem (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"David Beazley explains the GIL well","quote":"David Beazley does a good job on explaining the GIL.","text":"\"I never actually got around to learning -- or even thinking about!-- what exactly the GIL is\" David Beazley does a good job on explaining the GIL. There are multiple videos of his GIL talks on YouTube. This one, for instance: [VIDEO] He has a page about this at [LINK]"},{"id":37961690,"author":"rvanlaar","month":"2023-10","story":"Progress on No-GIL CPython","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Python 3.2 faster on one CPU than dual core","quote":"There's a GIL presentation [1] from 2010 which shows Python 3.2 on a 1 CPU machine was faster than on a dual core.","text":"There's a GIL presentation [1] from 2010 which shows Python 3.2 on a 1 CPU machine was faster than on a dual core. In all, there might be reason to expect GILless python to be faster single core in certain scenario's. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23500160,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2020-06","story":"Async Python is not faster","stance":"reference","why":"Shows multi-threaded Python can be slower due to lock contention","quote":"David Beazley did a talk illustrating this in 2010","text":"> > Python's multiprocessing library is needed to overcome the GIL > No it's not, just use threads. I just wanted to expand on this a little to describe some of the downsides to threads in Python. Multi-threaded logic can be (and often is) slower than single-threaded logic because threading introduces overhead of lock contention and context switching. David Beazley did a talk illustrating this in 2010: [VIDEO] He also did a great talk about coroutines in 2015 where he explores threading and coroutines a bit more: [LINK] In workloads that are often \"blocked\" like network calls our I/O bound work loads, threads can provide similar benefits to coroutines but with overhead. Coroutines seek to provide the same benefit without as much overhead (no lock contention, fewer context switches by the kernel). It's probably not the right guidelines for everyone but I generally use these when thinking about concurrency (and pseudo-concurrency) in Python: - Coroutines where I can. - Multi-processing where I need real concurrency. - Never threads."},{"id":13562988,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2017-02","story":"The multiprocessing module in Python","stance":"reference","why":"Threading context-switching overhead","quote":"","text":"I would recommend you use coroutines rather than multiprocessing.dummy for balancing blocking operations (like IO). I believe multiprocessing.dummy is just a wrapper for threading which has context switching overhead [1]. Python3 has asyncio as part of the stdlib but for Python2 there are a variety of coroutine libraries out there (I think the most widely recognized being gevent). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13484056,"author":"zerkten","month":"2017-01","story":"Problems I Have with Python","stance":"reference","why":"Well-researched critique of the GIL","quote":"","text":"> If you want speed you go to any other scripting language (other than Ruby). Ruby has historically had the same issues. Most Pythonistas I know aren't so evangelical. It's mostly a question of how to go about integrating C/C++ code. Many people complaining about the GIL (and the like) have some naive microbenchmark, don't understand the trade-offs/limitations of their runtime etc. That doesn't mean critique isn't important and required, but it's going to be better when it's properly researched and improves on the body of work out there ( [VIDEO] ). > Transpiling it to another language like Google did, shows that the underlying technology is not worth much. How is this any general indicator of the worth of the language? It shows for some cases, that Google thought this was a worthwhile investment. Google has experimented for a long time with ways to improve how Python code can be run. They ran the Unladen Swallow project, but spent more time on LLVM issues at the time making it infeasible to continue the project. They'll discontinue one path and try another. None of this is really a commentary from Google on CPython, the community, or the value that it has for most people. The people working on this stuff interact in a pretty friendly basis."}],"r":[["P3AyI_u66Bw",1],["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1],["Y4Gt3Xjd7G8",1]]},"Av0PQDVTP4A":{"wy":"HN recommends it in literate programming threads as a rare look at applying the idea to a huge, complex codebase, though some find it meandering.","q":{"x":"well worth watching: it talks about real, huge comolex systems, and how lp can help bring them back under control","i":11688446,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":14442369,"author":"elviejo","month":"2017-05","story":"Show HN: Legion, an as-simple-as-possible blockchain server written in Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on writing programs as readable books.","quote":"A nice talk about it","text":"\"Programs should be written for people to read, and only incidentally for machines to execute.\" (Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs\" by Abelson and Sussman) The idea is that you write a \"Book\" and the program is extracted, from the book. Literate Programming Wikipedia: [LINK] A nice talk about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12049239,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-07","story":"Literate programming: presenting code in human order","stance":"rec","why":"Rewriting Axiom as a literate program for maintainability.","quote":"There's a (somewhat rambling, but interesting) talk on the effort to rewrite the lisp symbolic math system Axiom[a]","text":"There's a (somewhat rambling, but interesting) talk on the effort to rewrite the lisp symbolic math system Axiom[a] as a literate program (spanning several books) - precisely in order to make it possible to refactor and maintain the code: \"Literate Programming in the Large\": [VIDEO] [a] [LINK]"},{"id":11688446,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-05","story":"Final “STEPS Toward the Reinvention of Programming” Paper [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on using literate programming for huge codebases.","quote":"well worth watching: it talks about real, huge comolex systems, and how lp can help bring them back under control","text":"I think there are two sound approaches to communicating complex systems between humans (which is the main goal here, a computer might be a bit slow compiling 200M LOC - but the real problem is that no human can read that much code, let alone understand it): 1) Write documentation that is also code. That is the idea of literate programming. I recently came across: \"Literate Programming in the Large\": [VIDEO] which is a bit of a wandering talk, but well worth watching: it talks about real, huge comolex systems, and how lp can help bring them back under control. 2) The other approach is to shift your perspective, so you don't have a complex solution to a compkex problem; but rather a few small and lightly connected straight forward solutions to simple problems. Now I doesn't matter if you're bad at communicating, because the ideas you try to convey are simple ideas. I think there are a few reason that STEPS appear to work: what we're doing with our complex software is simple stuff. If we know what we want to do (the hard part) doing so in a straightforward way becomes easy. If we can make hierarchies of abstraction and isolated sub-systems. Note that simple doesn't mean trivial. These solution often require quite a lot of theoretical education; but not an unreasonable amount."},{"id":11699137,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-05","story":"Literate programming: Two beefs with the classic version (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Reviving a 1.2 MLOC lisp codebase with literate programming.","quote":"it's a bit meandering, but INHO well worth a watch","text":"I found this talk about re-structuring and bringing back to life a 1.2 MLOC lisp codebase with classical LP interesting (it's a bit meandering, but INHO well worth a watch): \"Literate Programming in the Large\": [VIDEO] The reorganization is still ongoing, as can been seen at: [LINK]"},{"id":7613565,"author":"sparkie","month":"2014-04","story":"You Don't Read Code, You Explore It","stance":"rec","why":"Tim Daly on why code should be written as manuals.","quote":"giving high level overviews of our problems and specifying their implementation details inside the manuals.","text":"I like Tim Daly's viewpoint that we shouldn't just be writing code , but we should write manuals - giving high level overviews of our problems and specifying their implementation details inside the manuals. His talk \"Literate Programming in the Large\"[1] covers why. The example he gives in his talk is the Axiom algebra system[2], which was revised to use literate programming style - the source code, with usage examples, is contained entirely within the books. [1]: [VIDEO] , [sldes]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":38979540,"author":"daly","month":"2024-01","story":"Axiom – A scientific computation system","stance":"reference","why":"Author discussing his Axiom work and its structure.","quote":"","text":"Thanks for the \"all the work\" complement. Despite all, it is more fun than work. Computer algebra algorithms are basically functions. Axiom is unique in that it organizes the functions based on a group theory scaffold. Thus you can know that certain matrices commute but rectangular ones don't. Algorithms that know how to commute live in the commutative \"category\" in Axiom. So the game is to distribute LEAN's commutative axioms in their proper place in the group theory inheritance scaffold so they are available when they apply. Axiom arranges the world into \"categories\" (not category theory stuff) and \"domains\". The LEAN structure is a third structure (actually fourth but...) built in parallel so that a theorem that proves that certain matrices commute will only be available when it applies. When you try to prove your algorithm you have a collection of LEAN axioms, definitions, and tactics that are valid and apply to your particular function. If your algorithm used rectangular matrices the commutative theorems would not be visible and thus not available in a proof step. Due to the hierarchical nature, once you prove an algorithm you can also use it in other downstream proofs, for example, using proven data structures and their properties. I have not published any papers on the subject. I'm no longer anywhere in the academic pipeline so a paper wouldn't get very far. Plus this is \"in the cracks\" kind of research that doesn't seem to fit into either camp. As for the education or"},{"id":29861508,"author":"daly","month":"2022-01","story":"Literate programming: Knuth is doing it wrong (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own talk on literate programming.","quote":"I've even given a talk on the subject","text":"You wrote \"There's a fundamental problem with generating a beautifully typeset document for a codebase: it's dead. It can't render inside just about any actual programming environment (editor or IDE) on this planet, and so we can't make changes to it while we work on the codebase.\" Sorry, that's not correct. When writing a literate program you should also include a chunk containing a Makefile. Extract the Makefile and let it construct the program. I did this with the whole Clojure programming language. The whole source code for all of Clojure, as well as the test cases, are in the PDF. The sequence (from scratch is): Extract the Makefile from the Latex document. Run the Makefile. This: 1) extracts the code and tests 2) compiles the code 3) runs the tests 4) recreates the PDF from the Latex So you edit the Latex text and/or code with any editor and then re-run the Makefile. This will rebuild your program, runs the tests, and re-makes the PDF. If you have the PDF open next to your editor it is likely that the PDF viewer will reload the changes immediately. This ensures that (a) your program compiles, (b) your program passes tests, and (c) the PDF builds correctly. I've been working on literate programs for years. See [LINK] . I've even given a talk on the subject See [VIDEO] ). I find literate programming to be the MOST important change in programming since I started in 1970."},{"id":10071207,"author":"daly","month":"2015-08","story":"Ask HN: Why did literate programming not catch on?","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own talk on literate programming's value.","quote":"I gave a talk on this subject at the WriteTheDocs conference","text":"I've been using literate programming for 15 years on many projects. The largest and most visible one is Axiom ( [LINK] ) which currently has many thousands of pages with embedded code. I've talked to Knuth. He claims he could not have implemented MMIX without literate programming. Literate programming is really valuable but you only understand that once you really try it. I gave a talk on this subject at the WriteTheDocs conference: [VIDEO] You can write a literate program in any language, for instance, in HTML: [LINK] There are some \"gold standard\" literate programs: \"Physically Based Rendering\" by Pharr and Humphreys won an academy award. \"Lisp in Small Pieces\" contains a complete lisp implementation including the interpreter and compiler. The book \"Implementing Elliptic Curve Cryptography\" is another example. Suppose your business depends on a program. Suppose your team leaves (they all do eventually). Suppose you need to change it... THAT's why you need literate programming. Nobody is going be around to remember that the strange block of code is there to handle Palm Pilots. Companies should hire language majors, make them Editor-in-Chief, and put them on every programming team. Nobody checks in code until there is at least a paragraph that explains WHY this code was written. Anybody can figure out WHAT it does but reverse-engineering WHY it does it can be hard. Imagine a physics textbook that was \"just the equations\" without any surrounding text. That's the way we write c"}],"r":[["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["30jNsCVLpAE",1],["FV0DXNB94NE",1],["Nbj1AR_aAcE",1],["-Ht4qiDRZE8",1],["dljNabciEGg",1]]},"KaOC9danxNo":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it for the sense of wonder and chills it gives, and bring it up in threads about Bowie, space and astronauts.","q":{"x":"It gives me chills every time I listen to it.","i":18011962,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18011962,"author":"toomanybeersies","month":"2018-09","story":"Dear Moon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hadfield cover on the ISS that gives chills","quote":"It gives me chills every time I listen to it.","text":"Chris Hadfield recorded a cover of Bowie's Space Oddity on the ISS [1], including playing the guitar in space. It gives me chills every time I listen to it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10879358,"author":"LeoPanthera","month":"2016-01","story":"David Bowie Has Died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Chris Hadfield's Space Oddity cover recorded on the ISS","quote":"if you haven't seen Chris Hadfield's cover of Space Oddity, recorded on the ISS, you really should","text":"The chances that anyone here hasn't seen it are almost zero, but just in case, if you haven't seen Chris Hadfield's cover of Space Oddity, recorded on the ISS, you really should: [VIDEO] I don't have a source but I heard that David preferred this version to the original."},{"id":12812893,"author":"redthrowaway","month":"2016-10","story":"What Sid Meier’s Video Game Empire Got Right and Wrong About ‘Civilization’","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a sense of wonder and pride in humanity","quote":"gives me the same sense of wonder and pride in humanity","text":"Complaints about Civ's omission of the dark side of history ring hollow to me. Civ is, at its heart, a deeply aspirational and optimistic game. Watching the Baba Yetu intro from Civ IV[1] gives me the same sense of wonder and pride in humanity that Chris Hadfield's rendition of Space Oddity[2] does. Dwelling on the darkness in our history would be so discordant for a game like Civ. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43791693,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-04","story":"DeepMind releases Lyria 2 music generation model","stance":"reference","why":"Bowie covered in space","quote":"And Bowie's been covered in space","text":"To quote the wikipedia article: > When Danse macabre was first performed on 24 January 1875, it was not well received and caused widespread feelings of anxiety. The 21st century scholar, Roger Nichols, mentions adverse reaction to \"the deformed Dies irae plainsong\", the \"horrible screeching from solo violin\", the use of a xylophone, and \"the hypnotic repetitions\", in which Nichols hears a pre-echo of Ravel's Boléro. And Bowie's been covered in space: [VIDEO] It's impossible to forecast what future generations will and won't like."},{"id":36393994,"author":"_Microft","month":"2023-06","story":"‘Bowie said my Space Oddity was the most poignant version ever'","stance":"reference","why":"Chris Hadfield's cover of Space Oddity","quote":"","text":"Here's the link to Chris Hadfield's cover/version of \"Space Oddity\" since it's missing from the article: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23416037,"author":"zubspace","month":"2020-06","story":"Kraftwerk played live with an astronaut on the ISS (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Hadfield performed Space Oddity on the ISS in 2013","quote":"","text":"Also reminds me of \"Space Oddity\" [1] performed by Chris Hadfield [2] in 2013 on the IIS. He even released an album with songs recorded up there [3]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":18784889,"author":"dpflan","month":"2018-12","story":"An Introduction to Spacecraft Operations [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Hadfield's Space Oddity music video from the ISS","quote":"","text":"Related: I saw that Canadian astronaut, Chris Hadfield, is offering a class on \"Space Exploration\" on the Master Class site. I haven't used MC before, so I cannot attest to the quality of the offering, but it seems interesting. > MC Course Site : [LINK] > Chris Hadfield's 'Space Oddity' music video from ISS : [VIDEO]"},{"id":18268954,"author":"raattgift","month":"2018-10","story":"Sigmund Jähn, the first German to travel into space","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Perhaps Canada isn't sufficiently \"grand\", but: [LINK] and [LINK] respectively the first Canadian and just one of the twelve Canadians to have been in space. How about Spain? Recently: [LINK] (There are at least two current MEPs who have been in space, but I will grant you that few people in most EU member-states can name their own MEP, let alone any of the others elected from their country). One might also compare John Glenn, who while alive, was certainly well known in the USA: [LINK] Certainly there are many U.S. and Canadian astronauts who have never been anything close to well known public figures, but both sets certainly have enjoyed state propaganda (even on the official state broadcaster): [LINK] (for example) Perhaps \"grand scheme\" implies international name recognition? How soon Chris Hadfield's international prominence fades away is an open question; he certainly appears in the UK often enough (he was a \"get\" -- as in an invited guest the production staff felt fortunate to have present -- on more than one of the the annual BBC astronomy shows put together by Brian Cox and Dara Ó Briaian) and he has been on a number of other broadcasts as part of an \"outreach\" programme. You yourself may well remember him from [VIDEO]"},{"id":6698001,"author":"Mindless2112","month":"2013-11","story":"Co-Founder of Youtube makes his first comment in 8 years","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Headline is inaccurate, though I can't really blame the submitter. jawed occasionally makes comments on YouTube (example: [1]), but eventually hides everything but YouTube's first video from his channel (presumably so he can \"make his first comment in X years\" which then gets headlines). [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["u404SLJj7ig",1],["IJiHDmyhE1A",1],["kU3BceoMuaA",1],["_-mBeYC2KGc",1],["CJlrbMHLBd4",1],["ar-poc38C84",1]]},"V4V2bpZlqx8":{"wy":"HN points to it as a short, clear explanation of why Enigma was breakable and how search-space elimination works.","q":{"x":"Numberphile gives a nice, succinct explanation","i":15359599,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":44836845,"author":"robaato","month":"2025-08","story":"Encryption made for police and military radios may be easily cracked","stance":"rec","why":"A proper explanation of Enigma's weakness.","quote":"Also a more proper explanation","text":"There's the entertaining bit in the film The Imitation Game (the breaking of Enigma code by Alan Turing) where they realised that a weakness was the daily weather report always containing the words \"weatherreport\" and \"heil bloody hitler\" (with obvious addition!) [LINK] or longer version: [LINK] Also a more proper explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42638092,"author":"DarkmSparks","month":"2025-01","story":"Cracking a 512-bit DKIM key for less than $8 in the cloud","stance":"rec","why":"Explains search-space elimination like cracking Enigma.","quote":"numberphile has a great video on that one","text":"none of it is \"brute force\", GNFS is a process that rapidly excludes numbers from the search space that cannot be the answer, in principle similar to the way they broke enigma. numberphile has a great video on that one [VIDEO] Also, taking the OP as a \"worse case\", afaik: 512bit = $8 so 1024 = 8^2 = $64 2048 = 8^2^2 = $4,096 4096 = 8^2^2 = $16,777,216 noting $8 for 512 seems very expensive to me."},{"id":15359599,"author":"iClaudiusX","month":"2017-09","story":"Forthcoming book seeks to visualise, locate and expose many numbers stations","stance":"rec","why":"Succinct explanation of Enigma's exploitable flaws.","quote":"Numberphile gives a nice, succinct explanation","text":"In this case, a more relevant example from World War II might be the exploitation of flaws in the Enigma machine, namely: 1) the re-use of common phrases in weather reports, and 2) the fact that a letter could not be encoded as itself in the ciphertext, providing a mechanism of contradiction that drastically sped up elimination of possible settings [LINK] Numberphile gives a nice, succinct explanation [VIDEO]"},{"id":5078782,"author":"sproketboy","month":"2013-01","story":"The Enigma Machine: How Alan Turing Helped Break the Unbreakable Nazi Code","stance":"rec","why":"Describes the flaw that let codebreakers crack Enigma.","quote":"Some people might like this from Numberphile which describes the flaw in the enigma machine","text":"Some people might like this from Numberphile which describes the flaw in the enigma machine that British code breakers used to eventually break the code. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["VnbiVw_1FNs",1],["DKtVpvzUF1Y",1]]},"gSKzgpt4HBU":{"wy":"HN recommends it, and the whole channel, as a clear and deeper-than-usual physics explainer.","q":{"x":"The YouTube channel PBS SpaceTime gives an excellent overview of these same ideas.","i":16310945,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16310945,"author":"doctoboggan","month":"2018-02","story":"Three Meanings of E=mc²","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent, clear overview of mass-energy ideas","quote":"The YouTube channel PBS SpaceTime gives an excellent overview of these same ideas.","text":"The YouTube channel PBS SpaceTime gives an excellent overview of these same ideas. The presenter is great at making things as simple as possible, but not simpler. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":12375727,"author":"acidbaseextract","month":"2016-08","story":"New class of galaxy has been discovered, made almost entirely of dark matter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Directly explains the nature of matter and mass","quote":"This whole channel will be up your alley, but this video and","text":"\"The True Nature of Matter and Mass\" - [VIDEO] This whole channel will be up your alley, but this video and \"The Real Meaning of E=mc^2\" one directly answer your question."},{"id":29953924,"author":"MetricExpansion","month":"2022-01","story":"The Shameful Decline of Scientific American","stance":"rec","why":"Goes beyond general-audience science content in depth","quote":"And there are channels like PBS Spacetime which go far, far beyond anything","text":"Pithy, but I’d actually argue the opposite. As someone who consumes quite a bit of science content on YouTube, it’s actually quite jarring to go back to traditional TV science shows. It feels a lot less information dense and more time-wasting. An interesting example is when Veritasium, who normally does the usual YouTube science content, got some big budget funding for one video [1] and produced it in a style more like what you’d see on TV. It was striking how there was so much filler and dramatization to try to make it interesting, and the whole thing felt dumbed down compared to his usual content, for example [2], which is deeper and relatively information-dense. And there are channels like PBS Spacetime which go far, far beyond anything I’ve ever seen in any media aimed at a general audience [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K_q-xnYRugQ",1],["oLkfnc_UMcE",1],["cQWlnTyOSig",1],["Pi7l8mMjYVE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["czgOWmtGVGs",1],["kixAljyfdqU",1],["dGiQaabX3_o",1]]},"eLn6LC1RpAo":{"wy":"Commenters recommend the whole series as an entertaining survey of constructed languages, and this episode when Toki Pona or Lojban comes up.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching all episodes of their show","i":23411599,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23411599,"author":"anonymfus","month":"2020-06","story":"Toki Pona","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Conlang Critic review of Toki Pona, part of a series","quote":"I highly recommend watching all episodes of their show","text":"Conlang Critic is a fan of this language: [VIDEO] I highly recommend watching all episodes of their show if you like an idea of short text based video essays about constructed languages: [LINK]"},{"id":36010083,"author":"FireInsight","month":"2023-05","story":"Lojban: Constructed language to eliminate ambiguity from communication","stance":"rec","why":"Conlang Critic favors Toki Pona over Lojban","quote":"I take all my conlang opinions from jan Misali aka Conlang Critic","text":"I take all my conlang opinions from jan Misali aka Conlang Critic and he tells me to love toki pona [0] instead of lojban [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":29036314,"author":"rsapkf","month":"2021-10","story":"Toki Pona: A Language with a Hundred Words (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Playlist reviewing constructed languages","quote":"jan Misali has an interesting playlist[0] with reviews on various contructed languages including Toki Pona.","text":"If anyone is interested in learning about conlangs, jan Misali has an interesting playlist[0] with reviews on various contructed languages including Toki Pona. [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9F9ahZQ7oP0",1],["_7X6Yeydgyg",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["xg29TuWo0Yo",1]]},"HbUnatPfSgg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as practical advice on dealing with tech press, whenever someone asks how to pitch TechCrunch and similar outlets.","q":{"x":"Mike Arrington's talk from startup school on dealing with press is quite good","i":546044,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":3932390,"author":"SuperChihuahua","month":"2012-05","story":"Ask HN: Best practices when pitching your startup to Techcrunch, Mashable etc?","stance":"rec","why":"How to get and respond to press for your startup","quote":"Watch this video","text":"Watch this video: Mike Arrington speaks at Startup School 2008 about how to get, and how to respond to press for your startup. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2952605,"author":"pbh","month":"2011-09","story":"Michael Arrington launching venture fund","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into how Arrington views the startup press","quote":"I found Arrington's talk at Startup School '08 to be pretty good","text":"Somewhat related, I found Arrington's talk at Startup School '08 to be pretty good for getting an idea of how he views the startup tech press. [VIDEO]"},{"id":546044,"author":"wheels","month":"2009-04","story":"Ask YC:  How do you get your press?","stance":"rec","why":"Arrington on dealing with press at Startup School","quote":"Mike Arrington's talk from startup school on dealing with press is quite good","text":"Mike Arrington's talk from startup school on dealing with press is quite good: [VIDEO] It's not really geared towards the mainstream press, but a good watch nonetheless."},{"id":2361996,"author":"petercooper","month":"2011-03","story":"You’re not entitled to anything. Hustle for everything you’re worth","stance":"reference","why":"Arrington on getting tech press coverage","quote":"And Michael Arrington spent some time on the topic in this video","text":"Along similar lines, here are some links relating to actually getting coverage in the tech press (rather than sending nasty e-mails as mentioned in this post): [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] And Michael Arrington spent some time on the topic in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22623793,"author":"sgrove","month":"2020-03","story":"Across furtive videocons, junior VCs wait for the layoffs to begin","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I believe their entire credibility model is largely based on this","text":"I believe their entire credibility model is largely based on this, as per the TechCrunch founder himself at Startup School back in 2008 [VIDEO] (the whole talk is certainly a trip back in time!) I remember listening to that and thinking, \"So.... the info I have on close friends and business is valuable, and I should potentially sell it out to TC in order to curry favor.\" I wasn't in SVBA at the time, and even then I felt that it didn't quite match the spirit of the valley (I still believe that to be the case, having been here for 10+ years now)."}],"r":[["HkZDSqyE1do",1],["rnLWSC5p1XE",1]]},"LruaD7XhQ50":{"wy":"Offered as the quick, clear explainer on US federal land ownership whenever land use in the West comes up.","q":{"x":"You might enjoy CGP Grey's \"What is federal land?\" [0].","i":23533921,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24428169,"author":"cbm-vic-20","month":"2020-09","story":"The end of dispersed camping?","stance":"rec","why":"Differences between agencies that own federal land","quote":"CGP Grey did an interesting video on the differences between the agencies that own federal land in the US","text":"CGP Grey did an interesting video on the differences between the agencies that own federal land in the US: [VIDEO] (Start from the beginning for more context)"},{"id":23533921,"author":"closeparen","month":"2020-06","story":"A Guy Named Craig May Soon Have Control over a Large Swath of Utah","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what federal land is and why government owns it","quote":"You might enjoy CGP Grey's \"What is federal land?\" [0].","text":"You might enjoy CGP Grey's \"What is federal land?\" [0]. Making resources available to American industry is one of the government's purposes in owning it. There are certainly disagreements over how well that's balanced against other goals, or against the long term future. But it's not like it's inherently corrupt that federal land is fed into the economy. That's what it's for. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22428858,"author":"smegger001","month":"2020-02","story":"Monopoly was invented to demonstrate the evils of capitalism (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how much western land is federally owned","quote":"CGP Grey had a god video about federal land ownership the majorty of land in western states is federal land.","text":"Yeah CGP Grey had a god video about federal land ownership the majorty of land in western states is federal land. [VIDEO] Its kind of ridiculous. how little control states have over the land within their own boarders."},{"id":19193030,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2019-02","story":"Kodak's Discovery of A-Bomb Testing (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"CGP Grey video on federal land and the BLM","quote":"CGP Grey made a video on the topic recently","text":"> [1] Bureau of Land Management - kind of like a national forest but where they care less about the land under their control. CGP Grey made a video on the topic recently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19251309,"author":"mjevans","month":"2019-02","story":"Oregon expected to enact first state-wide rent control law","stance":"reference","why":"Mentioned among sources about land use in western states","quote":"","text":"Rent control is a bandaid that tries to fix fundamental systemic flaws and lack of planning by society. Problems: They do mention Oregon's land use laws. I didn't see it while skimming but (for west coast states) the land that is actually \"state\" instead of federal is also an issue (#1). However the biggest issue is that the Supply vs Demand curves for housing are simply insane. Demand is mostly dictated by jobs, and only slightly moderated by the quality of public transit and transportation infrastructure (mass transit AND freeways/parking included). As a nation the US has been housing constrained for at least 20 years, probably longer. Worse for a generation or two housing has been used as an investment rather than the cost center that it is; so all sorts of market incentives are massively messed up. Finally \"career\" jobs, which allow someone to live in the same house for their entire adult life more or less began a long slow death in the 1980s, which is the true beginning of the destruction of communities. Solutions to consider: Tax, based on land use opportunity cost/value. This should have a built in history consideration that gradually introduces rising land use potential over a number of years. (E.G. sliding window over 10 or 20 years) Tax, transparently, to fund infrastructure, and provide a place (website?) where someone can go to see what their tax dollars are actually being spent on. How they are getting value for services. Zone and re-zone and do urban development"},{"id":18548010,"author":"dangoor","month":"2018-11","story":"The FBI of the National Park Service","stance":"reference","why":"CGP Grey video on US federal land agencies","quote":"CGP Grey's new video about federal land","text":"Coincidentally, I just learned of the existence of this organization yesterday via CGP Grey's new video about federal land: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["ssZ_8cqfBlE",1],["NMvwHDFVpCE",1]]},"MCVj5RZOqwY":{"wy":"HN points to it as a lost example of intuitive low-code tooling, and as inspiration for newer products.","q":{"x":"The UI was very intuitive, and it did a better job with things like table relations than it's competitors.","i":35166392,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35166392,"author":"tyingq","month":"2023-03","story":"Smalltalk: An Entrepreneur’s Secret Weapon (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Original demo of an intuitive low-code CRUD web app builder.","quote":"The UI was very intuitive, and it did a better job with things like table relations than it's competitors.","text":"A good example in this space was DabbleDB[1], a low-code environment for producing web apps that did CRUD type stuff. Their original video demo: [VIDEO] I was pretty impressed when it came out. The UI was very intuitive, and it did a better job with things like table relations than it's competitors. They were also releasing new features based on requests faster than what I typically saw. It happened to have used the Seaside web framework mentioned in the article. Sadly, Twitter purchased it and shut it down...I assume they just wanted the team and not the product. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18636425,"author":"donarb","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Which abandoned proprietary software would you resurrect?","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to the online spreadsheet-like database DabbleDB.","quote":"this YouTube video gives a great introduction to this online spreadsheet-like database.","text":"DabbleDB, this YouTube video gives a great introduction to this online spreadsheet-like database. Sadly they were bought out by Twitter, never to be seen again. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14015174,"author":"tyingq","month":"2017-04","story":"Google App Maker","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of DabbleDB's smooth UI.","quote":"It had a very smooth UI","text":"I've tried a lot of these tools. The trouble is that things go extremely well until you hit a wall. With no way to extend them with something like freeform code, the wall doesn't move. The best ones, then, tend to be the ones that have been around long enough, or had enough foresight to have covered various corner cases. The other issue is that most of them have \"per user / per month\" pricing, which doesn't work for many use cases. Things like, for example, an applicant tracking app. If you use the functionality to a high degree, every job applicant is another end user license. Two of the better ones: [LINK] & [LINK] Other ones I've seen: [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] , [LINK] One of the more promising ones was DabbleDB. Twitter acquihired them and shut the tool down. It had a very smooth UI: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26663177,"author":"kgodey","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (April 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the inspiration for a product.","quote":"We are heavily inspired by Dabble DB","text":"Center of Complex Interventions | First Frontend Engineer | Remote (forever) | Full Time | Open Source We're looking for our first frontend engineer to help build a brand new open source product that helps users of all skill levels store, manipulate, get insights from, and collaborate with others on their data. We are heavily inspired by Dabble DB ( [VIDEO] ). Our team is based out of the nonprofit Center of Complex Interventions and we are advised by Joi Ito and Reid Hoffman. In addition to great technical skills, we are looking for someone who is an excellent communicator, proactive, product-minded, excited about making somthing new from scratch (with all the collaboration and iteration that entails) and interested in building an open source community around a product. Full job description: [LINK] Apply here: [LINK] Feel free to email me at kriti<at>centerofci<dot>org if you have any questions. Mention that you saw the HN post."},{"id":26304886,"author":"kgodey","month":"2021-03","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (March 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the inspiration for a product.","quote":"We are heavily inspired by Dabble DB","text":"Center of Complex Interventions | Frontend Engineer, Product Designer | REMOTE | Full Time We're looking for our first frontend engineer and product designer to help build a brand new open source product that helps users of all skill levels use, extend, and visualize their data. We are heavily inspired by Dabble DB ( [VIDEO] ). The current team is two engineers, we're alumni of Creative Commons and have experience working on open source projects and building community. We're aiming for an initial team size of four. We're based out of the nonprofit Center of Complex Interventions and are advised by Joi Ito and Reid Hoffman. Full job descriptions and application instructions: Frontend Engineer: [LINK] Product Designer: [LINK] Feel free to email me at kriti<at>centerofci<dot>org if you have any questions. Mention that you saw the HN post."},{"id":7123853,"author":"e12e","month":"2014-01","story":"What I Wish I Had Known Before Selling to Google","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've thought a bit about this, churning around various product ideas. In general I'd say that if it's a typical SaaS, I would probably only trust it with my data, my time (learning curve, adapting workflow etc) if it was a available under a licence that would allow me to self-host it if it was discontinued (or pay someone else to host it for me). This is for stuff like mail, images and other DAM (Digital Asset Management) systems, chat/im, bug trackers/knowledgestores and databases etc. Note, that I don't see any reason to trust Google either. Remember wave? Remember XMPP compatible gtalk? My conclusion has been that if/when I finally get started on my first product, I'll probably license it under AGPL, and make sure there's a realistic self-host story for users -- and then charge for the SaaS/PaaS-bit, but probably not for the client (or server side software under AGPL). Clearly it's a little more challenging -- you might end up with people competing with you for hosting -- but I can't see any other way to build customer trust. Nothing any company would say to me as a customer would make me trust them -- how could I ask anything else from my potential users? Since people still pay for hosted email (even if many, many do run off to freemium services) -- I'd say the business model is viable -- those that can afford to pay, and know that value can be added by proper hosting -- and not having to do everything themselves -- will consider becoming a customer. And I strongly suspec"}],"r":[["LPjzfGChGlE",1],["fifjjacjLBE",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1]]},"VjE0Kdfos4Y":{"wy":"Brought up whenever threads touch on mimicry, voice cloning or bird vocalization, and people say the ending is startling.","q":{"x":"The end of this BBC clip blew my mind the first time I saw it.","i":14188559,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14188559,"author":"coldsmoke","month":"2017-04","story":"Lyrebird – An API to copy the voice of anyone","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lyrebird impersonation clip, end is mind-blowing","quote":"The end of this BBC clip blew my mind the first time I saw it.","text":"I guess they've named it that because the lyrebird is an amazing impersonator. The end of this BBC clip blew my mind the first time I saw it. [VIDEO] But you may have a point, and the ethics section makes it clear that they are indeed very aware of that this may be misused."},{"id":12388077,"author":"paloaltokid","month":"2016-08","story":"The Greater Honeyguide, a Bird That Understands Humans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lyrebird copies bird songs and man-made sounds","quote":"Check out the link, it's mindblowing.","text":"Another amazing bird worth knowing about is the Lyrebird: [VIDEO] It copies not just the songs of other birds, but man-made sounds as well! Check out the link, it's mindblowing."},{"id":36843688,"author":"kristopolous","month":"2023-07","story":"Were small-brained early humans intelligent? Row erupts over scientists’ claim","stance":"reference","why":"Lyrebird vocalization example","quote":"lyre birds are way better at vocalization.","text":"Correct. You can't use a single number to accurately measure something that takes more than a single number to express. It's reductionist and impossible. All kinds of animals are far better then us at specific core brain functions with way smaller brains. Mantis shrimps are way better at vision than we are and lyre birds are way better at vocalization. ( [VIDEO] ) You can find all kinds of vastly superior aspects of systems in the animal world. Ants and bees for instance, are probably better at socializing. Some supercolonies span thousands of miles with hundreds of millions of ants - rivaling the size of human countries. Ants, 0.25mg brains. Doesn't seem that important in organizing either. We're certainly not 5.2 million times better (which is our difference in brain mass) We're the only animals that have built rocketships and computers - so there's certainly something there but it's fleeting and complicated. Reducing it to a single measurement is inherently inaccurate in the same way you cannot describe the material, color, shape and volume of 3 dimensional objects with 1 number. Brain size alone cannot be a complete or useful metric."},{"id":23739471,"author":"sva_","month":"2020-07","story":"A ‘viral’ new bird song in Canada is causing sparrows to change their tune","stance":"reference","why":"Lyrebird imitating chainsaws","quote":"camera shutter, chainsaws [1]","text":"Even more fascinating, in my opinion, are Lyrebirds [0]. They have been observed the imitate artificial (human-made) sounds such as those of camera shutter, chainsaws [1], and construction work [2]. Not only do I find it impressive that they're capable of producing these kinds of sounds, but probably much more the necessary memory-recall for this. Birds have rather little brain-mass after all, so they must have some excellent way of 'sampling', distinguishing, and storing auditory stimuli. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23441462,"author":"emmelaich","month":"2020-06","story":"New slats make the Golden Gate Bridge sound like a David Lynch movie","stance":"reference","why":"Lyrebird imitating a chainsaw, from BBC's Life on Earth","quote":"The lyrebird can imitate a chainsaw -- among many other things.","text":"The lyrebird can imitate a chainsaw -- among many other things. This is from the BBC's Life on Earth. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16659138,"author":"arbie","month":"2018-03","story":"Listening for illegal logging chainsaws using TensorFlow","stance":"reference","why":"Lyrebirds mimic nearly any complex sound","quote":"Lyrebirds can mimic nearly any complex sound","text":"I had to look this up because I though you were jesting. Turns out Lyrebirds can mimic nearly any complex sound: [VIDEO]"},{"id":230529,"author":"ryanmahoski","month":"2008-06","story":"Ask HN: I'm having dinner with the Mythbuster tomorrow night... got any questions?","stance":"reference","why":"Lyrebird mimics camera shutters, car alarms, chainsaws","quote":"The Lyre bird can perfectly mimick lots of man-made sounds: camera shutter, camera with a motor drive, car alarm, chainsaw.","text":"Ever watched a trained parrot? Birds imitate all kinds of sounds. Doubtless a cell phone ring is in the avian call repertoire. The Lyre bird can perfectly mimick lots of man-made sounds: camera shutter, camera with a motor drive, car alarm, chainsaw. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AvEqfg2sIH0",1],["98LdFA-_zfA",1]]},"ZBtMbBPzqHY":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as eyewitness testimony and multi-radar evidence in UFO threads, though some dispute it.","q":{"x":"At least watch this 60 Minutes short piece about this phenomenon","i":34670663,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34670663,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2023-02","story":"As a US Navy fighter pilot, I witnessed unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP)","stance":"rec","why":"Interviews with Navy pilots about unexplained phenomena.","quote":"At least watch this 60 Minutes short piece about this phenomenon","text":"> I wonder how much some of these unidentified phenomena might be checked for some similar effects. come on, man. at some point, after enough of these reports, toeing the line you're toeing starts to look ludicrous. these are trained observers, pilots who know what to expect in the sky. and do not take lightly that there is still a certain amount of embarrassment (a social cost) to even coming forward, so to even make this claim is still a brave act at this point. At least watch this 60 Minutes short piece about this phenomenon with interviews with real Navy pilots before you assert things like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34672840,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2023-02","story":"As a US Navy fighter pilot, I witnessed unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP)","stance":"rec","why":"Short broadcast on pilots seeing unknown objects on radar daily.","quote":"The USG has already admitted these things are real but have an unknown source.","text":"It's an irrelevant example. The question in THIS case is not whether \"the wings fell off before or after hitting the water, if at all\", but whether \"there was a plane hitting the water at all\" (also a plane that was caught on multiple radars). It's simply not a good comparison. The Navy pilots say they see bogies on radar (not just visually) every day . Watch the \"60 Minutes\" broadcast I linked elsewhere (which is only 13 minutes long: [VIDEO] ). The USG has already admitted these things are real but have an unknown source. To continue to maintain extreme skepticism at this point is to bury one's head in the sand."},{"id":30600563,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2022-03","story":"Flight characteristics of anomalous unidentified aerial vehicles (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Short piece that counters skeptical theories.","quote":"seen this shortish 60 Minutes piece yet. You should.","text":"It's clear from your comment that you haven't seen this shortish 60 Minutes piece yet. You should. It would fairly quickly dissuade you of this theory. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27726625,"author":"creamytaco","month":"2021-07","story":"America’s government comes clean on UFOs","stance":"rec","why":"Eyewitness testimony from Navy pilots.","quote":"I suggest you familiarize yourself with some of that eyewitness testimony","text":"I suggest you familiarize yourself with some of that eyewitness testimony because everything you wrote simply does not apply: [VIDEO] If you have more to say that you'd also want me to address, it better be directly relevant to the events described in this video."},{"id":34676312,"author":"nprateem","month":"2023-02","story":"As a US Navy fighter pilot, I witnessed unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP)","stance":"reference","why":"Head of Pentagon UAP task force lists capabilities.","quote":"He lists some pretty amazing capabilities around 2:10.","text":"This is the guy who headed up the AATIP (UAP) task force for the Pentagon. [VIDEO] He lists some pretty amazing capabilities around 2:10."},{"id":30600579,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2022-03","story":"Flight characteristics of anomalous unidentified aerial vehicles (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows multiple radars tracking the same objects.","quote":"multiple different radar signals tracking the same objects","text":"The information from multiple different radar signals tracking the same objects would seem to rule such things out: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30600619,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2022-03","story":"Flight characteristics of anomalous unidentified aerial vehicles (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as extraordinary evidence for UAPs.","quote":"Is this sufficiently extraordinary?","text":"Is this sufficiently extraordinary? [VIDEO] Also, it's already conceptually possible to get around the spacetime problem: [LINK] (This doesn't imply such a thing would be easy to build; but at least it's theoretically possible, maybe)"},{"id":30704426,"author":"cronix","month":"2022-03","story":"Famous Navy UFO video was camera glare, evidence suggests","stance":"reference","why":"Report on multi-sensor confirmation of the Navy encounter.","quote":"a 60 Minutes piece discussing it","text":"Here's a 60 Minutes piece discussing it: [VIDEO] Here's a 4 hour interview with the pilot, David Fravor, on Lex Fridman's show where he goes into much more detail than you'll probably read elsewhere: [LINK] This wasn't just a single radar. It was an AEGIS system compromised of multiple ships with state of the art radars, multiple plane radars, and visual confirmation by several pilots. They were tracking it for multiple days."},{"id":27592853,"author":"ostenning","month":"2021-06","story":"Do we believe in UFOs? that's the wrong question","stance":"reference","why":"Trained military observers describe regular encounters.","quote":"","text":"How is the idea of UFOs even a \"conspiracy theory\" anymore? UFOs or UAPs are now a verified phenomenon that seem to be observed fairly regularly by trained military, \"every day for a couple of years\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":27625460,"author":"CodeArtisan","month":"2021-06","story":"U.S. government prepares to issue landmark report on UFOs","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot describes how long the object was watched.","quote":"the relevant part","text":"i can't tell precisely but according to one of the pilots on CBS 60 minutes; there were four peoples \"watching it for five minutes\". the relevant part: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30602293,"author":"nobody9999","month":"2022-03","story":"Flight characteristics of anomalous unidentified aerial vehicles (2019)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":">Is this sufficiently extraordinary? [VIDEO] I have no idea. I'm not going to watch some random video. Where is the associated published, peer-reviewed paper? In the absence of hard data to support whatever the above purports to show, it's just as believable as this[0]. >Also, it's already conceptually possible to get around the spacetime problem: [LINK] (This doesn't imply such a thing would be easy to build; but at least it's theoretically possible, maybe) Is it? AIUI, the Alcubierre \"drive\" (it's not a propulsion system per se, but rather a mechanism for manipulating space-time, but that's something of a quibble) requires \"negative mass/energy.\" From the Wikipedia article you linked: ...a spacecraft could achieve apparent faster- than-light travel if a configurable energy- density field lower than that of vacuum (that is, negative mass) could be created. While the math of General Relativity allows for the creation of such a \"warp\" field, there isn't any (known) mechanism (or the means for generating the energy concentration required) for creating them. Creating an Alcubierre drive isn't just a matter of engineering . There's science that needs to be done to confirm that it's possible to create such a mechanism. The fields required for an Alcubierre drive is (at least for the moment) just as speculative as \"white holes\" or Einstein-Rosen bridges, which can also be described in the context of General Relativity. Given the ubiquity of the materials for life (Water, complex or"}],"r":[["yTkzNHF6rMs",1],["ygB4EZ7ggig",1]]},"ZC5Zr3NC2PY":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as an affordable, good-quality way to digitize analog video.","q":{"x":"does some good coverage of capturing/converting analogue video - hardware choices make huge difference.","i":24841852,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30908802,"author":"ocdtrekkie","month":"2022-04","story":"A blue connector does not USB 3.0 make","stance":"rec","why":"Trick using composite-to-HDMI upscaler and cheap capture dongle.","quote":"a composite-to-HDMI upscaler and a cheap HDMI capture dongle","text":"[VIDEO] introduces the \"use a composite-to-HDMI upscaler and a cheap HDMI capture dongle\" trick. It looks like he might've linked a different capture dongle, though the one I bought was pretty near identical to the one this article is about."},{"id":24841852,"author":"interestica","month":"2020-10","story":"My Eight-Year Quest to Digitize 45 Videotapes","stance":"rec","why":"Covers capturing analog video and hardware choices.","quote":"does some good coverage of capturing/converting analogue video - hardware choices make huge difference.","text":"For those in the Toronto-Hamilton-Montreal corridor, I've been running everpixels.ca for a bit as a side thing (though it's kinda on pause). It's primarily focused on photo digitization for families - but I've also done a ton of tape media. I can help any local HNers that need access to hardware. (Or just some info/direction) Technology Connections (YouTube) does some good coverage of capturing/converting analogue video - hardware choices make huge difference. [VIDEO] There's clearly a need/desire for this kind of stuff (though it's sad how many times I'm approached as someone is near death). Don't wait til that moment to save the stories and memories that you have. I've been back and forth about how much I want to dive in with this project. If anyone is local and really interested in some of it, drop me a line. There are some cool avenues I've touched - seniors, alzheimers, story capture."},{"id":20790823,"author":"Maxious","month":"2019-08","story":"Capturing PAL video with an SDR, and a few dead ends","stance":"rec","why":"Affordable, good quality way to capture analog video.","quote":"A reasonably priced and good quality bodge is described in","text":"A reasonably priced and good quality bodge is described in \"The Best Easy Way to Capture Analog Video (it's a little weird)\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":23483822,"author":"ladberg","month":"2020-06","story":"If it ain't broke: Share your oldest working gadgets","stance":"reference","why":"Shows TVs often have good built-in analog-to-digital converters.","quote":"They often have good analog to digital converters built in as shown by Technology Connections","text":"The TV by itself might do it just fine! They often have good analog to digital converters built in as shown by Technology Connections: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27919280,"author":"tablespoon","month":"2021-07","story":"YouTube says it pulled Bolsonaro videos for Covid-19 misinformation","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The people on a forum with really deep knowledge on the subject have poor things to say about it","text":"> Youtube was, is and will forever be for cat videos, not for serious information - or so at least the moderation policy makes it out to be. The problem isn't the moderation policy, it's the medium. It encourages easy passive absorption of anything presented in a slick matter by people who barely know what they're talking about. Take for instance this slick video talking about VHS capture by Technology Connections, which I used to have vaguely positive feelings about: [VIDEO] The people on a forum with really deep knowledge on the subject have poor things to say about it: [LINK] . They say he only has a poor gasp of the subject, and half the time sounds like he's just reading off of Wikipedia. > There are plenty of alternative video distribution channels out there which don't have this problem, the only thing which needs to be solved is video discovery. No, those \"alternative video distribution channels\" are usually far worse, precisely because of that lack of moderation. Except in a very few cases (specifically some gun videos, and Serikzhan Bilash), most of the original content that has to flee to them is garbage."}],"r":[["Erp8IAUouus",1],["6Cf7IL_eZ38",1]]},"o2HBHckrdQc":{"wy":"Recommended as the thorough case for replacing TCP in datacenters and for Ousterhout's Homa protocol.","q":{"x":"The Netdev talk I've linked below is really good","i":35234054,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35234054,"author":"eklitzke","month":"2023-03","story":"RPC vs. TCP (Redux)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thorough presentation of microsecond-scale RPC goals","quote":"The Netdev talk I've linked below is really good","text":"You can obviously build an RPC protocol on top of TCP, people have been doing it for decades. However there are things that are difficult to efficiently implement on top of TCP (multiplexing different logical streams over the same connection, message priorities) in the TCP model, and some things that are impossible to implement over TCP (for example, you don't really have any ability to influence what happens during congestion control scenarios). There are also a bunch of things about TCP that are fundamentally inefficient on modern hardware. Do these things matter if you're running some RPC service over the internet with a Python backend? Almost certainly not. But if you read John Ousterhout's paper or watch his Netdev talk^[1] it's also pretty clear that this isn't the use case he's been talking about. He's talking about RPC in the datacenter, where in theory modern hardware is capable of delivering RPC messages with microsecond scale latency but in practice no RPC systems built on TCP come anywhere close to that. The Netdev talk I've linked below is really good and John makes a really thorough presentation of his ideas and what he's actually trying to optimize for. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34890802,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2023-02","story":"It's TCP vs. RPC All over Again","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ousterhout explains Homa and replacing TCP","quote":"Ousterhout is right, and the data shows he is right.","text":"If you'd like to watch Ousterhoust explain Homa and his vision of replacing TCP: [VIDEO] Spoiler: Ousterhout is right, and the data shows he is right. Maybe not everyone knows it yet, or it's the kind of \"right\" that not everyone will ever get to (and we'll settle for \"good enough\" for much longer), but he is almost certainly right. Not to disparage the poster of this article of course -- I always love a good hot take or contrarian piece."},{"id":34890741,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2023-02","story":"Is It Time to Replace TCP in Data Centers?","stance":"rec","why":"Ousterhout's keynote on replacing TCP in datacenters","quote":"Netdev is an amazing conference btw","text":"If you want to watch Outsterhout in talk form: Netdev 0x16 - Keynote: It's time to replace TCP in the datacenter: [VIDEO] Netdev is an amazing conference btw, it is insane that we get access to the trailblazing being shown off at conferences for free on places like YT."},{"id":37945855,"author":"HippoBaro","month":"2023-10","story":"Is POSIX really outdated?","stance":"reference","why":"Ousterhout working on new protocol to fix TCP problems","quote":"John Ousterhout ( ) is currently working on a new network protocol","text":"I think there are a few misunderstandings there (the video is funny, btw). The kind of async IO I'm referring to exists one layer below whatever Apache and node are using. Both are event-driven, actually. They both use some flavor of epoll (see [LINK] ). Historically, buffered IO was sufficient to circumvent slow threads. Simply because buffered IO operations usually don't block (you merely memcpy, and the kernel flushes asynchronously in the background). That can only take you so far, however, and it's apparent today that hardware can go much further and that the gap is widening. It's a valid point, though, to question whether that additional performance is even needed. John Ousterhout ( [VIDEO] ) is currently working on a new network protocol to alleviate some of the problems TCP creates in software wrt performance, and he also questions whether there is even a need for very fast networking for real-world applications. IMO, the mere existence of stuff like DPDK is proof enough. Many folks use it and would rather use the kernel if it could provide comparable performance."}],"r":[["B5NULPSiOGw",1],["cYsVvV1aVss",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["iukBMY4apvI",1],["sK5i-N34im8",1],["HPuvDm8IC-4",1]]},"sbQhgEJuExY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a showcase of what the demoscene can do, with high-res antialiased vector graphics and motion blur.","q":{"x":"features animated, recursive SVGs with antialiasing and motion blur at high rez and high frame rate.","i":1362145,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":8882800,"author":"hansjorg","month":"2015-01","story":"Conspire: A Programming Environment for NOWHERE","stance":"rec","why":"Awesome demo with music, 2d engine and graphics by paniq.","quote":"did the music, 2d engine and graphics for the awesome Masagin","text":"Leonard Ritter is a very talented guy. Looking forward to NOWHERE. He's known as paniq in the demoscene and did the music, 2d engine and graphics for the awesome Masagin: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8065902,"author":"theandrewbailey","month":"2014-07","story":"Interview with Demoscener “kb” of Farbrausch","stance":"rec","why":"Farbrausch demo described as color intoxication.","quote":"It's about as close as you can get to color intoxication","text":"The one for NVScene 2008 kicked ass. It's about as close as you can get to color intoxication (which is the rough translation of \"farbrausch\"). [VIDEO]"},{"id":1362145,"author":"corysama","month":"2010-05","story":"The Demoscene -- an Overview","stance":"rec","why":"Demo with animated recursive SVGs, antialiasing and motion blur.","quote":"features animated, recursive SVGs with antialiasing and motion blur at high rez and high frame rate.","text":"Farbrausch's \"debris.\" shows just how much you can pack into 200k (including the audio, btw). [LINK] For the webby crowd: \"Masagin\" features animated, recursive SVGs with antialiasing and motion blur at high rez and high frame rate. [VIDEO] Or, you could download both demos from [LINK] and run them on your own PC."},{"id":36968837,"author":"pcardoso","month":"2023-08","story":"Epicycle Clock by Sophie Houlden","stance":"reference","why":"Nested dials synced to music resemble a clock project.","quote":"Reminds me of one part of the Masagin demo [1], with lots of nested dials synched to the music.","text":"Nice. Reminds me of one part of the Masagin demo [1], with lots of nested dials synched to the music. 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["bY7aRJE-oOY",1],["Hvx4xXhZMrU",1],["wqu_IpkOYBg",1],["Tfh0ytz8S0k",1],["db-7J5OaSag",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1]]},"wcBEOcPtlYk":{"wy":"Recommended as a systematic, surprising experiment, often cited in audio and gear-obsession threads.","q":{"x":"check out this guy who ran a much more systematic set of experiments","i":47565224,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47379220,"author":"mrob","month":"2026-03","story":"Wired headphone sales are exploding. What's with the Bluetooth backlash?","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates close amp emulation with simple EQ and distortion.","quote":"Jim Lill's video on guitar amp tone is an interesting demonstration.","text":"I'm convinced that at least 90% of \"analog sound\" can be simulated by taking the ideal block diagram and replacing every link with a parametric EQ->waveshaper->parametric EQ chain. Configuring those added components correctly is left as an exercise for the reader. Jim Lill's video on guitar amp tone is an interesting demonstration. Hear how close he gets to the original with an even simpler combination of EQ and distortion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47565224,"author":"gadtfly","month":"2026-03","story":"Comparison shows audiophiles waste a lot of money","stance":"rec","why":"Systematic experiments on where guitar and amp tone come from.","quote":"check out this guy who ran a much more systematic set of experiments","text":"If this is appealing to you, check out this guy who ran a much more systematic set of experiments on even more sacred and sensitive targets: Tested: Where Does The Tone Come From In An Electric Guitar? [LINK] Tested: Where Does The Tone Come From In A Guitar Amplifier? [VIDEO]"},{"id":45908509,"author":"WorkerBee28474","month":"2025-11","story":"How Tube Amplifiers Work","stance":"rec","why":"Investigates where guitar amplifier tone comes from.","quote":"I'd like to plug the YouTube Video by Jim Lill","text":"I'd like to plug the YouTube Video by Jim Lill - \"Tested: Where Does The Tone Come From In A Guitar Amplifier?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":44052841,"author":"mrob","month":"2025-05","story":"A Secret Trove of Rare Guitars Heads to the Met","stance":"rec","why":"Tests amp tone, showing it is mostly EQ and distortion.","quote":"It's almost entirely EQ and distortion, applied in various orders","text":"Jim Lill has a video testing amp tone: [VIDEO] It's almost entirely EQ and distortion, applied in various orders (the exact signal chain matters, because EQ after distortion sounds different than distortion after EQ). By getting this right you can simulate any tube amp tone with solid state to a high degree of accuracy."},{"id":42793894,"author":"leblancfg","month":"2025-01","story":"The Ribbon Microphone","stance":"reference","why":"DIY amp section matches EQ but dynamics sound thin.","quote":"Its EQ indeed sounds like the big names, but I'm sure you'll also notice how its dynamics also sound so thin.","text":"Love his series, but you really have to keep in mind that his testing methodology is \"reductionist\": a microphone's quality can't just be distilled to its EQ response. Phase, transient response, clipping, etc. are also part of the equation. edit: My \"case in point\" moment is Jim's (excellent!) DIY amp section in this video: [VIDEO] . Its EQ indeed sounds like the big names, but I'm sure you'll also notice how its dynamics also sound so thin. That's likely because solid state clipping != (saggy) tube overdrive."},{"id":41183533,"author":"woolion","month":"2024-08","story":"Gear Acquisition Syndrome","stance":"reference","why":"Shows EQ and distortion reproduce vintage amp tone.","quote":"with an eq, a compressor and a distortion you can reproduce any vintage amp sound","text":"I just discovered GAS therapy [0], as there was some controversy about the OP-1 pricing (also from Teenage Engineering). To summarize both sides, software is pretty cheap and limitless. Device are limited which can spark creativity by constraints, their tactile form factor feel great, and their portable nature can be a great advantage. However, there are other arguments that typically revolve around how \"software cannot replicate vintage sounds\" where the holy wars really begin. Nobody has ever proven that, but the myth endures. Recently enough [1] showed all that basically with an eq, a compressor and a distortion you can reproduce any vintage amp sound, you just need to know how it deviates from a flat sound response. How expensive the vintage or high-end hardware is might be a deterrent for some but a very attractive proposition for others. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["n02tImce3AE",1],["VYhAGnsnO7w",1],["MtxA20Q-Uss",1],["KEoGrbKAyKE",1]]},"B8ofWFx525s":{"wy":"Cited as an early, prescient warning about algorithmic personalization, echo chambers and social media feeds.","q":{"x":"The Filter Bubble Ted talk is what motivated me to dump social media.","i":12924190,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12924190,"author":"throwawy64","month":"2016-11","story":"Facebook admits it must do more to stop the spread of misinformation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Motivated the commenter to quit social media entirely.","quote":"The Filter Bubble Ted talk is what motivated me to dump social media.","text":"The Filter Bubble Ted talk is what motivated me to dump social media. [VIDEO] Gone are my Facebook, G+, Reddit accounts. I have a Twitter which I curate manually with numerous feeds from all over. I generally look at content like links first then read the Twitter discussion if any. I treat posting as a personal catharsis anymore, and use throwaway accounts. IMO my online opinions are going to get lost in the crush so why bother curating an identity. One that just creates a signal trail for advertising and snoopers? I talk to the people in my life more instead. These have been conscious decisions on my part and honestly I think I'm mentally happier and less frustrated \"at the world\" for it. MMS is good enough for sharing pics with grandma and grandpa. Though I've asked them to keep most off social media. It's too creepy having the world ogle my kids. They don't like it but abide. IMO despite being my mom she's old and has less life left than my kids. Nothing to worry about by posting her life on Facebook My kids on the other hand have their whole lives ahead of them. I would rather it not be broadcast without their consent. If they get older and decide to go all Jayden Smith or Paris Hilton on social media so be it. But these services are not what humanity needs to progress. They are today's fascination. And I am not into it. So fuck em"},{"id":3760755,"author":"AkThhhpppt","month":"2012-03","story":"Google told to halt auto-complete","stance":"rec","why":"Actual examples of personalization filtering news and search results.","quote":"I really suggest having a look at this","text":"I did put \"vaguely related\" in the rant warning, didn't I? :-) Though news.google.com is what I was thinking of with the current affairs example, it generalizes to the whole Google edifice, and to Facebook too. I really suggest having a look at this: [VIDEO] to see what I'm talking about, with actual examples. Minor quibble: autocomplete isn't to aid in searching common topics, it's to help Google avoid failed searches through misspelt queries. \",)"},{"id":43242350,"author":"poopoopadoo","month":"2025-03","story":"What Game Is Jeff Bezos Playing?","stance":"reference","why":"Prescient warning about filtering online.","quote":"If only someone had seen this coming","text":"If only someone had seen this coming: [VIDEO] It’s because humans latch onto language as if their map is reality Even here there’s a pretentious filtering to conform to the memory of the group in charge; this syntactic structure is of course the proper and correct format! It’s our biology choosing self which socially is to conserve our own perception Online everyone is being normalized the conserve the perception of mods/platform owners (aka just more normal vain humans, no need to deify them) rather than out in the world normalizing to a more unique creole of sorts The abstracts of STEM are a terrible philosophy for building social life. Created a bored and repetitive culture that depends on taking a knee before some webmaster rando for comments to avoid shadow bans"},{"id":40415590,"author":"hn_throwaway_99","month":"2024-05","story":"AI Personalization Fuels Groupthink and Uniformity","stance":"reference","why":"2011 talk on echo chamber effects of personalization.","quote":"nearly all of the negative impacts discussed in that talk came to pass and then some","text":"This really is completely independent of \"AI\". I remember seeing a famous presentation (maybe it was a TED talk?) about the \"echo chamber\" effect of personalization from the mid 00s - and IMO nearly all of the negative impacts discussed in that talk came to pass and then some. AI just makes it worse. Edit: I think it was this I was referring to about \"filter bubbles\", [VIDEO] . I was a tad mistaken, this was from 2011."},{"id":36577314,"author":"darkclouds","month":"2023-07","story":"Are You a Late Bloomer in Work or Love? Maybe You’re Right on Time","stance":"reference","why":"Early talk highlighting algorithmic filtering by major tech companies.","quote":"What Eli Pariser highlighted in his Ted talk 12 years ago","text":"Clap for Carers during Covid was a national psychological experiment to measure conformity. Brexit was the closest you could get to resurrecting the Nazi party in the UK 60 years later as another conformity experiment. How do you measure conformity across a country? TV Set top boxes being paused at 8pm on Thursday, wifi connected devices seeing a weakening signal, social media posts. The internet is a security services and big businesses surveillance tool wet dream. Whats been interesting since is how various websites and their algorithms are being used to challenge your view point. What Eli Pariser highlighted in his Ted talk 12 years ago ( [VIDEO] ) has grown to all the major US tech companies because if everyone has their nose in a device connected to some website, the state can control people in deniable spooky hacker ways. Remember intelligence is a cheaper and easier form of nipping problems in the bud before you have to puts boots on the ground, beit military boots or police boots, and at least here in the UK every element of the state is involved in surveillance, including the NHS and the educational system."},{"id":25516906,"author":"bewareFB00","month":"2020-12","story":"‘YouTube recommendations are toxic,’ says dev who worked on the algorithm (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Warning about filter bubbles in recommendation systems.","quote":"Beware the filter bubbles","text":"Beware the filter bubbles: [VIDEO] Using scientific unification as a cognitive guide to understand this holistically: [LINK] Developers! Startups! Cap tables! Unicorns! Disrupt YC and tune it out."},{"id":10823868,"author":"gengkev","month":"2016-01","story":"Iran's blogfather: Facebook, Instagram and Twitter are killing the web","stance":"reference","why":"Source for ideas about platform power over feed content.","quote":"Some of these ideas I found in a TED talk","text":"I think part of the problem is that one benefit of the web is to be exposed to new ideas, and passive users are hardly benefiting from seeing stuff that doesn't challenge them. Centralisation of power is not just a problem in that Facebook tailors content to your preferences, but also that it has the power to choose how it does so. For example, I believe that Facebook said that it might choose to ignore that a user clicked \"Show me less of this\", because what we say we want isn't always what we actually want. Another example is that when even people who have liked an organisation's page don't see its content, Facebook basically forces them to pay for advertising. Some of these ideas I found in a TED talk: [VIDEO]"}]},"WRdJCFEqFTU":{"wy":"Recommended as one of the best bridges between mind and computation, and as a sample of Bach's thinking on AI and philosophy.","q":{"x":"Recommend watching this video on Computational Meta-Psychology","i":24348527,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24348527,"author":"miketery","month":"2020-09","story":"Mind Emulation Foundation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best communication bridging mind and computation.","quote":"Recommend watching this video on Computational Meta-Psychology","text":"The best I've seen the topic of mind and computation bridged and well communicated has been by Joscha Bach. Recommend watching this video on Computational Meta-Psychology [1]. Also his 3 hour conversation with Lex Fridman is a whole other amazing rabbit hole [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23925091,"author":"maze-le","month":"2020-07","story":"Joscha Bach: Artificial Consciousness and the Nature of Reality [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Another Joscha Bach talk from CCC.","quote":"He is definitely one of the most interesting people to listen to when it comes to AI, Intelligence and Philosophy.","text":"He is definitely one of the most interesting people to listen to when it comes to AI, Intelligence and Philosophy. I've seen him in Leipzig on CCC events in the past, here are some of his other talks: * Computational Meta-Intelligence (32C3): [VIDEO] * Machine Dreams (33C3): [LINK] * The Ghost in the Machine (35C3): [LINK] He also did a very interesting podcast episode with Fefe and Frank from alternativlos! (in German): [LINK]"},{"id":16219459,"author":"freeflight","month":"2018-01","story":"Can staying awake beat depression?","stance":"reference","why":"Joscha Bach talk giving hope AI helps explain the mind.","quote":"stuff like talks by Joscha Bach on C3","text":"> In general I'd say the best tactic is to learn as much as you can yourself (while remembering to be honest with yourself) and pursue general purpose coping strategies across the board (for example, mindfulness based cognitive therapy). Yup, that's exactly what I've been doing for years now. I've read through quite a bit of stuff ranging from Freud, Jung, B.F. Skinner to Edward de Bono and countless others in addition to lots of Wikipedia surfing and study reading. A rather common theme among a lot of them: Many of them went into studying psychology as an attempt to diagnose their own issues and often it feels more like reading philosophy rather than actual medical science. I wonder if we will ever be able to articulate something like a \"Grand Unified Theory of the mind\"? Considering how much of this is subjective to the individual and atop of that the individual's brain physiology, it seems like an impossible task. But then I see stuff like talks by Joscha Bach on C3 [0] [1] [2] and it's quite inspirational as it gives me hope that creating strong AI might actually help us understand what's going on under our own hoods a little bit better. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["F9cO3-MLHOM",1],["e3K5UxWRRuY",1],["r6sGWTCMz2k",1],["0SARbwvhupQ",1]]},"s10etP1p2bU":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a simple method that makes omelettes approachable, while noting that the technique is harder than it looks.","q":{"x":"Watch this, incredibly simple method that made me actually like omelettes.","i":21582235,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21582235,"author":"RobertRoberts","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: What's the most valuable thing you can learn in an hour?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simple omelette method","quote":"Watch this, incredibly simple method that made me actually like omelettes.","text":"Watch this, incredibly simple method that made me actually like omelettes. (I hated them growing up) [VIDEO] \"Really good\" is subjective. It's the very notion something is possible and that you can do it that I see as valuable. Practice makes perfect, nothing can be perfect the first time you learn it."},{"id":13054830,"author":"wyclif","month":"2016-11","story":"Jacques Pepin, unlike other celebrity chefs, will actually teach you how to cook","stance":"rec","why":"Country versus classic French omelette technique","quote":"His omelette video is also great.","text":"His omelette video is also great. Shows the difference between a \"country omelette\" and a classic French omelette. I like that he denies one is superior to the other. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31415963,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2022-05","story":"Picasso's self portrait evolution from age 15 to age 90","stance":"reference","why":"Uncut omelette demo showing apparent simplicity hides skill","quote":"if you actually go ahead and try to make this it turns out to be INCREDIBLY difficult to get it right.","text":"Here's a more accessible analogy: Here is Jacques Pepin making an omelet in 2 ways: [VIDEO] You can see every motion, every measure, the size of the flame, there are no cuts. But if you actually go ahead and try to make this it turns out to be INCREDIBLY difficult to get it right. Not every random mish mash or scribble is as random as a layperson will at first think. The other analogy is look at the original post. Picasso COULD have painted like he did at 15 or 18 or 20 - which I assume you would agree you couldn't scribble yourself. But he CHOSE to paint in the style you are criticizing. You should default to assuming that there is more skill to it than you can see."}],"r":[["X1XoCQm5JSQ",1],["tIuj-oMN-Fk",1]]},"0iJZ_QGMLD0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an easy-to-digest explanation of time dilation and the twin paradox.","q":{"x":"Minute Physics did a good couple of videos on this","i":17625217,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":24176733,"author":"p1mrx","month":"2020-08","story":"Fastest star ever seen is moving at 8% the speed of light","stance":"rec","why":"Gives an idea of what happens at relativistic speeds","quote":"These minutephysics videos provide some idea of what's happening","text":"These minutephysics videos provide some idea of what's happening: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] (Although the time dilation in Interstellar is due to gravity, not high-speed travel.)"},{"id":17625217,"author":"Sean1708","month":"2018-07","story":"First Successful Test of General Relativity Near Supermassive Black Hole","stance":"rec","why":"Easy-to-digest explanation of time dilation","quote":"Minute Physics did a good couple of videos on this","text":"Minute Physics did a good couple of videos on this[0,1], pretty much the same info as is in the Wiki but might be easier to digest for some people. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28504112,"author":"godelski","month":"2021-09","story":"Why can’t I go faster than the speed of light?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for resolution of the twin paradox","quote":"","text":"I'm pretty sure this doesn't work out because you have to slow down. So thinking about it, if you are traveling at that speed because of your inertial reference frame it is equivalent that everyone else around you is moving at (or near) the speed of light and they are moving slowly through time. This is the classic twin paradox and there is a resolution to it, which is that you can't instantaneously turn around.[0] Or in our case, we have to turn around to slow down. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27020997,"author":"MichaelApproved","month":"2021-05","story":"Stars that race through space at nearly the speed of light","stance":"reference","why":"Shows common acceleration explanation, then contrasts with a correction","quote":"Here’s a minute physics video that shows how acceleration explains the twins paradox","text":"When you accelerate away from me in an elevator, only one of us experiences additional forces during that acceleration. Only your body feels heavier as the elevator speeds up. Here’s a minute physics video that shows how acceleration explains the twins paradox (the “common” explanation) [VIDEO] After you watch minute physics video, you might feel like you understand. Nope, can’t have any of that. Now, you must watch this Fermi lab video that says the common explanation is “not fundamentally correct” [LINK]"}]},"6b4ZZQkcNEo":{"wy":"Brought up as a favorite and as an inspiration for small, cheap jumping robots for obstacle crossing and planetary exploration.","q":{"x":"My favorite is the Sand Flea, a small car which can jump 9 meters (30-feet) into the air","i":11319878,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11319878,"author":"icebraining","month":"2016-03","story":"Why Google Wants to Sell Its Robots: Reality Is Hard","stance":"rec","why":"Small car robot jumping 9 meters into the air.","quote":"My favorite is the Sand Flea, a small car which can jump 9 meters (30-feet) into the air","text":"Have you seen the robots they developed? Reducing it to \"moving from point A to B\" is misleading, in my opinion, even if technically true. They essentially developed machines that could move in difficult terrains, often where humans themselves couldn't. My favorite is the Sand Flea, a small car which can jump 9 meters (30-feet) into the air: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6904822,"author":"iamshs","month":"2013-12","story":"Google Acquires Boston Dynamics","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite among Boston Dynamics' wild repertoire of robots.","quote":"Forget Big Dog, I like this robot a lot","text":"Aha, Boston Dynamics has such wild repertoire of robots. Well done. Forget Big Dog, I like this robot a lot: [VIDEO] Maps, Self-Driving car, robots and a high quality analytics team. On top of that, robotic team comprises of various reputed groups with talented well regarded individuals. I look forward to the future. Also, how much disparity will arise in quality of living between North America and various Asian countries, or even Europe."},{"id":42282987,"author":"Teever","month":"2024-11","story":"NASA's Europa Clipper: Miles Down, Instruments Deploying","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in discussion of small probe constellations and surface robots.","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure about that specifically, I've only taken an intro to orbital mechanics class so esoteric orbital maneuvers are a bit beyond me but I understand that in some cases it is possible. I would imagine that one technique that we have yet to try is putting fuel depots in long distance places to facilitate the execution of these manuevers. With that said what I had in mind when I wrote my comment wasn't using one rocket to launch a 100 probes to different solar bodies but instead to launch 100 probes to a single body in one go. I envision a time where we'll deploy massive constellations of remote sensing satellites, like mini-star link constellations for each solar body be it a moon or planet so that we can get real-time data across the whole body instead of having to stitch together pieces of data from one probe. Like Starlink these constellations will be able to relay data between the nodes should they lose line of sight with Earth and will be able to relay data to other constellations orbiting other nearby solar bodies to get the data to Earth if the whole constellation is blocked.[0] I could see a near future where we start sending these Starlink like communications/remote sensing craft at the same time we send craft designed to pepper the surface of a body with multiple rovers and stationary probes to survey the surface of planets and again relay the data they collect between themselves and the constellations above. It would be cool to see terrestrial bodies explored"},{"id":39057367,"author":"Teever","month":"2024-01","story":"Japan's first-ever soft lunar landing with SLIM spacecraft [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Flea robot inspires small disposable rover idea for Mars.","quote":"Imagine exploring mars with a combination of a dozen or so flea type robots","text":"I had similar thoughts when I saw the flea robot from Boston Dynamics.[0] Imagine exploring mars with a combination of a dozen or so flea type robots that have solar panels on them and a robot arm for sample retrieval that can fold up into the body. They can trundle around the surface taking video and picking up samples as desired and then return to the stationary lander that they came from when needed. The stationary lander can have all of the sample analysis machinery, seismograph, weather station, as well as a high bandwidth uplink to satellites in orbit / Earth. Keep landing more and more of these within the the same area so that the fleas can travel between the stationary landers as they travel further and further, heck you can even form a mesh network between all of the machines on the ground, and offload a lot of heavy computational processing to the stationary landers that could be powered by an RTG. You can risk individual fleas doing dangerous things that you otherwise wouldn't want to risk one large all in one lander on, as losing a flea only degrades a small fraction of your research and if fleas get stuck somewhere another flea can attempt to rescue it. You could even have the fleas return home to overwinter if they're not designed to withstand the winter. There's so much versatility that you gain in a model like this with small disposable rover robots paired with massive stationary landers. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37841072,"author":"93po","month":"2023-10","story":"Extreme Parkour with Legged Robots","stance":"reference","why":"Small 11-pound robot that jumps 30 feet.","quote":"less than a foot long, 11 pounds, and can jump 30 feet","text":"Here's a Boston Dynamics one that is less than a foot long, 11 pounds, and can jump 30 feet (easily from ground to roof of one story building) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24842121,"author":"capekwasright","month":"2020-10","story":"Throwable Robot Car Always Lands on Four Wheels","stance":"reference","why":"Shows robot snapping flat during ascent to land on wheels.","quote":"You can see it in action @ 0:17 in their sole released video","text":"Boston Dynamics tackled this problem with the SandFlea robot ca. 2010, albeit in a single axis while exploiting the intermediate axis theorem. You can see it in action @ 0:17 in their sole released video[1], where the robot snaps flat during the ascent phase. This was done primarily to increase the likelihood that the vehicle would land on all four wheels, in order to maximize energy dissipation by the wheels on landing and thus minimize shock seen by the body. As a secondary benefit, footage from the onboard camera stood a chance to be usable during a hop, instead of being a blurry mess. Lots of great details in their patent filing for the robot[2]. Source: I helped design this robot [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":17054013,"author":"sktrdie","month":"2018-05","story":"Nasa will send helicopter to Mars to test otherworldly flight","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests fleets of small jumping robots for planetary exploration.","quote":"why not send a fleet of these little small jumping-robots instead","text":"So if it falls on its side, something that has a probably a 50% chance of happening, can it get back up? I always thought that for exploring another planet, you shouldn't really use something with wheels or wings. Something different like small \"jumping robots\" would make a lot more sense in a place where you have literally the ability to do 0-hardware maintenance. Instead of a single expensive flying robot, why not send a fleet of these little small jumping-robots instead, to more quickly and cheaply explore the area: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17067202,"author":"throwaway84742","month":"2018-05","story":"Boston Dynamics will start selling its dog-like SpotMini robot in 2019","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests larger Sand Flea as cheap, deployable, obstacle-jumping robot.","quote":"A slightly larger version of Sand Flea seems like a better fit.","text":"A slightly larger version of Sand Flea seems like a better fit. Disposable, can be deployed relatively cheaply in large quantities, with good camo nearly invisible on the ground, can scale obstacles by jumping. Could be made to carry a fragmentation payload and/or spy gear. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13122880,"author":"awqrre","month":"2016-12","story":"Salto – UC Berkeley’s Agile Jumping Robot","stance":"reference","why":"Robot jumping onto a building in one jump.","quote":"a robot that can jump on top of a building in 1 jump sounds more useful to me","text":"Yes, a robot that can jump on top of a building in 1 jump sounds more useful to me, something like this one: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":7612507,"author":"User8712","month":"2014-04","story":"MorpHex, a hexapod robot","stance":"reference","why":"Example of practical robots.","quote":"When I think practical, I think Boston Dynamics.","text":"How is it practical? It's a beautiful design, but far simpler designs can run circles around it in every scenario I can imagine. A small version might make for a fun desk toy. When I think practical, I think Boston Dynamics. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"8PmM6SUn7Es":{"wy":"Cited as a sourced overview of what organic means and how it compares on pesticides and nutrition.","q":{"x":"Kurzgesagt has a pretty great video[0] about organic food, and goes a little bit into what the definition of \"organic\" means.","i":21459498,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":28777269,"author":"dnate","month":"2021-10","story":"Traces of 122 different pesticides in 12 most polluted fruit and veg products","stance":"rec","why":"Organic food doesn't have significantly fewer pesticides.","quote":"Here is a good video about it","text":"Organic food actually doesn't have significantly less pesticides in them. Here is a good video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21459498,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2019-11","story":"Glyphosate in Organic Hydroponic Food Production","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what organic means and pesticide use, with cited sources.","quote":"Kurzgesagt has a pretty great video[0] about organic food, and goes a little bit into what the definition of \"organic\" means.","text":"Kurzgesagt has a pretty great video[0] about organic food, and goes a little bit into what the definition of \"organic\" means. Digging through his citations[1] (which is awesome, btw), as far as American legal definitions and pesticides and the whatnot are concerned, the relevant law[2] says that pest and disease problems can be controlled using \"non-synthetic controls, such as...repellents\", meaning that pesticides can be used, as long as they're also \"organic\" (which, he notes later in the video, there are slightly less, but still non-zero traces of, and not necessarily safer[3]) That being said, there really shouldn't be a worry about traces of \"chemicals\" on organic OR non-organic produce, as the FDA has very strict guide lines and testing procedures that produce sellers go through[4] before sending out produce that almost everyone is well under. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":46361819,"author":"neuralkoi","month":"2025-12","story":"Chemical Hygiene","stance":"reference","why":"Organic may not be what you think.","quote":"organic might not exactly [0] be what you think it is [1].","text":"I can do all of this and be hit by a bus tomorrow. I'm not saying don't do it, but there's already so many assumptions made. It's also playing whack-a-mole. For example, organic might not exactly [0] be what you think it is [1]. What's more important is that you actually do eat enough fruits and vegetables. Reverse osmosis membranes are made of plastic [2] and might themselves be introducing tons of microplastics to the water [3]. Our parents got leaded gasoline. We get microplastics. The bandwidth to focus on these kind details usually comes with certain financial security. There's simply too many variables. Your average joe who can't use \"Locale\" to source their food. (An interesting experiment is to grab the text body of this blog post and ask AI to estimate the author's net worth and yearly salary.) “A ship in harbour is safe, but that is not what ships are built for.” -- John A Shedd [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":27033082,"author":"hypertele-Xii","month":"2021-05","story":"Green gold: Avocado farming on the rise in Africa","stance":"reference","why":"Meta-analysis suggests organic food slightly more nutritious.","quote":"A 2019 meta-analysis by Kurzgesagt [1] claims that organic food is slightly more nutritious with increased levels of antioxidants.","text":"The research is mixed and inconclusive. A 2019 meta-analysis by Kurzgesagt [1] claims that organic food is slightly more nutritious with increased levels of antioxidants. [1] [VIDEO] In my personal experience, I can taste the frigging poison in non-organic produce, and processed food just makes me more hungry."},{"id":18944381,"author":"mlindner","month":"2019-01","story":"Costco opening a $440M chicken farm to escape America's chicken monopoly","stance":"reference","why":"Organic food is not much different and worse for environment.","quote":"Organic food is negligibly different from non-organic food and is generally worse for the environment because of the additional resources required for it.","text":"Organic food is negligibly different from non-organic food and is generally worse for the environment because of the additional resources required for it. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3AtBE9BOvvk",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["GsyhGHUEt-k",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["Fy0aCDmgnxg",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["aVwxzDHniEw",1],["jGWFg7EDnyY",1],["goePYJ74Ydg",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1]]},"FzRP1qdwPKw":{"wy":"Recommended in Voyager threads as the best look at how operators actually contact the probe.","q":{"x":"Dave Jones of EEVBlog has a nice series of videos talking to Richard Stephenson, Operations Supervisor at the DSN's Canberra site.","i":17696243,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":35734942,"author":"7373737373","month":"2023-04","story":"NASA Power Hack Extends 45-Year Voyager 2 Mission Even Longer","stance":"rec","why":"How operators contact the Voyager 2 probe","quote":"Video recommendation: How To Contact The Voyager 2 Probe","text":"Video recommendation: How To Contact The Voyager 2 Probe ( [VIDEO] ) and Pinging The Voyager 2 Probe ( [LINK] ) by EEVDiscover"},{"id":17696243,"author":"misterS","month":"2018-08","story":"Deep Space Network Now: Status of communications with our deep space explorers","stance":"rec","why":"Dave Jones interviews a DSN Canberra operations supervisor","quote":"Dave Jones of EEVBlog has a nice series of videos talking to Richard Stephenson, Operations Supervisor at the DSN's Canberra site.","text":"Dave Jones of EEVBlog has a nice series of videos talking to Richard Stephenson, Operations Supervisor at the DSN's Canberra site. How To Contact The Voyager 2 Probe: Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK] Part 3: [LINK] (the playlist is a mess, and videos are unordered) There are also a couple of Behinde the Scenes videos."},{"id":40055455,"author":"mk_stjames","month":"2024-04","story":"NASA tries to jog Voyager 1's memory from 15B miles away","stance":"reference","why":"Context on DSN dishes and power used to reach Voyager","quote":"","text":"Well, we need much more on our end than the power of a cellular signal- For instance, one of the support antenna dishes of the Deep Space Network is DSS-42 in Canberra... which is a 70 meter diameter parabolic dish. And the transmit power on our side is in the tens of kilowatts. Literally tens of thousands of times more power needed than a cell phone and directed by a 70 meter dish.... We need that because the dish on the Voyager probes is 'only' 3.7 meters. Still... not a cell phone antenna. That said, the transmit power of the Voyagers is tiny in comparison to ours and it is closer to that of the cell phone analogy- still more, however, at about 23 watts [1]. So, our end task of receiving that, by the time it gets to us, is damn impressive indeed I think. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":24977601,"author":"_trampeltier","month":"2020-11","story":"Voyager 2 is back online after eight months of radio silence","stance":"reference","why":"Videos about the Canberra Deep Space Station","quote":"Dave Jones made a coupe of videos about the Deep Space Station in Canberra.","text":"Yes, there was even some new lately. \"Voyager Spacecraft Detect an Increase in The Density of Space Outside The Solar System\" [LINK] Dave Jones made a coupe of videos about the Deep Space Station in Canberra. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17678621,"author":"ncol","month":"2018-08","story":"JPL Open-Source Rover Project Based on the Rovers on Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Episodes on space engineering and operations","quote":"","text":"Haven't worked in this field, but here are some episodes discussing the topic: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] I think what you are talking about is mostly just normal engineering in the sense that the process is similar. What really makes space unique isn't space as such but that there is no real-world (real-space?) testing, no inspections after the fact or do-overs."},{"id":15829001,"author":"InclinedPlane","month":"2017-12","story":"After 37 years, Voyager has fired up its trajectory thrusters","stance":"reference","why":"Background on how the Deep Space Network talks to Voyager","quote":"","text":"There are high gain antennas on both ends, and use of error correcting codes, as well as extremely high grade low noise amplifiers on the Earth side. The Voyagers have a 3.7m diameter parabolic radio dish, larger than the Hubble space telescope's mirror even. That alone provides a huge amount of gain on communications. Additionally, the spacecraft have 10s of watts of power available for transmitting signals, which is a fair bit considering (while on the other end the ground stations have up to hundreds of thousands of watts to transmit). The ground-stations in the deep space network (DSN) are tens of meters across, a small antenna is 34m, the biggest ones are 70m across. That also provides a huge amount of gain alone. It means that there is more area to collect signals from the spacecraft and it means that the beam from the ground station to the spacecraft is much tighter, concentrating the total transmission power into a smaller cross-sectional area at the distance of the spacecraft. The spacecraft also uses error correcting codes, which involve transmitting many more bits than the underlying data, but in such a way that errors due to noise are not only detectable but correctable. On top of all of that you have the state of the art low noise amplifiers in the DSN antennae. A typical low noise amplifier is a carefully built electronics assembly made by experts. The DSN amplifiers? They use 99.95% purity ruby rods chilled to 4 degrees above absolute zero to form microwave MAS"}],"r":[["1rCrfQUcXDI",2],["5RKbSi87J5Q",1],["586Zn1ct-QA",1],["dCSIXLIzhzk",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["CiHN0ZWE5bk",1],["wfm2xCKOCNk",1],["f9EbD6iY9zI",1],["RjD1aLm4Thg",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1]]},"MSZgoFyuHC8":{"wy":"HN links it as a funny but fact-based critique of carbon capture in climate and energy threads.","q":{"x":"Comical, but fact-based take","i":28674703,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32795998,"author":"kornhole","month":"2022-09","story":"How to bury carbon? Let plants do the dirty work","stance":"rec","why":"Fun video about carbon capture.","quote":"Here is a fun video about carbon capture.","text":"I maintain a large garden and bury probably tons of organic matter underneath and in the beds. It is a pittance of carbon capture and no solution. Here is a fun video about carbon capture. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28515910,"author":"rfd4sgmk8u","month":"2021-09","story":"Australia is shaping up to be the villain of COP26 climate talks","stance":"rec","why":"Satire on fossil fuel capture of Australian politics.","quote":"If you want a good video that shows the entrenched problem with the fossil fuel industry","text":"If you want a good video that shows the entrenched problem with the fossil fuel industry that has the Australian political class captured: [VIDEO] (Honest Government Ad | Carbon Capture and Storage)"},{"id":31716126,"author":"robonerd","month":"2022-06","story":"Lilium achieves first main wing transition for all-electric aircraft [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Parody of carbon capture.","quote":"Related parody of carbon capture","text":"Atmospheric carbon capture seems like an petroleum industry scam to me (check the 'Members' page of that website.) In principle it's what plants do, but plants do it using scale; there's a whole bunch of them. CO2 is under 500ppm in our air, and air isn't particularly dense in the first place; the amount of air you'd need to move through your capture plant is immense. On the Costs & Outlook page of this site, they list their potential feedstocks; it's all biomass, except for the 'Technical Potential \"Unlimited\"' column, which mentions Power-to-Liquids. But how does that actually work and does it actually make sense? Related parody of carbon capture: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30042673,"author":"partomniscient","month":"2022-01","story":"Shell’s carbon capture plant is emitting more than it’s capturing","stance":"reference","why":"Satirical honest government ad on carbon capture accounting.","quote":"a satirical 'honest government ad' on carbon capture from an Australian viewpoint","text":"Here's ( [VIDEO] ) a satirical 'honest government ad' on carbon capture from an Australian viewpoint. We're attempting to apply dodgy accounting tricks to reality. It doesn't work. (I know there's greater transparency without it, but I too would really like []() markup for links on HN.)"},{"id":28674703,"author":"newt_slowly","month":"2021-09","story":"Europe’s energy crisis is about to go global as gas prices soar","stance":"reference","why":"Comical but fact-based critique of carbon capture.","quote":"Comical, but fact-based take","text":"Since you didn't go into detail, I'm going to guess that you are referring to atmospheric CCS being widely deployed in the future. Unfortunately CCS is not and will not be practical. [1] [2] I can explain my critiques in more detail if you clarify exactly what your argument is. [1] [LINK] [2] Comical, but fact-based take: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31832829,"author":"otikik","month":"2022-06","story":"Cheap, green hydrogen would be a breakthrough in clean energy","stance":"joke","why":"Sarcasm about carbon capture funding.","quote":"Carbon Capture and Storage works by capturing research funds and burying it deep","text":"Carbon Capture and Storage works by capturing research funds and burying it deep, deep into fossil fuel companies accounts, where no tax officer can reach them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30678333,"author":"otikik","month":"2022-03","story":"Antarctic sea ice hits lowest minimum on record","stance":"joke","why":"Sarcasm about carbon capture grants.","quote":"The only thing carbon capture is good at is at capturing public millions in grants","text":"The only thing carbon capture is good at is at capturing public millions in grants and storing them deep, deep into the accounts fossil fuel companies' bank accounts, where no tax officer can reach them. [VIDEO]"}]},"Pm8P4oCIY3g":{"wy":"HN commenters invoke it whenever a company relicenses, forks, or announces an open source plan, and enjoy Cantrill's energetic, opinionated delivery.","q":{"x":"Bryan Cantrill had an insightful talk \"Corporate Open Source Anti-Patterns","i":37083199,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37083199,"author":"geerlingguy","month":"2023-08","story":"CIQ, Oracle, SUSE Create Open Enterprise Linux Association","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful talk whose lessons echo current corporate open source events.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill had an insightful talk \"Corporate Open Source Anti-Patterns","text":"Bryan Cantrill had an insightful talk \"Corporate Open Source Anti-Patterns\" surrounding events from 8-12 years ago, and all I'll say is history definitely echoes sometimes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19322101,"author":"kakwa_","month":"2019-03","story":"Warren Buffett Joins the Crowd Struggling to Understand Oracle","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill is a passionate, entertaining speaker with sharp views on Oracle.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill is really fun to listen to, is a tech veteran now, and is still as passionate and energetic","text":"Just yesterday I came across one of his other videos, and I guess Bryan Cantrill still as a bit of grudge against Oracle: Light ranting: [VIDEO] A bit heavier ranting (with the fallout after his usenix ranting): [VIDEO] Selected quote: \"Any explanation of Oracle that doesn't end with a nazi allegory does not fully explain Oracle\" Bryan Cantrill is really fun to listen to, is a tech veteran now, and is still as passionate and energetic as he was the first day (Another interesting video about programming languages he experienced during his (long by tech standards) career: [LINK] )"},{"id":37083386,"author":"geerlingguy","month":"2023-08","story":"HashiCorp Adopts Business Source License","stance":"reference","why":"Talk names this pattern among corporate open source anti-patterns.","quote":"This is one of the Corporate Open Source anti-patterns Bryan Cantrill mentions in his talk a number of years ago","text":"This is one of the Corporate Open Source anti-patterns Bryan Cantrill mentions in his talk a number of years ago: [VIDEO] It always feels scummy having to assign copyright for my own contributions to a big company."},{"id":11176812,"author":"mh8h","month":"2016-02","story":"GPL Violations Related to Combining ZFS and Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Cantrill's discussion of different open source licenses.","quote":"See Bryan Cantrill's video about the history of Solaris [0] and about different licenses [1]","text":"I agree, many of the open source licenses are not pairwise-compatible. But the fact that GPL is a strong copyleft license makes collaboration more difficult. And the idea that CDDL was made specifically to be incompatible with GPL is at best a speculation. See Bryan Cantrill's video about the history of Solaris [0] and about different licenses [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9683039,"author":"foxhop","month":"2015-06","story":"“Swift will be open source later this year”","stance":"reference","why":"Talk arguing that announcing future open sourcing is an anti-pattern.","quote":"announcing something is going to be open sourced in the future is bad and is an anti pattern.","text":"[VIDEO] this is a talk by Joyant's Bryan Cantrill where he make the case that announcing something is going to be open sourced in the future is bad and is an anti pattern."},{"id":9454524,"author":"Flenser","month":"2015-04","story":"Release proposal: v2.0.0 (io.js)","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill's talk cited as basis for hoping for a follow-up.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill will do a follow up of sorts to Corporate Open Source Anti-patterns[1]","text":"I hope Bryan Cantrill will do a follow up of sorts to Corporate Open Source Anti-patterns[1] once this is all settled. It would be interesting to know what lessons there are to learn from this. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8717876,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2014-12","story":"Node.js is forked, not fucked","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill talk cited for the idea that forking is healthy.","quote":"forking is healthy because it allows (and encourages) experimentation with divergent ideas","text":"This piece is (by far) the best written on the io.js fork and its ramifications. As I have said many times before, we are forkaphilic[1] -- forking is healthy because it allows (and encourages) experimentation with divergent ideas. The primary idea that io.js appears to be experimenting with is \"more frequent releases\", in particular very closely tracking V8. This is somewhat ironic because the close tracking of V8 has been a major contributing factor to the delay in 0.12: despite good intentions, V8 breaks node.js frequently -- and debugging those problems is a time-consuming and arduous task requiring a great deal of technical sophistication. We have made clear that our technical priorities for the project are production-grade stability and performance; to the degree that other have different priorities, forking should be encouraged. In part to allow cross-pollination from any fork, we eliminated our CLA earlier this year[2]; the community can rest assured that good ideas from either will be able to be in both. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["yvhv7bgmz64",1],["CbHjcwIoTiY",1],["tDacjrSCeq4",1],["gSrnXgAmK8k",1]]},"S-3wEC6Fj_8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a rigorous, entertaining example of interface design review and notes the author later became head of design at MuseScore.","q":{"x":"In another video on Dorico [0], he did user testing. So he's not just pulling things out his ass.","i":26998737,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26998737,"author":"guitarbill","month":"2021-04","story":"I’m now in charge of Audacity [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Tantacrul's user testing of Dorico","quote":"In another video on Dorico [0], he did user testing. So he's not just pulling things out his ass.","text":"The MuseScore video is part entertainment, part critique. Personally, I don't think it's fair to dismiss it so quickly. He's very knowledgable in the field of music notation software, and is in a smaller niche of having used several of them professionally (most people stick to one or two). In another video on Dorico [0], he did user testing. So he's not just pulling things out his ass. It's easy to hate. MuseScore is better due to his involvement. The FOSS community should be more welcoming to UX devs in general, and especially who have a methodical approach and actually listen. Maybe wait and reserve judgement. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24996959,"author":"ohazi","month":"2020-11","story":"Update on MuseScore 3.6 and 4.0","stance":"rec","why":"Hilariously scathing UI/UX review of Dorico","quote":"some hilariously scathing UI/UX video reviews for some popular composing software tools","text":"For those out of the loop: Tantacrul is a composer who put together some hilariously scathing UI/UX video reviews for some popular composing software tools out there, including MuseScore [1] [2] [3]. The team developing MuseScore then brought him on as head of design. [1] Sibelius: [LINK] [2] MuseScore: [LINK] [3] Dorico: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27173202,"author":"lifthrasiir","month":"2021-05","story":"Audacity: Actions we propose to take on PR 835","stance":"reference","why":"Significant first-time user testing for Dorico","quote":"has once conducted a significant first-time user testing for Dorico","text":"Tantacrul, the project lead for both MuseScore and Audacity, is a professional UI/UX designer and has once conducted a significant first-time user testing for Dorico [1], so I'm pretty sure that he already knows what you are saying. Given his latest video about Audacity [2] though, I guess that Audacity has a very diverse user base and it might be hard to do the user testing while accounting for that diversity (the article you've linked specifically mention this issue). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] (includes an interview with original Audacity developers)"},{"id":26398187,"author":"jasode","month":"2021-03","story":"Music Software and Bad Interface Design: Avid’s Sibelius (2018) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"One-hour video on Dorico's interface","quote":"same uploader also made a 1 hour video about Dorico[2]","text":"Well, the founders of Sibelius created another music notation software called Dorico[1]. Is Dorico's UI more consistent? Does it address many of the issues of this video's criticism? Or put another way, does being a green-field software project allow the freedom to create a sane UI? Or did it have to deliberately copy may of Sibelius' faults so migrating users can quickly get up to speed with Dorico? (Analogous to MS Excel copying Lotus 123 buggy leap year of 1900 to be more compatible.) EDIT -- just noticed same uploader also made a 1 hour video about Dorico[2] but not sure if answers my questions. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23348490,"author":"Nullabillity","month":"2020-05","story":"Linux Sucks 2020","stance":"reference","why":"Tantacrul's Dorico review, mentions his hiring at MuseScore","quote":"Tantacrul did one on MuseScore[0] (a music composing program), and ended up getting hired as their head of design[1].","text":"Tantacrul did one on MuseScore[0] (a music composing program), and ended up getting hired as their head of design[1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: Mentioned in [VIDEO] , but don't have the exact timestamp"}],"r":[["MsbiO8EAsGw",1],["bM0PmwOlifE",1]]},"Um63OQz3bjo":{"wy":"HN commenters sent it to newcomers as a short, simple first explanation of Bitcoin.","q":{"x":"Usually I just send people that video","i":5656618,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5656618,"author":"munchor","month":"2013-05","story":"How to Explain Bitcoin to a 7-Year-Old","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter sends people this video to explain bitcoin.","quote":"Usually I just send people that video","text":"[VIDEO] I agree that explaining people (be them 7 or 20 years old) what bitcoins are and what the fuss is all about is quite complicated. Usually I just send people that video, but this explanation is pretty interesting (maybe not as effective as the video, though)."},{"id":2358210,"author":"sgornick","month":"2011-03","story":"What is Bitcoin? (Video)","stance":"rec","why":"Newcomer-friendly introduction to Bitcoin.","quote":"For those who are new to Bitcoin and would like to learn more: The new What Is Bitcoin? video -","text":"For those who are new to Bitcoin and would like to learn more: The new What Is Bitcoin? video - [VIDEO] Bitcoin FAQ - [LINK] Bitcoin Wiki - [LINK] How Bitcoin Works - [LINK] Installing Bitcoin - [LINK] HowTo Mining Bitcoin: Fedora 14: [LINK] and Ubuntu: [LINK] The Bitcoin Faucet (free bitcoins) - [LINK] Buying bitcoins - [LINK] Bitcoin 6-Month Price Chart - [LINK] Bitcoin Community Portal - [LINK] Recent Posts here on the forum - [LINK] ------------- Comments from February when BTC/USD first hit $1: [LINK]"},{"id":15866432,"author":"jsmthrowaway","month":"2017-12","story":"Steam is no longer supporting Bitcoin","stance":"reference","why":"Bitcoin pitched as a cash-payment replacement.","quote":"You can purchase video games, gifts, books, servers, and alpaca socks.","text":"> I don't think most reputable proponents have been saying that for years though. Define reputable. > Announcing the first release of Bitcoin, a new electronic cash system [LINK] > Instant transactions for points-of-sale [LINK] (That verbiage lasted most of 2013, but was removed a couple days after this capture, presumably because payments were starting to become less \"instant.\") > Bitcoin is a new payment system, independent from the traditional financial sector, and this could provide resilience to the economy in case of a crisis, as it creates a parallel payment system. Understanding Bitcoin: Cryptography, Engineering, and Economics by Pedro Franco, 2010, pp. 26. > You can purchase video games, gifts, books, servers, and alpaca socks. [...] Bitcoins are a great way for small businesses and freelancers to get noticed. It doesn't cost anything to start accepting them [...] and you'll get additional business from the Bitcoin economy. [VIDEO] Bitcoin has literally been presented as a replacement for cash payments since day 1. I could go on, and on. That history is now being rewritten, and I think it’s important to understand why. Bitcoin set out to do one thing, is failing at that one thing, and I’m discouraged to see people rewrite the narrative rather than own it and say it didn’t work for that. And you've mixed up your history: the research grants into the Internet were funded for that purpose, but it quickly morphed into an alternative usage once the new abilities were see"},{"id":7032986,"author":"sandyarmstrong","month":"2014-01","story":"Overstock.com Is Now Accepting Bitcoins","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"which says \"bitcoins\" about a million times","text":"Good luck with that. Overstock and Coinbase both link to this weusecoins YouTube video to explain the currency, which says \"bitcoins\" about a million times: [VIDEO]"}]},"ZDxLa6P6exc":{"wy":"Recommended in threads about SGI and vintage workstations as a good look at the hardware.","q":{"x":"he did a great video on it","i":39952525,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39952525,"author":"pixelpoet","month":"2024-04","story":"The Rise and Fall of Silicon Graphics","stance":"rec","why":"Video on the SGI Indigo2 workstation.","quote":"he did a great video on it","text":"Random trivia which will likely get buried but whatever: the rendering software Octane Render got its name from my refusing to let the author use my rendering software's name, and he had some historical beef with us, the two devs of Indigo Renderer, so he named his renderer after the SGI machine that came after the Indigo, Octane. He also leant his SGI Indigo2 to the LGR guy on YouTube, and he did a great video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30334084,"author":"ckozlowski","month":"2022-02","story":"A baby saved ‘Toy Story 2’ from near complete deletion (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Covers SGI workstations like the Octane.","quote":"LGR and RMC have both done videos on the SGI Octane and are worth watching","text":"You're on the right track. As told in the Next Web story, the workstation in question was a Silicon Graphics Octane or there abouts. These were incredibly expensive and were provided by the company. In addition, according to the story, they were the ones who set up the file sync for her to be able to work from home. LGR and RMC have both done videos on the SGI Octane and are worth watching: [VIDEO] , [LINK] Edit: It was Retro Man Cave, not Adrian's Digital Basement as originally written."},{"id":20106825,"author":"w_s_l","month":"2019-06","story":"The charm of buying old workstation hardware on the cheap","stance":"reference","why":"SGI workstation as object of nostalgia.","quote":"For one I would love to have an SGI workstation","text":"For one I would love to have an SGI workstation, I still have CG magazines from this era and its plastered everywhere in the ads, and I'm still amazed at the graphic output (so smooth) and what we had in the 90s. [VIDEO] I'd imagine something like this would've been used to make Crash Bandicoot and likes. It'd be really interesting to play games built on this thing."}],"r":[["Gv2I7qTux7g",1]]},"Zn7oWIhFffs":{"wy":"HN cites it in algorithmic-bias threads for the impossibility result that an algorithm cannot be procedurally fair, group fair and maximally useful at once.","q":{"x":"it's impossible to simultaneously treat everyone the same way, to have a useful model, and to have uniformly distributed 'bias'-free outcomes.","i":21961200,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26148386,"author":"PeterisP","month":"2021-02","story":"Who Should Stop Unethical A.I.?","stance":"rec","why":"Decent exploration of fairness tradeoffs, despite some flaws.","quote":"it has some flaws but is a decent exploration of the problem space.","text":"In many unbalanced situations you simply can't have an unbiased decision that's fair across all measures, no matter if the decision is done by a model, a human or a deity; you have to trade off between different types of unfairness. Equality of opportunity for individuals will result in unequal results for groups; equality of outcomes requires unequal opportunities if the historical circumstances have resulted in socioeconomic unequalities. In your financial evaluations example, many biases and disadvantages would remain even if you solely reduce the decision to relevant financial facts for a specific individual, because a poorer individual with lower socioeconomic status and less opportunities actually has a higher risk of non-payment, and a disadvantaged group will have disproportionally more such individuals. Should we accept that? Should we require the other groups to subsidize their non-payment? Both options are unfair in some aspect and fair in another, you can't have your cake and eat it too, and it's not the fault of the model you use - the only difference with a human is that they can better hide the factors they use, lie about the influences (perhaps also lie to themselves) and rationalize/invent factors to justify their decision. For this topic, perhaps this talk \"AI Ethics, Impossibility Theorems and Tradeoffs\" [VIDEO] or its slides [LINK] might be interesting for you, it has some flaws but is a decent exploration of the problem space."},{"id":18981722,"author":"dvtrn","month":"2019-01","story":"Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez Is Absolutely Right About Racist Algorithms","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation expanding on the slide deck.","quote":"found a video of his presentation if anyone else is interested.","text":"I was interested in the slide deck and wanted to hear the author expand on some of his thoughts, found a video of his presentation if anyone else is interested. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21961200,"author":"missosoup","month":"2020-01","story":"Why are we using black box models in AI when we don’t need to? (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Trilemma of equal treatment, usefulness, and bias-free outcomes.","quote":"it's impossible to simultaneously treat everyone the same way, to have a useful model, and to have uniformly distributed 'bias'-free outcomes.","text":"> I think it's also important to remember a lot of human models were abandoned because of the inherent bias The industry needs to stop misusing the term bias this way. Virtually every attempt to find this supposed human bias has failed. Latest public example was Amazon and hiring[1] Bias is the tendency to consistently mis-classify towards a certain class or tendency to consistently to over or under-estimate. Somehow the term has been hijacked to mean 'discriminate on factors that are politically incorrect'. You can have a super racist model that's bias free, and most models blinded to protected factors are in fact statistically biased. It's not constructive to conflate actual bias with political incorrectness. Operational decision making, whether AI or human or statistical, faces an inherent trilemma: it's impossible to simultaneously treat everyone the same way, to have a useful model, and to have uniformly distributed 'bias'-free outcomes. At best a model can strive to achieve two of these factors. See: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":21367270,"author":"missosoup","month":"2019-10","story":"New York Regulator Probes UnitedHealth Algorithm for Racial Bias","stance":"reference","why":"Fairness tradeoff: pick two of procedural, group, utility.","quote":"{procedural fairness, group fairness, utility}. Pick two.","text":"This 'algorithm has bias' kind of headline needs to die. Every system has '''bias''' in the colloquial sense so long as the inputs to that system are not uniformly distributed. The only thing the designer of the system can do is make a conscious choice about which way to '''bias''' the system and be able to justify that choice. In practical terms it means that systems that deal with diverse populations are always going to be 'unfair' towards some group of people. The only control we have is who that group of people will be. This is inherent and unavoidable. We need to accept this fact and keep it in consideration when designing such systems (or choosing not to design them in the first place). {procedural fairness, group fairness, utility}. Pick two. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21054963,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2019-09","story":"600k Images Removed from ImageNet After Art Project Exposes Racist Bias","stance":"reference","why":"Stucchio's impossibility theorem on algorithmic fairness.","quote":"it is impossible for a given algorithm to be procedurally fair, representationally fair, and utilitarian at the same time","text":"See also Stucchio's impossibility theorem: it is impossible for a given algorithm to be procedurally fair, representationally fair, and utilitarian at the same time: Video: [VIDEO] Slides: [LINK]"},{"id":19802042,"author":"joshmarlow","month":"2019-05","story":"Building Inclusive AI at Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"The talk accompanying an interesting slide deck.","quote":"I've not watched it yet, but this appears to be the talk","text":"That's a quite interesting slide deck! For others interested, I've not watched it yet, but this appears to be the talk [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["a-BOSpxYJ9M",1]]},"hdt18p-VMmQ":{"wy":"Makers recommend it as a behind-the-scenes look at crunch-time hardware engineering for a big YouTube production.","q":{"x":"the making off video of his squid game blood packs is really interesting from a maker perspective","i":38896868,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38896868,"author":"cf1241290841","month":"2024-01","story":"YouTube star MrBeast rejects Elon Musk's appeal to share content on X","stance":"rec","why":"Making-of the blood packs: 500 wifi bloodpacks in 3 weeks, development hell","quote":"the making off video of his squid game blood packs is really interesting from a maker perspective","text":"IMO a lot more interesting story around mr beast: If you have a bit of patience, while very much annoying, the making off video of his squid game blood packs is really interesting from a maker perspective. > How I Almost Ruined MrBeast Squid Game [VIDEO] or 7:30 if you know what a squib is They had 3 weeks (yes) to get 500 wifi controlled bloodpacks. And went through all stages of development hell. After technical problems, regulatory problems, supply chain problems, manufacturing problems and last but not least the obvious integration problems it started a day late (+500k$ for MrBeast) because the team with the targeting software had shit latency and drained their batteries prematurely. In the end they still added cgi for a better videoflow and used thin yellow paint to not get demonetized. Again, worth the pain of watching it. Turn down the sound a bit and stay strong. Skip forward if need be He also had an interview later once you are through with the video. > The TRUTH Behind Mr Beast's Squid Game Video [LINK]"},{"id":33020358,"author":"atribecalledqst","month":"2022-09","story":"On Mr. Beast and being alone in a circle for 100 days","stance":"rec","why":"Engineer's account of hacking together MrBeast's Squid Game event in three weeks","quote":"a video from one of the engineers involved in his Squid Game recreation that I thought was pretty interesting","text":"I'm not really interested in Mr. Beast's content but there is a video from one of the engineers involved in his Squid Game recreation that I thought was pretty interesting. Seems like this guy went for three whole weeks without sleeping, just piling on hack after hack to make the event come together. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37554284,"author":"gregdoesit","month":"2023-09","story":"How Jeff Geerling almost broke MrBeast's 1-100 video [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Team building 456 detonator units for the Squid Game video","quote":"another team build 456 \"detonator units\" for the Squid Game video: also finished in the nick of time","text":"It's pretty amusing how a bunch of teams are posting videos on how they built a specific, bespoke hardware/software component or two of MrBeast videos. This one was about the ~100 hardware button sets and LED strips required for the video. And here is another one [1] that is on how another team build 456 \"detonator units\" for the Squid Game video: also finished in the nick of time. It seems clever for MrBeast to hire DIY YouTubers to do these bespoke jobs: it's a win-win even after the video, as they teams create their own \"how we built this\" video. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Cyya23MPoAI",1]]},"hzGaO4Kdz2Y":{"wy":"Pointed to when people want to see the cams and gears inside a mechanical navigation computer.","q":{"x":"if you want to see inside a mechanical computer of this type","i":11465383,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13999202,"author":"david-given","month":"2017-03","story":"The Dornier DO-960 Analog Computer","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown of a B52 astrocompass mechanical analogue computer.","quote":"So, so many beautiful cams and moving arms...","text":"Eevblog has a teardown of the mechanical analogue computer that drove an astrocompass from a B52 bomber: [VIDEO] So, so many beautiful cams and moving arms..."},{"id":11465383,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2016-04","story":"When mechanical analog computers ruled the waves (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown of a mechanical analog navigation computer from a B52.","quote":"if you want to see inside a mechanical computer of this type","text":"Dave Jones of EEVBlog recently tore down an analog navigation computer used on the B52 if you want to see inside a mechanical computer of this type [VIDEO]"},{"id":11857904,"author":"gherkin0","month":"2016-06","story":"Planned GPS outages in southern California","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of a B-52 mechanical analog computer.","quote":"EEVBlog did a teardown part of one of these systems from a B-52","text":"EEVBlog did a teardown part of one of these systems from a B-52 ( [VIDEO] ). IIRC, it used a completely mechanical analog computer."}],"r":[["QspuCt1FM9M",1],["Nnuq9PXbywA",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1],["aHun58mz3vI",1]]},"kBLkX2VaQs4":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up as a reminder to appreciate technology instead of complaining, from Waymo to AI art to flight delays.","q":{"x":"I like to watch this Louis CK clip, it really puts me back in place...for a while.","i":28733294,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28733294,"author":"silisili","month":"2021-10","story":"Farmer born in 1842 talks about life and change (1929) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Puts impatience with instant gratification in perspective.","quote":"I like to watch this Louis CK clip, it really puts me back in place...for a while.","text":"Similar thought here. I don't know if it was the era or being relatively poor, but the big change in my life was giftgiving. You used to get a book or sweater or doodad and be wowed and thankful. As an adult it seems everyone has Amazon and everything they want, so what's to do but trade boring gift cards we don't need. How exciting. I will say one thing I noticed and dislike about myself is how impatient and 'privileged' I've become. I mean, we used to order from Sears or Penney's catalogues as a kid and that stuff took weeks to arrive, and we were thrilled. Today it's all instant gratification. Amazon is a day late and we(I) act perturbed. I like to watch this Louis CK clip, it really puts me back in place...for a while. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24721727,"author":"flokie","month":"2020-10","story":"Waymo is opening its driverless service to more people in Phoenix","stance":"rec","why":"Reminder that today's wonders get dismissed quickly.","quote":"before you comment watch","text":"I know the community is generally very negative/skeptic on this (at least based on the 21 comments so far below), but this is an amazing technical & business accomplishment. Congratulations! The blog post is relatively clear that ramp up will take a few weeks - \"we’ll start with those who are already a part of Waymo One and, over the next several weeks, welcome more people directly into the service through our app\" before you comment watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36130022,"author":"tlrobinson","month":"2023-05","story":"Turds in AI Generated Art","stance":"reference","why":"Reminder that we take amazing progress for granted.","quote":"nothing like this was remotely possible a year ago","text":"I’m reminded of Louis CK’s rant “ Everything Is Amazing and Nobody Is Happy”: [VIDEO] Like, nothing like this was remotely possible a year ago. I’m sure we’ll hit a wall at some point, but you certainly shouldn’t dismiss things as not possible because they’re not currently possible."},{"id":46523660,"author":"coldtea","month":"2026-01","story":"The creator of Claude Code's Claude setup","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"OK, so you have the unbearable pain of using a separate terminal app to use the magic thingie that does your programming for you on prompt, and which didn't exist merely 2 years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27524142,"author":"EMM_386","month":"2021-06","story":"Southwest Airlines grounds its entire fleet amid giant computer outage","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Louis CK bit on Conan about people complaining about flying.","text":"Louis CK bit on Conan about people complaining about flying. Certainly relatable reading some passenger complaints regarding this Southwest debacle. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23985921,"author":"anticristi","month":"2020-07","story":"Thoughts about the difference between European and American H/W and S/W design","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This. I sometimes turned to my girlfriend while taking off in a plane (back in 2019 :P) and tell her: \"OMFG! We realise humanities dream of flying!\" More examples from the comedian Louis CK: [VIDEO]"}]},"Dw3BZ6O_8LY":{"wy":"Recommended as an approachable explanation of how reinforcement learning works in practice, and cited for the constraints needed to stop exploits.","q":{"x":"be sure to check out the videos by Yosh. They're amazing, and do a great job explaining how reinforcement learning works in practice","i":40872860,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40872860,"author":"smokel","month":"2024-07","story":"The History of Machine Learning in Trackmania","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing explanations of how RL works in practice","quote":"be sure to check out the videos by Yosh. They're amazing, and do a great job explaining how reinforcement learning works in practice","text":"For those who only read the comments, be sure to check out the videos by Yosh. They're amazing, and do a great job explaining how reinforcement learning works in practice: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":43160209,"author":"szvsw","month":"2025-02","story":"AI-designed chips are so weird that 'humans cannot understand them'","stance":"reference","why":"RL on Trackmania needed constraints to prevent glitch exploits","quote":"someone recently used RL to solve trackmania, and ends up having to add all sorts of constraints/penalties","text":"There’s a few very cool examples where someone recently used RL to solve trackmania, and ends up having to add all sorts of constraints/penalties to prevent extremely strange exploits/glitches that are discovered IIRC… been a while since I watched. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":41950538,"author":"fragmede","month":"2024-10","story":"OmniParser for Pure Vision Based GUI Agent","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it lets you get a world record and a YouTube video with 14M views.","text":"Depends what you consider fun, and how far you take it. Some people enjoy programming more than repetitive clicking in a GUI. For a clicker game, writing a bot lets you iterate on strategies easier - is it faster to get to level 2 if I buy the upgrade for A or B first? For Trackmania, it lets you get a world record and a YouTube video with 14M views. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38903341,"author":"emporas","month":"2024-01","story":"Chess-GPT's Internal World Model","stance":"reference","why":"Example of encoding extra info in RL training","quote":"See for example this training experiment for Trackmania [1].","text":">The entire point of this is to not encode the board state! I am not sure about this. From the article \"The 50M parameter model played at 1300 ELO with 99.8% of its moves being legal within one day of training.\" I thought that the experiment was how well the model will perform, given that it's reward function is to predict text, rather than checkmate. Leela, Alpha0 their reward function is to win the game, checkmate or capture pieces. Also it goes without saying that Leela, Alpha0 cannot make illegal moves. The experiment does not need to include the whole board position if that's a problem, if that's an important point of interest. It could encode more information about squares covered by each side for example. See for example this training experiment for Trackmania [1]. There are techniques that the ML algorithm will *never* figure out by itself if this information is not encoded in it's training data. The point still stands. PGN notation certainly is not a good format if the goal (or one of the goals) of the experiment is to be a good chess player. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["c2lgEyNaop4",1]]},"GaRurhiK-Lk":{"wy":"HN points to it as a mind-blowing seed-cracking story related to other Minecraft RNG reversal work.","q":{"x":"it'll blow your mind","i":40085195,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40085195,"author":"ben_bai","month":"2024-04","story":"Randar: A Minecraft exploit that uses LLL lattice reduction to crack server RNG","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing Minecraft seed discovery","quote":"it'll blow your mind","text":"Yeah the Minecraft and MC anarchy community is insane. If you found this amazing, take a look at this, it'll blow your mind. [LINK] and [VIDEO]"},{"id":31546073,"author":"Qub3d","month":"2022-05","story":"How do video games stay in sync?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I mean, if you can acquire or otherwise reverse-engineer[0] the game seed, you can also just find resources by loading a local copy and noting the coordinates of ore. For major servers, anti-xray plugins will be installed as due diligence, but most of the anti-cheat efforts are focused on detection, reverting, and banning. Ultimately, if you have a big enough server to attract serious cheaters, you will (or at least should ) have tools that can also detect suspicious behavior based on heuristics (i.e. see if a player mined straight to an ore block). Tools like CoreProtect[1] can help detect and revert this. Ore obfuscation still works very well, however, for the majority of causal cheaters that just googled \"hacked minecraft client\" and installed the first result. One ore obfuscation technique used in PaperMC actually sends intentionally fake data to the user to \"muddy the waters\"[2]. (I know a lot of this because I help develop a Minecraft server management tool) [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":24621521,"author":"mastazi","month":"2020-09","story":"Minecraft's “Pack.png” Seed Reversal Methodology","stance":"reference","why":"Related discovery of the title screen seed","quote":"Related but different discovery: how the title screen was found","text":"For context: [LINK] if you prefer reading instead of watching a video [LINK] Related but different discovery: how the title screen was found [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ZUvGfuLlZus",1]]},"J9nv0Ol-R5Q":{"wy":"HN commenters offer it as an expert, skeptical overview of what cryptocurrency actually is and its weaknesses.","q":{"x":"fantastic overview of what cryptocurrency and Blockchain actually is","i":33684072,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33684072,"author":"roland35","month":"2022-11","story":"Let crypto burn","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Overview of what cryptocurrency and blockchain are and the case against them.","quote":"fantastic overview of what cryptocurrency and Blockchain actually is","text":"This lecture by Berkeley CS professor Nicholas Weaver is a fantastic overview of what cryptocurrency and Blockchain actually is and why it is a complete waste of time, energy, and money and needs to be reduced to 0: [VIDEO] I used to think \"well maybe there is some real application of Bitcoin\" but the more I learned about it I just realized the entire enterprise is awful and the only real benefit it has is uncensored money transfer. Which is great for ransomware. Now that Bitcoin is practically controlled by under 10 miners, there really is no decentralized part of it. Bitcoin maxis say you don't need to trust anyone, but that's not true. You gotta trust the code. Trust the miners. Also what good is magic internet money if I need to keep everything in an offline cold storage wallet to be secure? The only useful application now is using Bitcoin as a honeypot in your servers to notify you if a hacker has access to your systems (you'll see it when the wallet is drained!)."},{"id":37314854,"author":"jokoon","month":"2023-08","story":"Griffin - A fully-regulated, API-driven bank, with Clojure","stance":"reference","why":"One-hour security professor lecture on cryptocurrency.","quote":"A 1 hour lecture from a security professor on crypto currency","text":"Unless finance institutions and laws can protect wallets and insure crypto, I will never use it. Currency is not just a number you move around, currency is deeply intertwined with how civilization works, and it requires a high amount of trust for people to use it. Trust is rather a social concept that something you can really prove mathematically. It's collective trust. Unless you can DRASTICALLY reduce mis-use with bitcoin, no one with trust it ever without a lot of expensive protective measures. A 1 hour lecture from a security professor on crypto currency: [VIDEO] Something else: [LINK]"},{"id":31685815,"author":"agentultra","month":"2022-06","story":"In defense of cryptocurrency","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture offered as an expert source on cryptocurrency's weaknesses.","quote":"don't take my word for it","text":"I'm experienced enough to read what papers and proofs have been published, have been a programmer for longer than not, but don't take my word for it: [VIDEO] So they found one way to prevent Sybil attacks that has been a well-known problem. Big deal. Update A \"blockchain\" is not a novel concept. The PoW system used is one way to prevent Sybil attacks when distributing work among untrusted peers, and that's it. Novel, neat, but useless. If cryptocurrencies needed defending it's because they're a solution in search of a problem. All of the problems they've found so far appeal to conspiracy theorists: people who don't trust banks, government, and all kinds of things. In the mean time the people who've benefited the most have left thousands of people bereft of their life savings and ruined their lives. My favourite right now are all the scammers who've turned around and stopped ripping off banks and old people and started targeting people who buy NFTs and adverise it on their socials. It'd be freaking hilarious to watch unfold if so many people weren't getting burned in the process."},{"id":31376817,"author":"dandanua","month":"2022-05","story":"Why All Cryptocurrency Should Die in a Fire","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture clip by the article's author on what cryptocurrency is good for.","quote":"There is only one thing cryptocurrency is good for","text":"\"There is only one thing cryptocurrency is good for\" [VIDEO] It's from author's lecture."},{"id":31444388,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-05","story":"‘Extortion’: Why Web3 is making a lot of software developers angry","stance":"reference","why":"Berkeley CS professor's lecture critiquing cryptocurrency.","quote":"Cf. this video by a Berkeley CompSci professor","text":"> Venture capitalists and entrepreneurs are betting the emerging Web3 space is the next generation of the internet Venture capitalists that are \"betting on crypto\" are not, in fact, betting on crypto. They are committing securities fraud en masse . 1/ Invest in crypto startups 2/ Let them offer tokens to the public, which are, in effect, unlicensed securities (that's the fraud part) 3/ Either wait for the value of the startups to skyrocket, or, even better, obtain the crypto tokens for cheap and resell them yourself in exchange for real money 4/ Profit! Cf. this video by a Berkeley CompSci professor [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["MvgN5gCuLac",1]]},"0nCT8h8gO1g":{"wy":"Commenters point to it when discussing the lax regulation of cruise ships and flags of convenience.","q":{"x":"Hasan Minhaj did an interesting episode about cruise ships recently on \"Patriot Act\" if you're interested","i":21170346,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21170346,"author":"guptaneil","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: Cool stuff that's still completely unregulated?","stance":"rec","why":"Episode on cruise industry's lax regulation and flags of convenience","quote":"Hasan Minhaj did an interesting episode about cruise ships recently on \"Patriot Act\" if you're interested","text":"Yes, but nearly all cruise ships are registered with tiny countries like Panama and the Bahamas, in order to have the most lax laws applied to them. Hasan Minhaj did an interesting episode about cruise ships recently on \"Patriot Act\" if you're interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41480669,"author":"asib","month":"2024-09","story":"Cruise ships chopped in half are a license to print money","stance":"reference","why":"Cruise companies sweeping crimes at sea under the carpet","quote":"Cruise companies expend effort to sweep these crimes under the carpet","text":"> you don't let criminals onboard Plenty of crimes happen at sea. Cruise companies expend effort to sweep these crimes under the carpet: [VIDEO] Criminals are people who've been convicted of a crime. These people are either: - allowed on cruises (I'm not aware of a \"no conviction\" clause when buying a cruise ticket) - in jail, in which case you can also make the argument they're not allowed out in public So criminals are allowed on board, and people who might commit a crime are not necessarily criminals yet."},{"id":25590601,"author":"goblin89","month":"2020-12","story":"The Cruise Ship Suicides","stance":"reference","why":"Public scrutiny of the cruise industry","quote":"Cruise ship business was being slowly dragged","text":"This has to do with the fact that “high seas” are not legally under any jurisdiction, right? Cruise ship business was being slowly dragged¹ into the public eye as of last year… and then COVID hit. I imagine things must have gotten only worse in the general chaos of the pandemic. ¹ [VIDEO]"}]},"1vui-LupKJI":{"wy":"HN links it as the historical source for two projects: Hy's first announcement and the earliest recorded Docker talk.","m":[{"id":6700546,"author":"paultag","month":"2013-11","story":"Try Hy","stance":"rec","why":"Quick 5 minute lightning talk introducing Hy, by its creator.","quote":"","text":"Well done! If anyone wants to learn more: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] and a quick 5 minute lightning talk: [VIDEO] (Creator here) Hack on!"},{"id":47291440,"author":"pixelmonkey","month":"2026-03","story":"A Decade of Docker Containers","stance":"reference","why":"Earliest recorded Docker talk by Solomon Hykes, PyCon 2013 lightning talks.","quote":"","text":"The math of “a decade” seemed wrong to me, since I remembered Docker debuting in 2013 at PyCon US Santa Clara. Then I found an HN comment I wrote a few years ago that confirmed this: [...] I remember that day pretty clearly because in the same lightning talk session, Solomon Hykes introduced the Python community to docker, while still working on dotCloud. This is what I think might have been the earliest public and recorded tech talk on the subject. [VIDEO] [Starts at 26:19 (t=1579).] This was also the same PyCon that Eben Upton opened with a keynote to discuss the mission of Raspberry Pi, while also announcing that each of the 2,500 attendees would receive a free physical Raspberry Pi at the conference. It was a pretty darn good year of PyCon; 2013 was apparently a good vintage for open source tech with Python connections. Just being pedantic though. That’s about 13 years ago. The lightning talk is fun as a bit of computing history."},{"id":41618371,"author":"PuercoPop","month":"2024-09","story":"Hy 1.0 – Lisp dialect for Python","stance":"reference","why":"Original Hy announcement showing it compiles Lisp to Python bytecode.","quote":"","text":"The original hy annoucement makes it clear that they embed a Lisp by compiling with Python bytecode. You can see it in the following video about the 16:25 mark [VIDEO]"},{"id":41626592,"author":"Slackwise","month":"2024-09","story":"Hy 1.0 – Lisp dialect for Python","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes the speaker saying Hy compiles entirely to Python AST.","quote":"","text":"The actual statement in the video is: > ...because this is a frontend like LLVM or GCC that compiles instead of bytecode, uh, to Python AST, um, so this Lisp compiles entirely to Python @ [VIDEO]"},{"id":20605780,"author":"pixelmonkey","month":"2019-08","story":"Hy","stance":"reference","why":"Historic PyCon 2013 lightning talks: Hy's debut and Docker's first public talk.","quote":"","text":"hy was first introduced to me at PyCon 2013 in a lightning talk by @paultag, the language's creator. It was a very fun lightning talk. You can still watch it online here: [VIDEO] I remember that day pretty clearly because in the same lightning talk session, Solomon Hykes introduced the Python community to docker, while still working on dotCloud. This is what I think might have been the earliest public and recorded tech talk on the subject. [VIDEO] This was also the same PyCon that Eben Upton opened with a keynote to discuss the mission of Raspberry Pi, while also announcing that each of the 2,500 attendees would receive a free physical Raspberry Pi at the conference. It was a pretty darn good year of PyCon; 2013 was apparently a good vintage for open source tech with Python connections."}]},"2f2kGHcdJYU":{"wy":"HN cites it as a compact explanation of the housing crash and of institutions keeping their fingers on the scales.","q":{"x":"I had to search for this excellent clip from Margin Call, which explains this better than I could","i":22073310,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22073310,"author":"golergka","month":"2020-01","story":"Mozilla lays off 70","stance":"rec","why":"Margin Call clip explaining the housing crash.","quote":"I had to search for this excellent clip from Margin Call, which explains this better than I could","text":"Housing market got destroyed by people not paying back their loans. Assigning this to be a moral responsibility of banks and regulators is simplistic and naive. I had to search for this excellent clip from Margin Call, which explains this better than I could: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39498550,"author":"crotchfire","month":"2024-02","story":"Institutions try to preserve the problem to which they are the solution","stance":"reference","why":"Margin Call quote on being necessary and fingers on the scales.","quote":"The only reason that they all get to continue living like kings is cause we got our fingers on the scales in their favor.","text":"> On a lot of these issues I feel we're all stuck on some rollercoaster ride that almost no one really likes, but also no one really has the courage to stop. As Will Emerson says in Margin Call : \"Jesus, Seth. Listen, if you really wanna do this with your life you have to believe you're necessary and you are. People wanna live like this in their cars and big fuckin' houses they can't even pay for, then you're necessary. The only reason that they all get to continue living like kings is cause we got our fingers on the scales in their favor. I take my hand off and then the whole world gets really fuckin' fair really fuckin' quickly and nobody actually wants that. They say they do but they don't.\" [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":36167970,"author":"candiodari","month":"2023-06","story":"When Markets Melt Down, These Traders Cash In","stance":"reference","why":"Clip on banks keeping a hand on the scale.","quote":"","text":"I wonder how this strategy is affected by the 2008 experience. You see, back then all sorts of, frankly, illegal measures were used to prevent those who bet against the housing market to cash out. Markets were frozen, contracts and even stocks artificially priced, for most investors right until the contracts were worthless due to bankruptcy, as pointed out in \"the Big Short\". Yes, VERY few investors did exactly the right thing at exactly the right time and got lucky. [LINK] Wall Street outright lied to offload known crashed investments to unsuspecting buyers. Again, this is obviously highly illegal. Nobody got punished, we can argue about why not, but one can remark it would have seriously affected the government. [LINK] It would have made both parties unelectable for a century because Will Emerson would have been forced \"to take his hand off the scale\". [VIDEO] (second part of this clip, after 1m05s) The banks will not play fair. This can be proven by pointing out that they simply can't. If they make profits, they get those out of the company rapidly, and by design, the system cannot pay out the contracts it underwrites beyond a minimal \"safety margin\". They simply cannot cover the contracts they underwrite in the event of a crash, they don't have the money. You'd have to hope that the FED covers those contracts ... and ... not even if hell freezes over will they do so. And of course even this is ignoring real \"black swan\" events. Here's the thing: if a real meteor or comet "}],"r":[["8X69_42Mj-g",1],["d6iQrh2TK98",1]]},"7mmlmxamw_k":{"wy":"Cited as an example of someone publicly changing their mind on the Moon landing hoax and of why skeptics had rational-seeming doubts.","q":{"x":"I really liked Joe Rogan's video on this topic","i":17578561,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17578561,"author":"amorphid","month":"2018-07","story":"Why do people believe the moon landing hoax or other conspiracy theories?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how doctored training images gave skeptics a rational doubt","quote":"I really liked Joe Rogan's video on this topic","text":"I really liked Joe Rogan's video on this topic, titled \"Joe Rogan on Why he changed his stance on the Moon landing conspiracy\". [1] The short, short version is that some training images got doctored and passed off as real space images, which gave people reason to doubt more substantial claims of having traveled to the moon. It was the first time a skeptics disbelief in our having visited the moon seemed like a rational stance to me. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43239539,"author":"Mountain_Skies","month":"2025-03","story":"Firefly ‘Blue Ghost’ lunar lander touches down on the moon","stance":"reference","why":"Rogan explains why he dropped the moon landing hoax belief","quote":"Rogan hasn't believed that for nearly a decade because he learned more about the physics of the Moon","text":"Rogan hasn't believed that for nearly a decade because he learned more about the physics of the Moon and the engineering of the time. Since you have an interest in truth and honesty, I'm sure now that you've been informed you won't spread this misinformation anymore and will do some reflection on how you ended up holding this view for so long past when it no longer was true. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21334260,"author":"EpicEng","month":"2019-10","story":"Edward Snowden on The Joe Rogan Experience [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Rogan used to believe moon conspiracies and no longer does","quote":"He used to believe that, and now he doesn't.","text":">He is also into things like moon landing conspiracies and all that good stuff that generate a lot of internet traffic, but I think in a bit of a hypocritical way as I'm pretty sure he doesn't really believe in any of that stuff and just talks about it for the clicks. [VIDEO] He used to believe that, and now he doesn't."},{"id":19421887,"author":"zby","month":"2019-03","story":"The rise and fall of scientific authority, and how to bring it back","stance":"reference","why":"Source for theory that fake moon photos were press-officer stand-ins","quote":"Some moon landing photos (and maybe films) are really fake.","text":"There is now a general crisis of authority. Just recently I have learned one peculiar theory (from [VIDEO] ). Some moon landing photos (and maybe films) are really fake. There are people who believe that this is a proof that the whole moon landing was just staged - it is kind of natural to think like that, but a more probable theory is that it just proofs that a press officer did not have good material and just used what was available. Traditional media are kind of not very strict with this - I even have some personal experience about that, but people have much more romantic view on it and treat each photo or other material as fact and true. With the internet we have more to reconcile. I don't think this is bad - maybe media just needs to change. With some time we'll adapt. It is similar case with science, maybe it is not that perfect as we used to view it. It is very good at where the incentives are well aligned - but they are not always 'adequate': [LINK]"},{"id":16099466,"author":"viridian","month":"2018-01","story":"James Damore has filed a class action lawsuit against Google","stance":"reference","why":"Rogan explaining why he changed his stance on the moon landing","quote":"He has changed his stance, as seen here","text":"He denied it 11 years ago you mean, by that blog post. He has changed his stance, as seen here: [VIDEO] You should listen to his episode with Neil DeGrasse Tyson, they talk about the mindset of denialism quite a bit."}],"r":[["y3qfeoqErtY",1]]},"8moePxHpvok":{"wy":"Shared as a compact critique of how news framing breeds passivity and despair.","q":{"x":"we just watch sad news each day and it makes us think all that can be done is say \"oh dear","i":23127817,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":14722100,"author":"joosters","month":"2017-07","story":"All Watched Over by Machines of Loving Grace (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Short Curtis clip on news reporting and oh dearism","quote":"which are also worth watching, e.g. his talk about news reporting and \"oh dearism","text":"As well as the long multi-part documentaries, Adam Curtis wrote short five minute clips for the show NewsWipe, which are also worth watching, e.g. his talk about news reporting and \"oh dearism\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23127817,"author":"pizza","month":"2020-05","story":"Secessio plebis","stance":"reference","why":"Short Curtis video on how sad news breeds passivity","quote":"we just watch sad news each day and it makes us think all that can be done is say \"oh dear","text":"I have a sense part of that is the \"Oh Dearism\" - we just watch sad news each day and it makes us think all that can be done is say \"oh dear\" - reflected in this short video by Adam Curtis - [VIDEO] (also featuring Charlie Brooker, creator of Black Mirror)"},{"id":7185278,"author":"alecco","month":"2014-02","story":"Ask HN: What are pressing issues that will affect humanity this decade?","stance":"reference","why":"Adam Curtis on oh dearism in news","quote":"Adam Curtis on \"Oh Dear\"-ism","text":"1) Drought, expensive food, lack of freshwater (e.g. California, Nevada, Pakistan) NASA video on groundwater reserves [LINK] 2) Big Data takeover by government (citizen profiling) and private sector (financial and health discrimination) 3) Youth-based uprisings changing the political landscape (driven by unemployment) But I think all these issues will play a wake-up call. Societies will become less self-centered, with less nationalism, less exceptionalism, and with more involvement and cooperation. There is already a rise in positive social activism. Kids are watching less TV and young people don't read newspapers, I love that! Lies are quickly demystified on places like Twitter. The negative impact will matter mostly on how early people decide to get involved. [LINK] [LINK] ($0.30/gallon in US, not viable) [VIDEO] Adam Curtis on \"Oh Dear\"-ism"}],"r":[["7kQk9-KLPfU",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1]]},"BzznBt8tVnI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an interesting explanation of how concert pitch ended up at exactly 440 Hz.","q":{"x":"Here's an interesting 12tone video explaining how we ended up at exactly that","i":29866716,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29866716,"author":"lillesvin","month":"2022-01","story":"Music Tuned to 440 Hz versus 432 Hz and the Health Effects","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how concert pitch ended up at exactly 440 Hz.","quote":"Here's an interesting 12tone video explaining how we ended up at exactly that","text":"Lots of people here mentioning that concert pitch wasn't always A = 440 Hz. Here's an interesting 12tone video explaining how we ended up at exactly that: [VIDEO] (Spoiler: It involves nothing less than the Treaty of Versailles.)"},{"id":26403757,"author":"Jap2-0","month":"2021-03","story":"ISO-8601 date format reference not publicly available","stance":"reference","why":"History of how A=440 became standard.","quote":"","text":"Putting aside that notes don't really have to be in tune to be notes, this is not where A=440hz originated. Correction: upon further research, this is where 440hz originated. However, it was not the original standard (or where musical notes come from), and I find the history interesting, so I'm going to post it anyway. Tuning used to be based off of whatever instrument was not easily tunable (the piano or organ), or some other instrument (oboe?) if there was none. For much of musical history tuning was significantly lower than A=440, albeit with a lot more variation; however, it tended to rise[0][1] in order to create a more brilliant-sounding timbre (particularly in larger venues), especially among instrumental pieces. This led to some contention, especially with vocalists who found the increasingly-high notes more and more difficult to sing. There were several efforts to readjust tuning to something more reasonable, but ultimately none of them worked for long. Frequency was actually orignically standardized in Article 282, section 22 of the Treaty of Versailles.[2] This may not make a lot of sense at initial glance, but when thinking about it as how good orchestras sound, it does make some sense as an economic policy This is where I originally messed up, though. The Treaty of Versailles references something else (which I don't have a link for), but which, per [1], standardizes to... 435hz. But again this crept up, and apparently the US and UK did some shenanigans with inter"},{"id":21992640,"author":"Tsiklon","month":"2020-01","story":"Loudness war","stance":"reference","why":"Laws defining concert A pitch.","quote":"laws were made to define what Concert A would be","text":"I remember watching a video by a music theorist on YouTube (12Tone) where he mentioned that laws were made to define what Concert A would be - [VIDEO]"}]},"CKh7UOELpPo":{"wy":"Pointed to as the original talk by the author of the SIMD-in-JavaScript proposal, whenever SIMD on the web comes up.","q":{"x":"Check \"Bringing SIMD to the Web via Dart\" by John McCutchan","i":7877674,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7877674,"author":"ahoge","month":"2014-06","story":"Intel Bringing SIMD to JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the author of the ECMAScript SIMD proposal","quote":"Check \"Bringing SIMD to the Web via Dart\" by John McCutchan","text":"Check \"Bringing SIMD to the Web via Dart\" by John McCutchan: [VIDEO] He's also the guy wrote the proposal for ECMAScript: [LINK] Also, using SIMD is way easier than using shaders. You just write something like this: double average (Float32x4List list) { var n = list.length; var sum = new Float32x4.zero(); for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { sum += list[i]; } var total = sum.x + sum.y + sum.z + sum.w; return total / (n * 4); } Instead of: double average (Float32List list) { var n = list.length; var sum = 0.0; for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { sum += list[i]; } return sum / n; }"},{"id":5559942,"author":"mythz","month":"2013-04","story":"Core libraries stabilize with Dart's new M4 release","stance":"reference","why":"Native SIMD in Dart VM for gaming and graphics","quote":"being able to use native SIMD instructions inside the Dart VM should help with gaming and other graphics intensive apps","text":"They've also published a new RayTracer OO benchmark showing the Dart VM far out performing V8: [LINK] Guessing being able to use native SIMD instructions inside the Dart VM should help with gaming and other graphics intensive apps: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5435624,"author":"michaelwww","month":"2013-03","story":"Why or why not Dart?","stance":"reference","why":"Dart VM SIMD support speeds up things like WebGL","quote":"SIMD - Single Instruction Multiple Data - Speeds up things like WebGL, added to Dart VM","text":"Why Dart? SIMD - Single Instruction Multiple Data - Speeds up things like WebGL, added to Dart VM, not available in JavaScript except through an experimental technique in FireFox [VIDEO]"}]},"DsVzKCk066g":{"wy":"Cited in threads on exercise and cognition as an accessible overview of the movement-brain connection.","q":{"x":"They talk about how complex movement in creatures typically correlates with the size of the brain.","i":15510847,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15510847,"author":"dkersten","month":"2017-10","story":"Training exercise boosts brain power, Johns Hopkins researchers say","stance":"rec","why":"Links movement to brain size and cognition","quote":"They talk about how complex movement in creatures typically correlates with the size of the brain.","text":"This video [VIDEO] and this book [LINK] suggest that it’s movement. They talk about how complex movement in creatures typically correlates with the size of the brain. Anecdotally, I’ve been doing the Cambridge Brain Sciences[1] tests every so often over the past few months to see how various things affect my score (mainly because I wanted to see which nootropics were most effective) and the best scores always occurred on days where I got exercise and the worst scores were in days where I didn’t and also got little sleep. My absolute best scores (99.5 percentile, although the next days scores were much, much lower and my average has been about 50-60ish) were on a day when I got approx 1.5 hours of brisk walking (throughout the day, not all at once), 8+ hours sleep the night before and I had been taking nootropics. I imagine diet also plays a part, but I’ve not yet experimented. I’m also not sure f sleep or exercise affects me most positive, but together they certainly make a huge difference. Nothing conclusive, for sure, but it does make me think that exercise/movement may play a big part. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":17549064,"author":"lylecubed","month":"2018-07","story":"Think everyone died young in ancient societies? Think again","stance":"reference","why":"Supports link between body and mind","quote":"The ancient greeks also believed there was a connection between improving the body and improving the mind.","text":"> And now a days I think it goes without saying that the average person exerts substantially less energy per day than even our luxurious Ancient Greeks did. Lives are much easier. That right there could be your reason. The human body was not designed to be stagnant. I read recently that the whole reason for the evolutionary development of brains in both animals and humans is to facilitate complex movement. Several studies done throughout the decades show a correlation between greater physical activity and better academic/learning results. The ancient greeks also believed there was a connection between improving the body and improving the mind. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16097863,"author":"Jemmeh","month":"2018-01","story":"Exercise Alters Our Microbiome","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on exercise benefits for learning via BDNF","quote":"","text":"Exercise has a lot of great benefits even though it's effect on weight loss is rather minimal. One of the biggest reasons I personally work out is for the ability to learn faster. Exercise gives you more Brain Derived Neurotrophic Factor (BDNF) which \"improves the function of neurons, encourages their growth, and strengthens and protects them against the process of cell death.\" \"WHY Exercise is so Underrated (Brain Power & Movement Link)\" [VIDEO] \"We found that vocabulary learning was 20 percent faster after intense physical exercise as compared to the other two conditions.\" [LINK]"}],"r":[["cidchWg74Y4",1],["dXdoim96v5A",1],["PY9DcIMGxMs",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1]]},"FY_NtO7SIrY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it for numbers on Facebook's moderation scale and for Destin's ability to get platforms to speak openly about manipulation.","q":{"x":"Destin of Smarter Every Day on YT went and talked to FB and their content mod team","i":20716388,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":23453670,"author":"xelxebar","month":"2020-06","story":"America is giving up on the pandemic?","stance":"rec","why":"Series on social platforms and manipulation, ending on hope","quote":"Destin is really good at conveying a message of hope.","text":"> no wonder people don’t believe in global warming, these “experts” prioritize politics over everything I read this as a clear expression of your frustration. Completely reasonable given the situation. Are you familiar with the YouTube channel \"Smarter Every Day\"? There's this three-part series that might offer a new perspective on the distrust and disillusionment you seem to be feeling: [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] In particular, the channel creator Destin Sandlin explores how YouTube, Twitter, and Facebook are being used as a sort of vanguard in a meta-game to undermine our trust in the social institutions we rely on. It's a pretty dystopian-sounding and bleak theme. However, Destin is really good at conveying a message of hope. Use your Critical Mind, of course, but also realize that there's a personal cost to adopting absolutist views like `\"experts\" prioritize politics over everything.'"},{"id":25011984,"author":"NoOneNew","month":"2020-11","story":"YouTube's Fake Animal Rescue Ring [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Platforms acknowledge manipulation in interviews","quote":"Smarter Everyday interviewed all 3 and their folks talk about this directly.","text":"Yea, that's well known. Not just from federal law enforcement with proof of it, but twitter, youtube and facebook know it and admit to it too. Smarter Everyday interviewed all 3 and their folks talk about this directly. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] The actual social media platforms saying it's happening. At the same time though, it's not all their fault. It takes two to tango and if you're gullible enough to wreck your own life on the words of someone you've never, and will never meet... that's a special kind of stupid. Here's my hope. All of this gets out of our systems. After whoever wins this election, folks are going to go, \"Hold on, we're all acting like crazy people. Let's be sensible and stop going to the extremes and work together.\" This is the fantasy that fills my dreams. Not flying, being rich or anything like that. That, society being sensible and taking some personal responsibility. That's my crazy, wild fantasy..."},{"id":20716388,"author":"Balgair","month":"2019-08","story":"YouTube’s LGBTQ Problem Is a Business-Model Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Destin interviews Facebook's team about moderation staffing","quote":"Destin of Smarter Every Day on YT went and talked to FB and their content mod team","text":"> However, doing that really changes the economics of the platform Getting profit-loss data on Youtube is very difficult. If they are profitable, it can't be by a large amount. So, if the plan is to have a LOT of real people just trying to monitor YT all day long it will really gut YT's economics, not just change it. Destin of Smarter Every Day on YT went and talked to FB and their content mod team a few months back [0]. He talked with Rob Leathern of FB's Cyber Security Department. Rob mentiond that they now use ~30k real people to monitor FB. Soon thereafter, The Verge released a scoop on the horrific conditions these 30k people must endure [1] in that job. YT would likely have to have a proportinate number of people in similar conditions as well, or likely scale up to make things less worse and then have to pay more people. YT simply cannot possibly afford to have enough people moderate YT. Likely, this version of YT will become something of a blip in history. All this investment will likely not pan out as the economics of video platforms like YT cannot work as they are today. The real question is what is the subscription amount that does make YT work the way we want it to work? [0] [VIDEO] Also, watch Destin's body language with Nate Gleischer (~14:30 mark). It's so strange. Something wierd happened in that interview with Nate. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":19919933,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: Why not rethink Web Identity with browser sync and push?","stance":"reference","why":"Facebook's moderation scale, about 30k reviewers","quote":"FB are now deleting ~1m accounts/dy, employing over 30k reviewers","text":"I'd suggest thinking through the Usenet model. What it was, what worked, what did not. And the design and flaws of HTTP basic auth. Keep in mind that FB are now deleting ~1m accounts/dy, employing over 30k reviewers. [VIDEO] (at about 10m50s)."}],"r":[["V-1RhQ1uuQ4",1],["R7EEoWg6Ekk",1],["v7Mt0GYHU9A",1],["1PGm8LslEb4",1],["z8MVKianh54",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1]]},"KZokQov_aH0":{"wy":"HN uses it mainly as a critical review of the game, saying it captures a lack of respect for the player, though one commenter suggests watching it after finishing.","q":{"x":"I think this was a fair critique of the Witness and why it lacks respect for the player","i":30943045,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30449568,"author":"Jach","month":"2022-02","story":"A Shader Trick","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended to watch after finishing the game.","quote":"When you're done playing, I recommend watching this video","text":"When you're done playing, I recommend watching this video: [VIDEO] Games are part art, what's more to understand? There's some fun image out there I can't exactly recall, but it's basically of an interview with some game devs, and one of them saying something like \"We wanted to have a pot with flowers on the table here, and when you come back to this room later, the color of the flowers changes. No gameplay consequences. But we cut it for time. Why did we want it? Because we thought it'd be cool.\" But in a lot of games there's tons of stuff like that which doesn't get cut. Gamedev is full of those \"because it'd be cool\" or other vague artistic reasons (as opposed to business reasons or researched game testing reasons) for something to be there, or something to be polished well beyond reason."},{"id":30957300,"author":"passion__desire","month":"2022-04","story":"Thoughts on the Witness (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I loved the interpretation videos out there on Witness. It's like an experimental style Alan Watts video. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46311479,"author":"dsego","month":"2025-12","story":"Jonathan Blow has spent the past decade designing 1,400 puzzles","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There is good review of the game on youtube by the title","text":"How do you compare it to the Portal games or the Talos principle? I find those superior in puzzle mechanics, sense of achievement and playing dynamics. They can be challenging but you never feel aimlessly going around without a purpose like the Witness. There is good review of the game on youtube by the title \"The Witness - A Great Game That You Shouldn't Play\", it covers a lot and resonates well with my experience, the panels could've been a standalone mobile/tablet game. Everything else in the game is beautiful but frustrating. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31621529,"author":"gravypod","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Non-violent video games with great stories?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I think the critiques in this video [0] sum up my main feelings about the game.","text":"> \"attempting to impress by affecting greater importance, talent, culture, etc., than is actually possessed\" I think the critiques in this video [0] sum up my main feelings about the game. The puzzles are fantastic. The <spoiler> puzzles are fantastic. The movement, environment, graphics, etc are all fantastic. But, the \"story\" and \"message\" of the game can be interpreted in many ways. It is art in the sense that everyone gets something different from it. But it is pretentious because, if everyone gets something completely different from your \"experience\", you really didn't convey anything. That's what most people (I think) are talking about when they say \"<art> is pretentious\". [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30943045,"author":"dsego","month":"2022-04","story":"Thoughts on the Witness (2016)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I think this was a fair critique of the Witness and why it lacks respect for the player","text":"Had high expectations for this game, anticipating with great excitement. I loved Braid, was following all the blog posts and videos with great admiration. And the game wasn't that bad, it was interesting for a while, but also frustrating. Talos on the other hand was a revelation, a thoroughly fun and enjoyable experience, very similar to Portal games. I think this was a fair critique of the Witness and why it lacks respect for the player: The Witness - A Great Game That You Shouldn't Play [VIDEO]"},{"id":26077459,"author":"bmn__","month":"2021-02","story":"CD Projekt Red has been subject to a cyber attack and ransom demand","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> he believes time is valuable and should be respected That's what he says , but not what he does . [VIDEO] Haven't experienced a bigger author disconnect in gaming in the decade."}],"r":[["P76Vbsk_3J0",1],["dCuZkaaou0Q",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["FjHJ7FmV0M4",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1]]},"LikxFZZO2sk":{"wy":"HN cites it as a striking demonstration of how far humanoid robot agility had come.","q":{"x":"Their latest video of Atlas doing parkour is just insane","i":18236105,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18236105,"author":"elteto","month":"2018-10","story":"Boston Dynamics: UpTown Spot","stance":"rec","why":"Atlas robot performing parkour","quote":"Their latest video of Atlas doing parkour is just insane","text":"I still can't understand why Google sold Boston Dynamics, it baffles me. These guys have been pushing the boundaries of robotic motion for years. I see it as another example of Google's inability to focus on anything that is not core services. Their latest video of Atlas doing parkour is just insane [0]. You'd think that with Google's focus on AI they'd want to own and keep under a tight lid tech as impressive as BD's. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28211418,"author":"shadowgovt","month":"2021-08","story":"Atlas robot does parkour","stance":"reference","why":"Atlas progress over two years","quote":"","text":"It's interesting to see how this has progressed from their video two years ago [ [VIDEO] ] and one year ago [ [LINK] ]"},{"id":23487209,"author":"PeterisP","month":"2020-06","story":"How much did AlphaGo Zero cost? (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Bipedal walking and running on uneven terrain has been demonstrated years ago - Boston Dynamics has a lot of nice videos; e.g. here's a four year old video on walking [LINK] , here's two year old videos on running [LINK] and [VIDEO] - perhaps it's debatable if it's \"full human level\" but it seems sufficient to me for most purposes. IMHO any improvements to that would be mostly for sensors (seeing what terrain is there even in poor conditions e.g. fog/rain/snow) and modelling environment (understanding which objects might break or slide if you step on them), not walking/running as such. The main problem for bipedal robots that makes them still impractical is the hardware expense (wheels are simpler and cheaper) and the power supply required, so for most use cases it's more efficient to use something other than a bipedal robot and there's limited business application and future revenue in scaling up research demos of bipedal walking to practicality, so most people who are working on walking algorithms are doing so in simulated virtual environments (where we have algorithms that can learn walking and running \"from scratch\" through experimentation) and not building very expensive hardware."},{"id":21155653,"author":"icebraining","month":"2019-10","story":"Paralysed man moves in mind-reading exoskeleton","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"While full control is obviously better for some things, I wonder if overall this isn't better. It would reduce the training from having to adapt your brain to control a foreign body, to just issuing simpler commands, and might be safer too. Imagine using Atlas[1] as the exoskeleton, you just think about going forward and it navigates the terrain for you. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AdKNCBrkZQ4",1],["v3pYRn5j7oI",1]]},"QdVEez20X_s":{"wy":"Cited as a short, fun introduction to the phenomenology of music and as an explanation and critique of vaporwave.","q":{"x":"The following fun, short talk does a great job of introducing the concept of through its analysis of \"vaporwave.","i":13080245,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13080245,"author":"diydsp","month":"2016-12","story":"Artificial Intelligence Generates Christmas Song from Holiday Image","stance":"rec","why":"Short talk introducing the phenomenology of music through vaporwave analysis.","quote":"The following fun, short talk does a great job of introducing the concept of through its analysis of \"vaporwave.","text":"Auto-synthesis of music has been a topic of academic interest since the 1950s, when the first mainframe scribbled out code on paper tape to be translated into sheet music and performed. UToronto's work here is the latest expression of this desire. The huge gap between our cultures' actual music and these synthetic projects can to an extent be described through \"receptivity\" or the phenomenology of music, in other words, how it's experienced. The following fun, short talk does a great job of introducing the concept of through its analysis of \"vaporwave.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":43707114,"author":"amiga386","month":"2025-04","story":"Attention K-Mart Shoppers","stance":"reference","why":"Explains and critiques vaporwave with a parody track.","quote":"Adam Neely not only explains and critiques Vaporwave but takes a big steaming dump on it","text":"From \"The music theory of V A P O R W A V E\" where Adam Neely not only explains and critiques Vaporwave but takes a big steaming dump on it by creating the track that captures and parodies its entire aesthetic. [VIDEO] He got his source material directly from this archive.org collection, as it says in the opening titles: \"On October 2, 2015, Mark Davis posted his prized collection of digitized K-mart elevator music cassette tapes to archive.org, free for anybody to use. Vaporwave producers rejoiced.\""},{"id":41198821,"author":"romwell","month":"2024-08","story":"Andy Warhol's lost Amiga art found","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of vaporwave as music.","quote":"","text":">I've re-read this paragraph like twenty times and I'm still not sure what to make of it. Re-read what it was written in response to. You argued a lot that something like Black Square has little \"artistic merit\" because it is very easy to re-create, and someone could do it by accident. Let me pose a question: if so, why hasn't there been a painting like that in the 19th century? 18th? Or at any time over the millenia that the humanity has produced paintings in rectangular frames? Black, after all, is the simplest, most easily available color. Another line of thought for you to consider. You might have heard of Vaporwave[1], which is a musical[2] as well as visual art aesthetic/movement. I would urge you to read [1], as it will help you understand the answers to your questions, then come back here. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["z71h9XZbAWY",1],["DnPmg0R1M04",1]]},"W07ZpEv9Sog":{"wy":"HN points to it in threads about physical security and tamper-evident seals as an informative look at how easily they are bypassed.","q":{"x":"is interesting to watch","i":10196234,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10196234,"author":"sllabres","month":"2015-09","story":"TSA Master Keys","stance":"rec","why":"DEF CON intro to tamper-evident devices and how easily they are bypassed.","quote":"is interesting to watch","text":"unfortunately several of these professional seals are only a bit more obscure, but not much harder to open than a zip tie. Introduction to Tamper Evident Devices from datagram at defcon 19 [VIDEO] is interesting to watch"},{"id":37963509,"author":"OmarAssadi","month":"2023-10","story":"How to mitigate the Hetzner/Linode XMPP.ru MitM interception incident","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in discussion of tamper-evident seals on hardware.","quote":"","text":"Assuming this was done at the government's request, I assume Hetzner is more than willing to comply with a court order mandating they allow them to monitor and physically size a machine. And outside of nation-state requests, even ignoring the fact that someone could probably pay-off an employee, I think ease would depend a lot on the datacenter and target; judging by the awesome and hilarious story behind the Fremont Cabal accidentally becoming an internet exchange by essentially having some dude barely secretly slipping unauthorized cables into the raceways [1], I figure there are a lot of places where if your target is simply renting a couple rack units or single rack rather than an entire locked cage, you can probably get physical access by doing the same. Also, a lot of Deviant Ollam's stories about industrial security and the dozens of ways he's broken into utility companies, server rooms, etc — mostly just by being confident, looking the part, bad doors [2], and badge cloning [3] — don't give me a ton of confidence that someone with skill couldn't feasibly either get direct access to servers they shouldn't, or at the very least, access to an important part of the supply chain for their target. And speaking of supply chain, my processor died recently, so I ordered a brand-new in box replacement Ryzen, and when it arrived last night, out of curiosity, I wanted to see if I could get the CPU out of the box without breaking the tamper-evident authenticity seal... ... and abo"},{"id":31906543,"author":"capableweb","month":"2022-06","story":"Detecting unauthorized physical access with beans, lentils and colored rice (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"DEF CON tamper-evident contest where all devices were defeated.","quote":"Like the computer world, many of these devices are overmarketed","text":"> A company makes security seals for this very purpose and markets them under the name \"TamperTell\". They have a serial number on them and a matching receipt tab that you remove before flight then check against on arrival. I wouldn't put too much trust into the marketing of products like this. Time and time again they fail to protect against the most basic of \"lockpicking\", and unless I've seen multiple skilled people failing at unlocking the \"TamperTell\", I'd put it in the same bucket as the rest. > DEF CON 18 (2010) held the first ever \"Tamper Evident\" contest, where contestants were given a box sealed with a variety of tamper evident devices, many of which purport to be \"tamper proof.\" All of these devices were defeated, even by those with little experience and a limited toolkit. Like the computer world, many of these devices are overmarketed [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["f0gMdGrVteI",1],["5Ge2RcvDlgw",1],["ZMQbHMgK2rw",1]]},"Xe1a1wHxTyo":{"wy":"HN mostly posts it as a joke whenever someone says things were harder in the old days.","m":[{"id":38179789,"author":"DougN7","month":"2023-11","story":"Parkinson's patient able to walk 6km without problems after spinal implant","stance":"rec","why":"Classic sketch of one-upping hardship stories.","quote":"And for anyone that didn’t get it, this video will make your day","text":"LOL - didn’t expect that reference, but it’s perfect :) And for anyone that didn’t get it, this video will make your day [VIDEO]"},{"id":13925267,"author":"dboreham","month":"2017-03","story":"The Biggest Difference Between Coding Today and When I Started in the 80s","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I would cite this sketch whenever someone talked about how good coding used to be back in the day","text":"Back in the 80s I would cite this sketch whenever someone talked about how good coding used to be back in the day: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11920393,"author":"kirubakaran","month":"2016-06","story":"The illegal city of Somerville","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wow $2200 for a 2 bed? Luxury! That's how much you'll pay for a studio here in Mission, SF... if you're lucky. (and I wish this were a Four Yorkshiremen reference[1]. No!) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11148096,"author":"Mandatum","month":"2016-02","story":"An Open Letter to Millennials Like Talia…","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sorry, had to throw a Monty Python reference there","text":"Fourteen hour shifts? Fourteen hour shifts!? In my day we'd work from 1AM until 3AM the following morning, go to sleep for -2 hours, wake up and do it all over again! We were paid in 19th century New Zealand shillings and had to pay all of it back for living on the boss's premises! (Sorry, had to throw a Monty Python reference there: [VIDEO] ) I don't believe just because something was done one way before, means it should affect how things are now. If we believe the way \"something is\" is fundamentally wrong - just because it has been that way (or worse) should not provide be a valid argument against said \"something\". Kind of fits in to: [LINK]"},{"id":10538051,"author":"RobertoG","month":"2015-11","story":"Young People Are Happier Than They Used to Be","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Younger generations are always flawed. It's a traditional thing. In order to encourage you, I recommend this classic report about the issue: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4584471,"author":"confluence","month":"2012-09","story":"The Programmers Before Us Were Better","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Monty Python made fun of this \"in my time, you see\" bullshit with the Four Yorkshiremen skit","text":"That's complete crap and merely a manifestation of \"Rosy Retrospection\" ( [LINK] ). Monty Python made fun of this \"in my time, you see\" bullshit with the Four Yorkshiremen skit ( [VIDEO] ). Woody Allen's movie Midnight in Paris did the same ( [LINK] ). The programmers before us were the exact same as the programmers of today and they will be the exact same as the one's that come after us. The only difference between them is whose shoulders they stood upon and what they were be able to do using the tools at hand. Programmers of the 60s were happy just to get things to add up. The guys in the 80s squealed with glee at GUIs. The guys in the 90s made the internet what it is today. And the guys of the 2010s will bring AI and robotics into the main stream. Everyone glues things together - it's how creation works ( [LINK] ). The only difference between generations is the things you have available to glue together. Sometimes you glue together a stick and a rock to make a spear, and other times you glue together a machine gun, a camera, and a computer. Either way - same people, different situations."},{"id":3756337,"author":"huxley","month":"2012-03","story":"Ability to learn is affected by the timing of sleep","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"see: the Four Yorkshiremen","text":"Don't be afraid to discount some of those stories as bravado and self-aggrandizing reports. (see: the Four Yorkshiremen [VIDEO] )"},{"id":2096332,"author":"jdee","month":"2011-01","story":"For what are the Windows A:\\ and B:\\ drives used?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Luxury! I used to have to snip endless chads out of punched cards to represent the very zeroes and ones of the program i was writing! Thanks to [VIDEO]"},{"id":2047253,"author":"iwwr","month":"2010-12","story":"Redditors share the way the world was 20 years ago","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Off topic, but obligatory Monty Python piece","text":"If you wanted to know something, there was no Google or Wikipedia. You might be able to find out a basic fact if you had a set of encyclopedias. But most information, from important stuff to basic trivia (\"who was in that movie?\") was not available unless you had a reference book or went to the library and really searched. Rumors, lies, media manipulation, thwarted by a few seconds of research. If more people bothered, it would be a better world. Off topic, but obligatory Monty Python piece [VIDEO]"},{"id":1713674,"author":"erikstarck","month":"2010-09","story":"Being Poor by John Scalzi","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm sorry, I know this is a serious subject but this thread reminds me of this Monty Python sketch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1015356,"author":"DanielBMarkham","month":"2009-12","story":"Every ISP Is Trying to Scare You Into Paying for Internet You Don't Need","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"3 baud? Luxury. We had 1 baud and we kept it in a box in the middle of the road. Every day we'd get up, clean the box, read the bit, and then go to the factory to work for 20 hours. And when we got home every night our father would cut us with a knife. [VIDEO] Merry Christmas!"},{"id":47305,"author":"mynameishere","month":"2007-08","story":"do not. I am just letting it out.","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm strongly urged to one-up you (because I could, believe me) but what's the point? You're either going to A) Buck up, or B) Get some Xanax. That's about it. [VIDEO]"}]},"YE8MVNMzpbo":{"wy":"HN points to it as the explanation of how The Witness solved walking and collision.","q":{"x":"talking about the problem and how they solved it in The Witness","i":28439188,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28439188,"author":"db48x","month":"2021-09","story":"Why video game doors are so hard to get right [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Developer explains how The Witness solved walking and collision","quote":"talking about the problem and how they solved it in The Witness","text":"[VIDEO] Here’s a game developer talking about the problem and how they solved it in The Witness, but note well the limitations he describes. It’s not directly useful if the player can jump, for instance."},{"id":44615777,"author":"wasmperson","month":"2025-07","story":"The Big Oops: Anatomy of a Thirty-Five-Year Mistake [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Casey explains The Witness movement vs engine limitations","quote":"There's a talk that Casey gives where he explains how he implemented the movement system for The Witness","text":"A custom engine is never _needed_ technically speaking since COTS engines are often customizable to the point where you can do anything you want with them. That doesn't mean that they don't influence the design of games, though. There's a talk that Casey gives where he explains how he implemented the movement system for The Witness, in which he shows examples of Unity-based \"walking simulator\"-type games dealing with limitations of the engine in ways that The Witness was able to totally avoid. This allowed the game's artists to be creative with set design without worrying as much about performance issues or collision bugs, thus potentially opening up more design space. [VIDEO] Here's some praise of The Witness by Fabien Giesen: > That’s where I am right now. I have never seen another game as cohesive as this. Not even close. Without getting into specifics or spoiler territory, I have never seen a game with such manifest intention behind every single detail, nor one where all the details cohere this well into a whole. This goes for the design and gameplay itself, but also across traditional hard boundaries. Game design and art play off each other, and both of these are tightly coupled with some of the engine tech in the game. It’s amazing. There is no single detail in the game that would be hard to accomplish by itself, but the whole is much more than the sum of its parts, with no signs of the endless compromise that are normally a reality in every project. [LINK]"},{"id":42947065,"author":"sunrunner","month":"2025-02","story":"Splitting from Handmade Network","stance":"reference","why":"Passing mention among discussion of Casey Muratori","quote":"","text":"I feel the same about Casey and his work, and like another commenter said, his simple, practical and honest approach to his work is refreshing in an era of languages, environments and tools that feel bloated, slow and ineffective (IMHO). > That being said the whole network idea, and the fetishisation of his particular coding philosophy was always cringe. And I agree with this too. The point of Handmade Hero to me was always to show that the complex software systems that you interact with can be understood despite their size and complexity, and that it's still possible (though perhaps in a lot of cases impractical) to recreate these from the ground up. Also that there's tremendous value in understanding how something works from first principles (see also Casey's 'How to Open a Black Box' lecture, [LINK] ). Having said that, while I feel it's important to aspire to some level of simplicity and solving-the-actual-problem that Casey's coding style embodies, it also feels like an approach that works best only when a) the number of people working on a single program is relatively small and b) the expertise is deep and so the need for abstractions is low or non-existent. With Casey's background in game tools it makes a lot of sense, but this approach doesn't seem like it scales up, and he's essentially the only contributor to Handmade Hero too. > It took him and his team 8 years of dedicated work to make The Witness, and it's just not a very technically impressive game. I'm a big fa"}],"r":[["aeWyp2vXxqA",1],["FlQYU3m1e80",1],["wymmCdLdPvM",1],["c33AZBnRHks",1],["6JwEYamjXpA",1]]},"bBx2Y5HhplI":{"wy":"Cited in debates about taxes, job creation and wealth redistribution, and for the controversy around its release.","q":{"x":"You really need to watch","i":14187844,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14187844,"author":"dsparkman","month":"2017-04","story":"Trump Wants Tax Plan to Cut Corporate Rate to 15%","stance":"rec","why":"Challenges the claim that lower corporate taxes create jobs.","quote":"You really need to watch","text":"You really need to watch [VIDEO] if you think that because corporations have to pay less in taxes that they will suddenly spend more on R&D and jobs."},{"id":22535999,"author":"eliomattia","month":"2020-03","story":"Pay/receive Universal Basic Income without middlemen","stance":"reference","why":"Example of wealthy people wanting to contribute to redistribution.","quote":"","text":"Interesting! Government(-backed) UBI is the ultimate goal. But how to make it happen? First, why has it not happened already? All the data indicate that it's good. A no-brainer, then? Well, - It's expensive - Governments vest financial power in businesses, not in themselves. End results? 1) A very oiled mechanism for income concentration, no questions asked 2) No mechanism for income distribution (!) 3) Blaming the government for not providing UBI already 4) No UBI, be it voluntary or government 5) Many people, who in fact want to contribute towards it, cannot. Why not start by creating that mechanism for income distribution, then? A quote about Sam Altman[1]: «Altman suggests researching Universal Basic Income. (Y Combinator has already begun work on a UBI project in Oakland.) “We should set a goal of eliminating poverty in the country,” Altman writes on his website. “I’m not yet sure what a reasonable timeframe for this goal is, but I do feel a moral obligation to figure out how to do it.” Unions aren’t working, Altman says, and wages are stagnant, so we need something better, even though he isn’t sure what that is yet.» Some examples of people who would like to contribute, yet cannot: - Nick Hanauer[2,3] - Letter for a wealth tax[4], not exactly on income but on wealth, yet indicative of the supply - The Giving Pledge[5] - Yusaku Maezawa[6] - Andrew Yang[7,8] has just brought together donors who are pledging millions for UBI - Patriotic Millionaires[9] - The World Economic"},{"id":11130381,"author":"spdy","month":"2016-02","story":"Bernie’s Army of Coders","stance":"reference","why":"Hanauer's TED talk on inequality.","quote":"","text":"He is not radical or left when seen from an EU view and with 20 years experience on Capitol Hill he knows exactly how the game is played. So he is able to get stuff done i have no doubt about that. Two of his main agenda points are no-brainers: - Medicare for all - Tuition Free College And he is right with his assessment that the rising inequality is not a good thing for our future. Two Youtube videos: [LINK] Talk with Bernie Sanders in 2014 where he goes on what it takes to change this system. [VIDEO] Ted Talk from a Nick Hanauer on inequality"},{"id":3988792,"author":"alecco","month":"2012-05","story":"TED and inequality: The real story","stance":"reference","why":"Released video of Hanauer's censored TED talk.","quote":"","text":"Released video. [VIDEO] \"This idea is an oracle of faith for republicans and seldom challenged by democrats.\" Clearly against bi-partisan and not pro-democrat. Very bad move by TED. Also, the quality of many TED presentations is a joke so they can't pull that card."},{"id":3989164,"author":"thaumaturgy","month":"2012-05","story":"TED and inequality: The real story","stance":"reference","why":"Short talk analysed point by point.","quote":"","text":"I watched the video after reading TED's response, and I'm skeptical of some of their claims, but I can also see why they didn't initially choose to publish it on their site. The video is at [VIDEO] Since it's short, I'll go over it point-by-point. 1. \"It is astounding how significantly one idea can shape a society and its policies. Consider this one: if taxes on the rich go up, job creation will go down. This idea is an article of faith for Republicans, and seldom challenged by Democrats, and has indeed shaped much of the economic landscape.\" The first line of his talk is a bit fluffy, but I don't see anything else here that isn't objectively true. Supply-side economics and increased benefits for the rich are a major part of the Republican platform, and because this idea has been accepted as true by society at large, it is not being directly challenged by the Democrats. (Who, arguably, want just as much to protect the interests of the rich & powerful as the Republicans do.) 2. \"But sometimes the ideas that we are certain are true, are dead wrong. Consider that for thousands of years, humans believed that the Earth was the center of the universe. It's not, and an astronomer who still believed that it was, would do some pretty terrible astronomy. Likewise, a policymaker who believes that the rich are job creators and therefore should not be taxed, will do equally terrible policy.\" This is just bad rhetoric and bad logic, IMO. I get the point he's trying to make, but he's not ma"}]},"hWTFG3J1CP8":{"wy":"HN posts it as a clever, catchy musical joke whenever Tetris or Soviet history comes up.","q":{"x":"Besides being \"meta\", it's also clever and kinda catchy.","i":3308067,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":3308067,"author":"dextorious","month":"2011-12","story":"EA relaunches Tetris on iOS with a $30/year subscription (really)","stance":"rec","why":"Clever, catchy history of USSR to Tetris tune","quote":"Besides being \"meta\", it's also clever and kinda catchy.","text":"There is a song that uses the concept of Tetris, and the folk tune used in the game, in order to tell the \"complete history in the soviet union\". Besides being \"meta\", it's also clever and kinda catchy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16306095,"author":"my_first_acct","month":"2018-02","story":"The Story of Tetris [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"a 6-minute video from 2010","text":"Not completely off-topic, a 6-minute video from 2010: Complete History Of The Soviet Union, Arranged To The Melody Of Tetris [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27065268,"author":"neffy","month":"2021-05","story":"Hatetris – Tetris which always gives you the worst piece","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Well here is the soundtrack...","text":"Well here is the soundtrack... [VIDEO] Compete History of the Soviet Union, arranged to the music of Tetris. Pigwiththefaceofaboy."},{"id":23066715,"author":"chrisma0","month":"2020-05","story":"Tetris: The Soviet 'mind game' that took over the world (2019)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"immediately starts playing in my head","text":"Whenever I read about Tetris, the \"Complete History Of The Soviet Union, Arranged To The Melody Of Tetris\" immediately starts playing in my head: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20815960,"author":"squirrelicus","month":"2019-08","story":"What are children dying from and what can we do about it?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"sings along \"I've got plenty of gold, and I'm standing in line for a loaf of bread...","text":"That is so catastrophically false I can't even. Capitalism has empirically been, by several orders of magnitude, the most powerful force in the world for eradication of poverty. But hey, when you define poverty by relative income levels instead of absolute levels of depravation, you can say what you said. But you are forgetting the tragic suffering of the historical poor and those under socialism, feudalism, and pretty much all of history for all time. Today's $10k/yr poor live way, way, way better than the hanseatic merchants of yore. There is no enforced policy that keeps people down. People just look far down from above, even if the tide is actually, in reality, lifting all extraordinarily rapidly. Edit: sings along \"I've got plenty of gold, and I'm standing in line for a loaf of bread...\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":18220060,"author":"bitwize","month":"2018-10","story":"We Need More Pessimistic Games","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Tetris I work so hard In arranging the blocks","text":"> Tetris I work so hard In arranging the blocks But each night I come home To my wife in tears. What's the point of it all When you're building a wall And in front of your eyes It disappears? Source: [VIDEO]"}]},"iSSRaIU9_Vc":{"wy":"HN people cite it as a memorable example of a clever low-cost hardware hack and as a reason to admire Kamkar's work.","q":{"x":"Here is a video where he demonstrates how to open garage doors with a wireless Mattel toy","i":11689803,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11689803,"author":"pizza","month":"2016-05","story":"Ask HN: Which hackers do you look up to the most?","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates opening garage doors with a wireless Mattel toy.","quote":"Here is a video where he demonstrates how to open garage doors with a wireless Mattel toy","text":"Samy Kamkar! Here is a video where he demonstrates how to open garage doors with a wireless Mattel toy: [VIDEO] He also wrote that one XSS worm for myspace several years ago. Hi Samy, in case you happen to read this ;)"},{"id":18610978,"author":"rmetzler","month":"2018-12","story":"Thieves boosting signal from key fobs inside homes to steal vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Reminded of OpenSesame hack on garage openers.","quote":"Samy Kamkar's OpenSesame Hack [0]","text":"I just read \"Garage opener\" and automatically I have to think about Samy Kamkar's OpenSesame Hack [0]. Regarding the keyfobs, I've seen demonstrations on video where the RF signal was relayed. I can search for them, but they were in German. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14032500,"author":"feld","month":"2017-04","story":"iPhone App will not stay open - just flashes when trying to launch","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as threat example of toy brute-forcing neighborhood garage doors.","quote":"Or they could get a kids toy and reprogram it to brute force all the garage doors in the neighborhood.","text":"I'm using their product. Let me tell my story. I have an old garage door opener -- ancient Craftsman. It doesn't even have a secure RF opener; no rollover codes or faux-crypto. My wife lost her garage door opener remote. I looked for replacements and couldn't find any that were less than $40 new or $30 used. Ridiculous. I have an outdoor keypad on the garage, which is detached. It doesn't work very well in the extreme cold because the 9V battery gives out. This solves a unique problem for me: I do not want to buy a new garage door opener. Mine was recently rebuilt with new gears and should be good to go for years. Further, would be a few hundred dollars without labor, and I don't want to do the labor myself. I've done it before; not worth my time. Now, Garadget exists. I got mine for $69. Now I have infinite remotes as long as my wifi works (It's on my IoT VLAN which only can do outbound 80 and 443). So, Garadget solved a unique problem for me in 5 minutes of work and saved me money. Hilarious that a device this advanced is cheaper than a simple RF remote, but that's supply and demand in action. Now is this a security concern for me? No. If someone wants to break into my garage they can eBay some garage door keys and just disengage the garage door from the opener via the keyhole on the front of every modern garage door. Or pick it I guess? Or they could get a kids toy and reprogram it to brute force all the garage doors in the neighborhood. [VIDEO] tl;dr while this is the Int"}],"r":[["oRLON3ddZIw",1]]},"jtMWEiCdsfc":{"wy":"Cited as nostalgic period documentation of the early UK microcomputer era, with commenters fondly remembering the series and the BBC Micro.","q":{"x":"A very good look at changing markets.","i":10732935,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10732935,"author":"brudgers","month":"2015-12","story":"The Computer Programme and Micro Live","stance":"rec","why":"Episode list of the BBC Computer Programme; shows changing markets.","quote":"A very good look at changing markets.","text":"Episodes: 1. It's Happening Now: [VIDEO] 2. Getting Down to Basic: [LINK] 3. Micro Live [with visit to Alan Turing play]: [LINK] 4. Clive Sinclair and Alan Sugar: [LINK] A very good look at changing markets. 5. -- Compilation 6. Blocked by YouTube."},{"id":45036892,"author":"AlbertoGP","month":"2025-08","story":"The man with a Home Computer (1967) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Episode 1 of the 1982 BBC series.","quote":"","text":"That man, Rex Malik, participated in (among other things) the 1982 BBC series “The Computer Programme” ( [LINK] ), typically in a small section at the end on an episode but also as narrator in other parts and is credited as “Programme Adviser”: Episode 1 - “It’s Happening Now”: [VIDEO] Episode 4 - “It’s on the Computer”: [LINK] Episode 5 - “The New Media“: [LINK] Episode 10 - “Things to Come”: [LINK]"},{"id":7281869,"author":"dtf","month":"2014-02","story":"Mac and the Micro – memories of Ian McNaught-Davis","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I loved that programme and I loved my Model B.","text":"I loved that programme and I loved my Model B. Serle & Mac were so easy to watch. It was on BBC2 (then so were a bunch of great early morning Open University programmes on subjects that were way over my young head). [VIDEO] Wasn't there also a weekly Radio 4 computer programme where they'd transmit a program that you could record on tape and then load onto your computer?"}],"r":[["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["CZIINXhGDcs",1],["dljNabciEGg",1],["Tl9kD693ie4",1]]},"mxDSiwqM2nw":{"wy":"Posted in F-35 threads as an insider's critique from a legendary aircraft designer.","q":{"x":"this entertaining video of A-10 designer Pierre Sprey talking about how the new F-35 is garbage","i":7357006,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7357006,"author":"frou_dh","month":"2014-03","story":"Dark spot under cockpit of A-10s","stance":"rec","why":"A-10 designer Sprey on F-35 being garbage.","quote":"this entertaining video of A-10 designer Pierre Sprey talking about how the new F-35 is garbage","text":"I don't know how accurate it is, but I remember this entertaining video of A-10 designer Pierre Sprey talking about how the new F-35 is garbage: [VIDEO] Themes of bloat and unwarranted complexity are perhaps universal."},{"id":9315326,"author":"skeuomorf","month":"2015-04","story":"The F-35's $400k helmet","stance":"reference","why":"Sprey's opinion that F-35 is a failure.","quote":"here's Pierre Sprey's opinion on it (Designer of the F-16)","text":"While the technology in this helmet may turn out to be pretty great, I still can't view the project as a whole (The F-35) as more than a failure. It's taken them a lot of time and money to develop a mediocre aircraft, here's Pierre Sprey's opinion on it (Designer of the F-16) \"The F-35 was born of an exceptionally dumb piece of airforce PR spin\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":8714504,"author":"Guthur","month":"2014-12","story":"Luke AFB changes refueling truck color, mitigates F-35 shutdowns","stance":"reference","why":"Expert view that F-35 is over-engineered and expensive.","quote":"And here is a comment by someone who knows a lot more than I do.","text":"IMO another example of how the F-35 is an over specified, over engineered and expensive turkey. And here is a comment by someone who knows a lot more than I do. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7989871,"author":"rkowalick","month":"2014-07","story":"U.S. military grounds F-35 Joint Strike Fighter after fire","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with F-16 designer on concepts behind F-35 design.","quote":"this interview with the designer of the F-16 about the concepts behind the design of the F-35","text":"Whenever the F-35 is mentioned, I am reminded of this interview with the designer of the F-16 about the concepts behind the design of the F-35: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7990194,"author":"mmaunder","month":"2014-07","story":"U.S. military grounds F-35 Joint Strike Fighter after fire","stance":"reference","why":"Sprey is the designer of the F16 and A10.","quote":"Pierre Sprey is the designer of the F16 and the A10.","text":"We need the same kind of innovation in the jet fighter sector as we're seeing by SpaceX in orbital launches. The F-35 is a disaster because it was designed by committee and has ended up being good for nothing. The body is wide because it had to accommodate the vertical takeoff jet and nozzle and the wings had to be short. This makes it horrible for dog-fights, a fuel consumption pig, unable to loiter over an area, unable to provide close air support. The fact that it's stealth is debatable too. There have been instances where low frequency radar has been repurposed to detect stealth aircraft like the F117 shot down over Serbia. Basically nothing can hide from L-band radar (1 to 2 Ghz). [VIDEO] Pierre Sprey is the designer of the F16 and the A10."},{"id":8009349,"author":"cipher0","month":"2014-07","story":"The Pentagon's $399 Billion Plane to Nowhere","stance":"reference","why":"Sprey calls the F-35 born of dumb PR spin.","quote":"The F-35 was born of an exceptionally dumb piece of airforce PR spin","text":"Pierre Sprey (F-16's designer) on the F-35 \"The F-35 was born of an exceptionally dumb piece of airforce PR spin\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":7906759,"author":"pravda","month":"2014-06","story":"Pierre Sprey on the F-35 [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The real mission is for the US government to send money to Lockheed.","text":"Q: What is the point of this plane? A: The real mission is for the US government to send money to Lockheed. [VIDEO] By that standard, the F-35 has been a trememdous success!"}]},"nBaIRm4610o":{"wy":"HN uses it in vaccine-safety threads as a source on aspiration and the consequences of inadvertent intravenous injection, with mixed enthusiasm.","m":[{"id":39658714,"author":"cbeach","month":"2024-03","story":"mRNA vaccines: let's eradicate herpes, cure cancer, and be sick a whole lot less","stance":"rec","why":"Explains aspiration technique and consequences of inadvertent intravenous injection.","quote":"Doctor John Campbell is a good place to start","text":"Doctor John Campbell is a good place to start: This video focusses on the aspiration technique, and the consequences of inadvertantly giving an mRNA injection intravenously: [VIDEO] His bio: \"My name is John Campbell and I am a retired Nurse Teacher and A and E nurse based in England. I also do some teaching in Asia and Africa when time permits. These videos are to help students to learn the background to all forms of health care. My PhD focused on the development of open learning resources for nurses nationally and internationally.\""},{"id":34136047,"author":"johnchristopher","month":"2022-12","story":"Rogue antibody and mystery pathogen behind AstraZeneca blood clots: study","stance":"reference","why":"Doctor discussing vaccine injection handling.","quote":"it's definitely the guy","text":"Oh, I saw a video in 2021 in which a retired doctor/uni. prof. explained AZ complications (it was someone from the UK) were most likely/could be related to how nurses handled the needle and where they stung it. Like, he was concerned about the lack of instructions given to vaccinating squad. I blacked this info out because it was too much of a yet another thing I had to watch for at the time on top of others but for my first booster I had a chat with the nurse about the dosage of the booster and... yeah... better check these kind of things out yourselves, things can go wrong really fast (and mark your legs and operation instructions on your body before going under the scalpel :). Edit: not sure if it's the video I watched but it's definitely the guy [VIDEO]"},{"id":32959637,"author":"fakethenews2022","month":"2022-09","story":"Censorship by big tech at the behest of the U.S. government?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I linked to a Youtube video by a doctor.","text":"This wasn't editing. It was censorship. I linked to a Youtube video by a doctor. Included in the video's description was a link to the paper. Dang claims that users flagged it and linked to the FAQ. There is nothing in the link that would violate the FAQ. I cussed him out in another comment and he blocked that too. My link to the paper itself was downvoted too. [VIDEO] [LINK] Now myocarditis is accepted and found on the CDC site on the mRNA vaccines. Hacker News is shit. I have used it far less since that time."}]},"pRjsu9-Vznc":{"wy":"Cited in audio-streaming threads as a critical breakdown showing MQA is neither lossless nor transparent.","q":{"x":"the video breakdowns I've watched on MQA are interesting listens even as someone who doesn't use Tidal.","i":27183857,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27183857,"author":"throw_nbvc1234","month":"2021-05","story":"Apple Music Announces Spatial Audio and Lossless Audio","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting breakdown of MQA even for non-Tidal users.","quote":"the video breakdowns I've watched on MQA are interesting listens even as someone who doesn't use Tidal.","text":"Just to add to this comment, the video breakdowns I've watched on MQA are interesting listens even as someone who doesn't use Tidal. [VIDEO] -- Critque of MQA [LINK] -- in defense of MQA At the end of the day, people should listen to what they think sounds good (is this an opinion?). If a placebo makes someone enjoy something more, is it really a placebo? If someone can't tell the difference between low quality recordings and high quality recordings, why should they go through the trouble of sourcing the high quality stuff. It's so easy to lose track of the subjective nature of audio in all of the objective details."},{"id":34605427,"author":"LeoPanthera","month":"2023-02","story":"Spotify is first music streaming service to surpass 200M paid subscribers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"MQA isn't lossless and distorts audio","text":"I can't forgive Tidal for their promotion of MQA, nothing more than a scam to fleece audiophiles who don't know better out of their money. MQA isn't lossless and distorts audio: [VIDEO] Tidal HiFi isn't lossless either: [LINK] I don't think any of the streaming services are particularly friendly to artists either, but Apple Music pays more to the artist than Spotify does, and includes (real) lossless audio at no extra cost. For now, they've got my dollar."},{"id":29999649,"author":"PaulDavisThe1st","month":"2022-01","story":"Is old music killing new music?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Alas, this is BS.","text":"> a Tidal subscription (which has masters at supposedly higher quality than CDs) Alas, this is BS. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"}]},"qBpiXcyB7wU":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining primer on grid problems, and cited for aging equipment causing fires.","q":{"x":"You should probably watch John Oliver's Power Grid episode","i":29148988,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29148988,"author":"daly","month":"2021-11","story":"Scientists pour cold water on Bill Gates' nuclear plans","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver episode explaining the power grid.","quote":"You should probably watch John Oliver's Power Grid episode","text":"You should probably watch John Oliver's Power Grid episode The Power Grid: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO) [VIDEO]"},{"id":42676870,"author":"cassepipe","month":"2025-01","story":"Two fire experts interviewed about L.A. wildfires","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on aging electric line equipment causing fires.","quote":"90 year old electric line equipment responsible for fires","text":"Bad forest management or ... bad electrical equipment ? This excerpt of the last week tonight show 90 year old electric line equipment responsible for fires : [VIDEO] I don't pretend to know anything and I don't know how common this is but it is pretty concerning and I wonder if anything has been done about that."},{"id":31896357,"author":"soraminazuki","month":"2022-06","story":"Energy crunch concerns lead Japan’s government to call for reduced AC use","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for US power grid problems.","quote":"","text":"Those two states are indeed what I had in mind. I was assuming that many other states are in a similar situation considering that the power grid is a frequent topic of US issues [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aSEQfqNYNAc",1]]},"sXPOw2unxy0":{"wy":"Cited in cannabis and drug-war threads as an eye-opening look at the political reasons for criminalization.","q":{"x":"What I found pretty eye-opening in the sense of \"Oh wow, that's so obvious","i":22487245,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22487245,"author":"klingonopera","month":"2020-03","story":"Malawi legalises the growing, selling and export of cannabis","stance":"rec","why":"Eye-opening on political reasons for criminalizing weed","quote":"What I found pretty eye-opening in the sense of \"Oh wow, that's so obvious","text":"Yeah, there are many political reasons for criminalizing it. What I found pretty eye-opening in the sense of \"Oh wow, that's so obvious, I actually didn't think about that \" was that one of the reasons was the American Prohibition in the 30s, and after it was abolished, a bunch of people were about to lose their jobs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31050557,"author":"nandhinianand","month":"2022-04","story":"My take on Elon's offer for Twitter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Okay.. this comment finally got me to login and chime-in on this topic. > And I don’t care how rare it is. It’s not rare enough. It’s not good if it happens once. “It is better that ten guilty persons escape than that one innocent suffer.” And again, I agree that sometimes people just said something shitty or at least that got interpreted shittily, but I have never seen something even once where it didn’t feel like a horridly disproportionate response. Not even once. Completely agree... No arguments with this .. And as always there is a but.. (Note: following is not a personal or specific counter-argument to this comment). The general calling it \"cancel-culture\" or \"leftist political correctness forcing it's way (Aka fascist-leftist\" narrative misses the point as much as the common \"it's rare, but these people deserve it. They're horrible people\" narrative. The way i look at it \"freedom to innovate, and have a system that sets up a market that encourages innovation\" has brought us this current social media centered around outrage, fear, anger and rage centered way of connecting to people. Those employers are \"free-to-fire\" as much as each person in the mob that responds with rage is free to express their opinion and the one person who uncensoredly tweeted something is free to make mistakes. So what's the solution, I don't know anything specific outside of going all mystic/mythic/wishful saying we should all practice empathy better, we should find better ways of connecting to "},{"id":24533801,"author":"znpy","month":"2020-09","story":"Dream Vendor \"Canna_Bars\" Sentenced to Prison","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on the true reasons weed is illegal","quote":"Adam Conove made a whole episode in his show, \"Adam ruins everything\", about the true reasons why weed is illegal.","text":"That's not \"considerable information\", that's a known fact. Adam Conove made a whole episode in his show, \"Adam ruins everything\", about the true reasons why weed is illegal. Link: [VIDEO] Spoiler: it's about giving the government of the US a tool to discriminate black people and mexicans."},{"id":17899956,"author":"Abekkus","month":"2018-09","story":"Military robots are getting smaller and more capable","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The drug war has always been a cultural and class war","text":"The drug war has always been a cultural and class war, and of course it cuts along racial lines, as well as political ones. Not nonsense at all. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["DnPmg0R1M04",1]]},"uOJCS1W1uzg":{"wy":"Initially praised as a nicely done explainer, it is now more often cited as an example of overhyped quantum coverage.","m":[{"id":33806721,"author":"russellbeattie","month":"2022-11","story":"Physicists Create a Wormhole Using a Quantum Computer","stance":"rec","why":"Explainer video on the quantum computer wormhole experiment.","quote":"The accompanying video was quite nicely done","text":"The accompanying video was quite nicely done: [VIDEO] I have to say that I have a general suspicion of quantum computing research as it seems that the scientists could be making up success criteria as they go along and we'd have zero idea either way. That said, I respect these institutions enough to accept that people much more brilliant than myself are solving problems I can't begin to understand, and so I take these sorts of pronouncements at face value until proved otherwise."},{"id":42252457,"author":"godelski","month":"2024-11","story":"Teen mathematicians tie knots through a mind-blowing fractal","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The best example is the quantum wormhole article and video[0,1], because it is egregious","text":"Unfortunately I have not found this to be true (though it is in this case). There are quite a number of articles that are misleading or flat out false. The best example is the quantum wormhole article and video[0,1], because it is egregious and doesn't take much nuance or expertise see the issues. I'm glad they made a note and wrote a follow-up[2], but all this illustrates what is wrong with the picture. For one, the article and video were published the same day as it was published in Nature[3]. Sure, they are getting wind of the preprints, but in this case there was none! They're often acting as a PR firm for many of the big universities and companies, unfortunately so is Nature. The article was published Nov 30th, but the note didn't come till March 29th![4] You might think, oh it took that much time to figure out that there were problems, but no, only a few days after the publication (Dec 2nd) even Ars Technica was posting about the misinformation. They even waited over a month after Kobrin, Schuster, and Yao placed their comment on ArXiv[6]. Scott Aaronson had already written about it[7]. There was so much dissent in that time frame that it is hard to explain it as an accident. A week or two and it wouldn't be an issue. But I think Peter Woit explains it best[8] (published, yes, Nov 30th). This work is getting the full-press promotional package: no preprint on the arXiv, embargoed info to journalists, with reveal at a press conference, a cover story in Nature, accompanied"},{"id":38209829,"author":"godelski","month":"2023-11","story":"Show HN: Bulletpapers – ArXiv AI paper summarizer, won Anthropic Hackathon","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Quanta published a shit piece recently about quantum wormholes and machine learning","text":"I think it matters who we want to write papers to. Right now we write papers and abstracts to reviewers. That's because that's how we're measured and that's where we compete. But I'd say that we generally believe that papers are written to other researchers, which I agree that that should be the goal. But as this competition is increasing we're starting to write more to media as this can usually pass review and gathers lots of citations (these people tend to be from big schools too which have large media arms and are willing to pay for articles in news venues). This is why I'm deeply frustrated with academia right now. Papers are supposed to be how I communicate to my fellow researchers working on the same or similar topic. They're not for communicating to someone in a different field and not for communicating to the public layman (nor should they be!). It is the job of science communicators to act as the bridge between laymen and researcher, which a lot do a poor job as they're beholden to the YouTube algorithm, not accuracy. Hell, Quanta published a shit piece recently about quantum wormholes and machine learning and what did they do when it was called out? Just write another article and add a note on their youtube video. Nature is pulling similar shit. I get wanting to make science popular and exciting, but truth/accuracy has a lower bound in complexity whereas fantasy doesn't. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33829413,"author":"pera","month":"2022-12","story":"The Death of Quanta Magazine","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"checks almost all my indicators of low quality quantum woo","text":"> For me the worst part of the video was at 11:53, where they showed a graph with a bright point labelled “negative energy peak” on it. The problem is that this is not a plot of data, it’s just a drawing, with no connection to the experiment. Lay people will think they are seeing actual data, so this is straightforward disinformation. This is exactly how I interpreted that part of the video [1], and it makes me feel sad because Quanta is the only kinda pop-science magazine I enjoy reading. As a lay parson, when it comes to quantum mechanics I have to use my own set of heuristics to identify quackery: this Quanta's video checks almost all my indicators of low quality quantum woo - I don't like to say this about someone's else work but just the aesthetics reminds me to pseudo-scientific documentaries. [1] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MBT1OK6VAIU",1],["gUnxzVOs3rk",1]]},"2C-zWrhFqpM":{"wy":"HN mostly links it as background in political arguments or for a memorable moment, rather than as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":17749456,"author":"uRreading2slow","month":"2018-08","story":"Google's censored Chinese search engine: a catalogue of ethical violations?","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky talk linked in a debate on corporate ethics.","quote":"","text":"> Maybe we should be, though? [..] Great. I'm not sure that should top your list of injustices, though. It doesn't top mine, because it's not one thats happening in my back yard. So do you think we should care, or shouldn't we? If your point ultimately is no, we should not care, then why not be honest and say \"but we shouldn't care, and here's why\". And how can you claim \"nobody\" cares? Are you sure there aren't people in many countries who wish companies wouldn't just deal with their butcherers, but give the oppressed a voice? If you don't care, just say it. It's not about where it happens, it's about whether my \"friends\" are involved in it. If Google didn't use this site as a PR platform, too, there'd be no point in talking about it. But since many people here use their products, because they claim a \"Hi fellow Kids\" kind of humanness, it is relevant. When some total stranger acts horrible to others, but they can handle it themselves, I probably wouldn't bother getting involved. But if that guy was chatting to me 5 minutes prior to that, and considers himself someone on good terms with me, I would absolutely at least say something. It doesn't matter what's on \"top\" of the \"list of priorites\", it only matters that it matters, and that I have a position on it I must make known or be complicit by silence. It's really simple, we understand that intuitively in all sorts of situations, it doesn't get more complicated because the behaviour to have a position on gets worse. Totalit"},{"id":14955212,"author":"thinkfurther","month":"2017-08","story":"Government Report Finds Drastic Impact of Climate Change on U.S","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as supporting background in a political argument.","quote":"","text":"So you're turning being held accountable into somehow furthering lack of accountability. Not that I think the agreement is super perfect, but that still sticks out like a sore thumb. > Let's fund solar power and electric cars You've always been free to do that. You still are. Instead of, say, waging a war of aggression for control of oil, littering the world with military bases, all the while complaining about the specter of a \"world government\" -- which is anything but the US desires of hegemony, right? No problem with the main superpower being a brute instead of a boon, but a huge problem with the means to defend ourselves. Also see [VIDEO] , and for now in general I'll just say \"no\". Not good enough. Too little, too late."},{"id":15617246,"author":"madmax108","month":"2017-11","story":"A “Silicon Valley” actor is terrified by what’s happening in Silicon Valley","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"which I consider one of the nicest \"burn\" moments ever","text":"It's scary (Stanford prison experiment-like) how easy it is to convince a regular joe employee that what he's working on is \"for the betterment of the world\", while that statement is very very fuzzy to say the least (and as recent events have shown) where one man's Utopia is another man's surveillance state. We put waaay too much impetus on \"world changing ideas\" while completely ignoring the real life implications of them, as the article rightfully points out. Reminds me of the statement that Noam Chomsky ended his recent talk at Google with (which I consider one of the nicest \"burn\" moments ever): Interviewer: It's not everyday that a non-google gets to sit in a room full of people who work at google, and are s/w engineers, and are advertising experts, and are market experts in different fields. Do you have anything that you'd like to ask us? Chomsky: <shrugs> Why not do some of the serious things? [VIDEO]"},{"id":14885095,"author":"k1m","month":"2017-07","story":"Talks at Google: Noam Chomsky [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The last part is great","text":"The last part is great: Khalil: \"It's not every day that a non-Googler gets to sit in a room full of people who work at Google, and are software engineers, and are advertising experts, and are you know market experts in different fields. Do you have anything that you'd like to ask us?\" Chomsky: \"Why not do some of the serious things?\" :) [VIDEO]"}]},"4iFYaeoE3n4":{"wy":"Linked as a critical example of official indifference to civilian costs of foreign policy.","m":[{"id":47113094,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-02","story":"Iran students stage first large anti-government protests since deadly crackdown","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Sanctions\" are just a sanitized way of saying \"forced starvation\" and \"denying basic medical care\" because that's what happens. For Cuba, this has been going on so long that the CIA documents about the effect of sanctions and a blockade itself has been declassified ( in 2005 ) [1]. When faced with a UN report that estimated 500,000 children had been killed by US sanctions in 1996, then UN Ambassador and later US Secretary of State Madeline Albright famously said \"the price was worth it\" [2]. And sanctions don't actually work. Not against enemies anyway. Just like Cuba has endured 60+ years of sanctions and Russia has endured Ukraine-related sanctions, enemies have or build an economy to be resilient to the sanctions to the point that the regime survives, even thrives in the face of perceived exteranl threats. Probably the only successful use of sanctions was South Africa. Why? Because apartheid South Africa was an ally so the BDS movement crippled the economy. And most of the time sanctions have no other reason than the affected country dared to not be exploited by the West and Western companies. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38656774,"author":"ajsnigrutin","month":"2023-12","story":"September 11th That You Have Never Seen","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yep, and many others, some before 9/11, some after and some because of it. Considering the amount of bad things some western countries have done and are still doing, or are helping do, the number of terrorist attacks is surprisingly low.. Terrorism is a war of the weak against the strong and with such massive atrocities and killings as in eg. gaza now, you're getting more and more people with literally nothing left to lose, no home, family dead, friends dead, some already in europe/USS, some soon on their way there. And when something explodes, the media will be asking themselves \"who radicalized them?!\". But, some say that \"the price is worth it\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":30671311,"author":"someotherperson","month":"2022-03","story":"Mapping and Seizing the Oligarchs' Assets","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Morallly obtuse\" as if that wasn't what the US was doing[0]. I guess the Red Cross centre my cousin worked in was a legitimate military target when it was hit with a US airstrike :) Perhaps if anyone asks the Russians they can just say it was worth it[1]. Let's stop with pretending that the reason why Ukrainian deaths are different is for any other reason than because they're pale skilled Europeans with blue eyes and blonde hair. Most major media have already admitted it, governments have already applauded it in parliament, it's probably about time that HN stops pretending it's about other reasons too. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"DkUpZkeqhF4":{"wy":"Mostly cited in critical or skeptical comments about Buterin, with a little admiration for the talk coming from a 19-year-old.","m":[{"id":16927149,"author":"DalasNoin","month":"2018-04","story":"Ethereum Sharding FAQ","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Very impressive talk for a 19 year old.","text":"i'm honestly trying to verify this. i'm quiet skeptical about the character of some developers of bitcoin, i dont know about peter todd. bitcoinerrorlog is linking to this video of him talking about quantum computers as a proof. this is the closest i have come to hard evidence of this extreme claim. i have listened to the first half of the video, just him talking about quantum computers. Very impressive talk for a 19 year old. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33886543,"author":"kevinak","month":"2022-12","story":"What in the Ethereum application ecosystem excites me","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Vitalik tried scamming people with a \"quantum computer simulation mining\" thing back in 2013","text":"I wonder if people around here know that Vitalik tried scamming people with a \"quantum computer simulation mining\" thing back in 2013? Amazing that more people aren't more vary of him. 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. YT video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27067367,"author":"lottin","month":"2021-05","story":"Starting a crypto project","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"If this isn't crackpottery of the highest degree I don't what is.","text":"The element of controversy isn't whether efficient quantum simulation is possible, but the idea that by running such a simulation one would be able to break cryptography, as if they were using an actual quantum computer. This is a talk where he makes this claim [VIDEO] If this isn't crackpottery of the highest degree I don't what is."}]},"EzTWjNUb4H4":{"wy":"HN cites it in IPv6 threads as an example of a large adopter, especially for address-space collisions after mergers and conflicts in 10/8.","q":{"x":"See this talk from Wells Fargo as an example","i":44670774,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44670774,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2025-07","story":"BGP.Tools: Browse the Internet Ecosystem","stance":"reference","why":"Wells Fargo talk on 10/8 conflicts in mergers","quote":"See this talk from Wells Fargo as an example","text":"> Not sure why you'd need more than 10/8 Large organizations have moved to IPv6 because they, and everyone else, are using 10/8, and so when mergers and acquisitions happen trying to connect the networks together becomes a nightmare. See this talk from Wells Fargo as an example: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40008999,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-04","story":"Do You Need IPv4 Anymore?","stance":"reference","why":"Wells Fargo as an example of a large IPv6 adopter","quote":"","text":"> I have yet to meet an infrastructure engineer or even hear of one who switched to IPv6 on purpose because they thought it was better. T-Mobile US: * [LINK] Wells Fargo: * [VIDEO] Microsoft * [LINK] * [LINK] Google: * [LINK] LinkedIn: * [LINK] Facebook is famously IPv6-only internally (and as of 2016, 60% of user traffic was over IPv6): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":40044058,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-04","story":"Why No IPv6?","stance":"reference","why":"Wells Fargo listed among IPv6 adopters","quote":"","text":"Tell that to T-Mobile US: * [LINK] Wells Fargo: * [VIDEO] Microsoft: * [LINK] * [LINK] Google: * [LINK] LinkedIn (pre-Microsoft): * [LINK]"},{"id":40050646,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-04","story":"Why No IPv6?","stance":"reference","why":"Wells Fargo listed among IPv6 adopters","quote":"Also Wells Fargo","text":"> IPv6 can work side by side with IPv4 on the servers, but it cannot replace IPv4. Facebook is famously IPv6-only internally (and as of 2016, 60% of user traffic was IPv6-only): * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] Microsoft and Google are IPv6-only single-stack (or moving in that direction) on their internal corporate networks: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] Also Wells Fargo: * [VIDEO] The main 'advantage' IPv4 has is inertia."},{"id":38223237,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-11","story":"AWS to start charging for IPv4 usage, but critical services don't support IPv6","stance":"reference","why":"Wells Fargo cites address collisions in mergers as IPv6 reason","quote":"Complications with address space collisions (esp. M&A) was one of the reasons give in this Wells Fargo presentation from 2014","text":"> There’s (IMO) a much better reason: so your endpoints can have globally unique addresses. Complications with address space collisions (esp. M&A) was one of the reasons give in this Wells Fargo presentation from 2014: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":29032859,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-10","story":"IPv6 Progress Top Sites 2021","stance":"reference","why":"Wells Fargo's business case for IPv6 including merger address conflicts","quote":"","text":"> What is the sales pitch you would bring to the CEO of a Fortune 500? When you buy or merge companies, and they both have RFC 1918 addresses, you'll probably have a conflict between the two entities. You'll probably have to implement NAT with-in your own network and hope the two sides don't have to talk to each other that much. (Or you completely re-IP the acquisition.) With IPv6, either the companies will be using their own PI IPv6 space and/or they will be using unique ULA prefixes, so the chance of conflicts will be very small. This at least was one of the business cases for Wells Fargo, \"the fourth largest bank in the United States by total assets and is one of the largest as ranked by bank deposits and market capitalization\": * [VIDEO]"}]},"HODxnUrFX6k":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as primary-source evidence that the public was misled about the second Tonkin attack.","q":{"x":"The American public was told there were two distinct attacks on two separate nights, but that wasn't true","i":18803913,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":34713352,"author":"LarryMullins","month":"2023-02","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"McNamara's tacit admission the second incident did not occur.","quote":"McNamara's tacit admission","text":"For completeness, the \"Gulf of Tonkin\" incident was two alleged incidents. One of them probably happened and the other almost certainly didn't (admitted by Robert McNamara years later.) The first incident, the one that probably happened, was insufficient casus belli for LBJ and then conveniently for the war hawks the second incident, which never actually happened, sealed the deal two days later. McNamara's tacit admission: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23582721,"author":"catalogia","month":"2020-06","story":"The Nova Scotia shooter case has hallmarks of an undercover operation?","stance":"reference","why":"McNamara admitting the second attack never happened.","quote":"Straight from the horse's mouth","text":"> A lot of other people would cite the Gulf of Tonkin as an example too, but that's something I know less about so I'll leave it to someone else. The Gulf of Tonkin incident was explained to the American public at the time as pair of attacks on two separate nights against the US Navy by the North Vietnamese. This was used as the casus belli for the Vietnam War; in response to the two attacks congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution which authorized LBJ to start a war (without formally declaring a war...) The first attack probably did happen, however in 2003 Robert McNamara (who had been the US Secretary of Defense at the time of the incident) admitted the second attack never happened. Straight from the horse's mouth: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18803913,"author":"darkpuma","month":"2019-01","story":"Drones Used to Find Toy-Like “Butterfly” Land Mines","stance":"reference","why":"McNamara admitting the second Tonkin attack never happened.","quote":"The American public was told there were two distinct attacks on two separate nights, but that wasn't true","text":"That's fair context, here is some more: In 2017, there were 14 recorded casualties from mines/unexploded remnants of war (ERW) in Vietnam, while in 2016 there were 1,943 in Afghanistan. However since 1975 there have been 105,073 casualties from mines/ERW in Vietnam, while there have been 29,662 casualties from mines/ERW in Afghanistan since 1978. [LINK] [LINK] The Soviet-Afghan War was started in 1979 when Alfa Group (the KGB's 'spetsnaz') dressed in Afghani uniforms stormed the Queens Palace in Kabul and murdered the Afghani president Hafizullah Amin. Amin had been in office only three months, and came to power by deposing and ordering the murder of his predecessor Nur Muhammad Taraki. Taraki was a communist and before his demise, had plead with the Soviets for military intervention. Before Taraki's demise he and Amin had worked together and together both are known for murdering thousands of civilians and political opponents. The Vietnam War was sold to the American public in 1964 after two separate attacks on USN ships in the Gulf of Tonkin. However in 2003 Robert McNarmara (who was the Secretary of Defense when this happened) publicly admitted the second attack had never happened. The American public was told there were two distinct attacks on two separate nights, but that wasn't true: [VIDEO] I suppose my point is that both wars were huge unpopular clusterfucks that were started on false pretexts, that neither Afghanistan nor Vietnam were placid before foreign interventio"}]},"L9oh3gqOEKU":{"wy":"It is linked as an audio and visual example of IBM's corporate rally songs in threads about IBM culture.","q":{"x":"IBM actually had its own rally song","i":16652202,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":39922965,"author":"tecleandor","month":"2024-04","story":"Recreating the Flying Toasters screen saver for the Vision Pro","stance":"reference","why":"The IBM song, cited while recalling a similar tune.","quote":"","text":"Something kicked in my memory when I started that video and for a second I couldn't temember properly if I was hearing the Flying Toasters song or Ever Onward IBM [0]. I remember having a DOS program with a PC Speaker karaoke version of that song, maybe on a disk that also included Alley Cat by IBM too. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16652202,"author":"archagon","month":"2018-03","story":"Cutting ‘Old Heads’ at IBM","stance":"reference","why":"IBM's own rally song.","quote":"IBM actually had its own rally song","text":"I was surprised when I visited the Computer History Museum recently and discovered that IBM actually had its own rally song: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8245036,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2014-08","story":"Tripping through IBM’s 1937 corporate songbook","stance":"reference","why":"Recording of the IBM 'Ever Onward' song from the songbook.","quote":"You can also hear 'Ever Onward' from the songbook here, which I am oddly drawn to.","text":"A snippet of that song is on YouTube from that documentary. [LINK] You can also hear 'Ever Onward' from the songbook here, which I am oddly drawn to. [VIDEO]"}]},"L_Guz73e6fw":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it for specific Altman quotes, such as models as reasoning engines rather than databases, while many commenters found the interview shallow or insincere.","q":{"x":"too much of the processing power is going into using the model as a database instead of using the model as a reasoning engine","i":35306665,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35416915,"author":"qwertox","month":"2023-04","story":"ChatGPT as a Calculator for Words","stance":"reference","why":"Altman transcript on models as reasoning engines","quote":"I suspect too much of the like processing power for lack of a better word is going into using the models as a database","text":"From his interview with Lex Friedman: Quoting what he says [0][1]: > You know, a funny thing about the way we're training these models is I suspect too much of the like processing power for lack of a better word is going into using the models as a database instead of using the model as a reasoning engine. The thing that's really amazing about the system is that it, for some definition of reasoning, and we could of course quibble about it and there's plenty for which definitions this wouldn't be accurate. But for some definition it can do some kind of reasoning. And, you know, maybe like the scholars and the experts and like the armchair quarterbacks on Twitter would say, no, it can't. You're misusing the word, you know, whatever, whatever. But I think most people who have used the system would say, okay, it's doing something in this direction. And I think that's remarkable. And the thing that's most exciting and somehow out of ingesting human knowledge, it's coming up with this reasoning capability. However, we're gonna talk about that. Now, in some senses, I think that will be additive to human wisdom. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35306665,"author":"bluehorseray","month":"2023-03","story":"OpenAI CEO Sam Altman on Lex Fridman Podcast","stance":"reference","why":"Altman on models as reasoning engines rather than databases","quote":"too much of the processing power is going into using the model as a database instead of using the model as a reasoning engine","text":"14:17: \"I suspect too much of the processing power is going into using the model as a database instead of using the model as a reasoning engine\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":35306735,"author":"diimdeep","month":"2023-03","story":"OpenAI CEO Sam Altman on Lex Fridman Podcast","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Flaws in communication style is a feature, not a bug in general, at least for humans in power.","text":"Lex Fridman: charisma is a dangerous thing. Flaws in communication style is a feature, not a bug in general, at least for humans in power. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35307078,"author":"braindead_in","month":"2023-03","story":"OpenAI CEO Sam Altman on Lex Fridman Podcast","stance":"reference","why":"Altman on consciousness and simulation","quote":"Interesting comment about consciousness being the fundamental substrate of the world and we are in a dream or simulation.","text":"Interesting comment about consciousness being the fundamental substrate of the world and we are in a dream or simulation. That is exactly the Nondualist position. Did he say Bramhan? [VIDEO]"},{"id":35388957,"author":"hamburga","month":"2023-03","story":"If AI scaling is to be shut down, let it be for a coherent reason","stance":"reference","why":"Altman interview cited in AI risk discussion","quote":"","text":"I am trying really hard to understand the AI optimists' perspective, but I am shocked at how hard it is to find people responding to the substantive arguments made about AI existential risk. As far as I'm concerned, you sort of have to address the big, tough points in Bostrom's Superintelligence[1], and probably Yudkowsky's List of Lethalities[2]. They have to do with intelligence explosions, with instrumental convergence, and with orthogonality of goals, and all kinds of deceptive behavior that we would expect from advanced AI. Throw in Bostrom's \"Vulnerable World\" thought experiment for good measure as well[3]. If you're not addressing these ideas, there's no point in debating. Strawmanning \"AI will kill us all\" out of contexte will indeed sound like wacko fear-mongering. What surprises me is that everybody's familiar with the \"paperclip maximizer\" meme, and yet I'm not hearing any equivalently memey-yet-valid rebuttals to it. Maybe I'm missing it. Please point me in the right direction. Aaronson certainly does not address the core theoretical fears. Instead we get: > Would your rationale for this pause have applied to basically any nascent technology — the printing press, radio, airplanes, the Internet? “We don’t yet know the implications, but there’s an excellent chance terrible people will misuse this, ergo the only responsible choice is to pause until we’re confident that they won’t”? We did not have any reason to believe that any of these technologies could lead to an "},{"id":38333141,"author":"zzzceo","month":"2023-11","story":"OpenAI Board Urged by Microsoft, Investors to Restore Altman","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Altman's responses, if you could call them that, were uninteresting to the extreme.","text":"I was not at all surprised re: Altman's ouster, and OpenAI should be both firm and unapolegetic in keeping him out. What took them so long!? I remember when I dumped him myself, after watching Altman's snore-inducing interview on the ever-engaging Lex Fridman podcast: [VIDEO] Lex's questions were the only inciteful part; Altman's responses, if you could call them that, were uninteresting to the extreme. This is the billionaire CEO of the hottest tech company around? WTF? Now I can see why Hacker News never changed, still has 1999 interface, etc. If this is leadership, call me an Uber. Ditto Brockman's GPT-4 reveal, all giddy doodads: Oh, look-see, it can compose poems in the style of [sic] dude! nobody cares. Zzzz. Altman is just so damn boring, his energy level and engagement on par with a cup of decaffeinated tea. Listen to him on Fridman. Find a single incite from Altman in the entire 2+ hours that suggests engagement beyond the purely superficial. You can't. You feel actual pain as Fridman works from every side to pull something, anything out of Altman's blasé bleh blah."},{"id":36129416,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2023-05","story":"A.I. Poses ‘Risk of Extinction,’ Industry Leaders Warn","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I didn't think he seemed very sincere.","text":"Indeed. It's probably what I would do if I were him. Or direct OpenAI entirely into AI safety research and stop doing capabilities research. I watched his interview with Lex Fridman,[1] and I didn't think he seemed very sincere. On the other hand I think there are a lot of people who are very sincere, like Max Tegmark.[2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":35321533,"author":"desmosxxx","month":"2023-03","story":"OpenAI CEO Sam Altman on Lex Fridman Podcast","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Just very shallow and vapid, which is disappointing given the guests","text":"Every time Sam asked Lex a question I was excited for some discussion and then disappointed when Lex started talking. He either goes off on random tangent or doesn't get to the heart of the question / debate. Just very shallow and vapid, which is disappointing given the guests and Lex supposedly being of a technical background. I'd rather Joe Rogan interview Lex's guest and that says a lot. Example: [VIDEO] He does have interesting guests though that I don't see on other podcasts so maybe he's seen as safe / controllable and is PR approved."}]},"QFgcqB8-AxE":{"wy":"Linked by commenters as a reaction or meme reference in unrelated threads, with little explanation.","m":[{"id":48757911,"author":"chii","month":"2026-07","story":"Age verification is just a precursor to automated attribution of speech","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"for such a wide ranging effects, brexit's vote had only 2% margin of error, with 48% against. The people's stupidity is why \"important\" questions like that should not be put to a vote; they can't be expected to understand the entirety of the outcome and consequences. As the other comment has posted: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45738304,"author":"throwforfeds","month":"2025-10","story":"Using AI to negotiate a $195k hospital bill down to $33k","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Though why do you Americans put up with all this? I have heard the US is a democracy. So then insurance-based healthcare is what American people truly want? It's because our politicians are largely owned by our corporations and spend a ridiculous amount of money protecting their interests [1]. We almost had a public option with the original \"Obamacare\", but it was forced out of the bill [2]. Also, just turn on Fox News for an evening and realize it's been the number one news channel in the US for 20-something years. They've been a right wing corporate propaganda machine for a long time, all while brilliantly portraying themselves as the \"underdog\" fighting the mainstream media. Americans aren't very educated and take pride in their ignorance, unfortunately. [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45429986,"author":"byearthithatius","month":"2025-09","story":"50 Things I Know","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Not all people. But look what happened to Reddit. Started as mostly coders now its slowly being turned into another content machine."},{"id":41567974,"author":"DiggyJohnson","month":"2024-09","story":"Amazon tells employees to return to office five days a week","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I thought you were referencing this","text":"I thought you were referencing this: [VIDEO] > people don’t want what they want Which makes the opposite point, funny enough. Who is the “they”"},{"id":34761264,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-02","story":"Japan Adopts Plan to Make Maximum Use of Nuclear Power","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> People are scared of nuclear energy. \"But the people are retarded.\" [1]. \"The proximate cause of the disaster was the 2011 Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami, which occurred on the afternoon of 11 March 2011 and *remains the most powerful earthquake ever recorded in Japan*. The earthquake triggered a powerful tsunami, with 13–14-meter-high waves damaging the nuclear power plant's emergency diesel generators, leading to a loss of electric power. The result was the most severe nuclear accident since the Chernobyl disaster in 1986, classified as level seven on the International Nuclear Event Scale (INES) [...] and thus joining Chernobyl as the only other accident to receive such classification. [...] the INES ranks incidents by impact on population, so Chernobyl (335,000 people evacuated) and Fukushima (154,000 evacuated) [...]\" [2] Man, nuclear's weak. Worst two disasters end with just a few dozen people dead at most and merely a ton of economical damage? A single dam failure and you've got 100 thousand people dead. Coal alone kills 100 thousand people a year. Nuclear needs to step it up. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":48223893,"author":"xeonmc","month":"2026-05","story":"Hating AI Is Good","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Because democracy basically means, government, by the people, of the people, for the people. But the people are... [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"U3Ak-SmyHHQ":{"wy":"Used as a joke whenever someone dismisses scientific consensus.","m":[{"id":32476466,"author":"sdwr","month":"2022-08","story":"The Big Bang didn't happen: What do the James Webb images show?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Anyone know why this took so long to discover, if true? Is the James Webb that much more powerful than the Hubble? I'd love if this turned out to be an epicycle moment. It reinforces my belief that being paradigm-flexible is valuable."},{"id":32197744,"author":"nemothekid","month":"2022-07","story":"Polio Detected in US","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Science is a liar sometimes\"[1]","text":"There's a super strange trend I've been seeing, since COVID, where science is no longer allowed to be wrong. Even though the very discipline is about making hypothesis and proving them wrong; I'm seeing a lot of arguments that kind of boil down to \"Science is a liar sometimes\"[1]. Do you have the same opinion on particle physics? Scientists have spent the better part of 80 years on an obviously wrong standard model - has that caused you to doubt Maxwell's equations or how the circuits in your computer works? More seriously, \"lack of skepticism\" is bunk when the \"skeptics\" don't provide equal or greater research and reasoning. People have started to think of themselves as nobel laureates because they can say \"no, I don't think so\", and have 0 data to back them up. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30919400,"author":"mgfist","month":"2022-04","story":"One App – Two Worlds: This Is TikTok in Russia and Ukraine","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This whole discussions reminds me of this great scene","text":"This whole discussions reminds me of this great scene [VIDEO] Humans will always accept some axiom as truth without really verifying it. It's impossible to do so. Can any single person truly know how everything in their computer works? Or how the machines that made the semiconducter work? Nope. All we can do is try to determine the truth by proxy, which means the truth can and will always be manipulated."},{"id":28504532,"author":"nemothekid","month":"2021-09","story":"I Got a 'Mild' Breakthrough Case","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is the \"Science is a Liar sometimes\"[1] defense","text":"> We need to decide if we're okay with science agencies lying to us. This is the \"Science is a Liar sometimes\"[1] defense, and it can be used to discredit any sort of scientific research. You are basically asking that our agencies be 100% correct the first time and be perfect predictors of the future. Secondly I don't understand how you were mislead about vaccines. The efficiency rates were always described in preventing you from going to the hospital. With COVID, the main issue has always been overstressing the health system - thats where the breakdown happens and thats why everyone needs to take the vaccine. Even now, ICU beds are not majority unvaccinated COVID cases, which overworks nurses and prevents people with other diseases from getting proper treatment. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"WboggjN_G-4":{"wy":"HN people post it as a joke whenever factions that are nearly identical fight each other.","m":[{"id":49140293,"author":"cadamsdotcom","month":"2026-08","story":"I ♥ RSS – A directory of people who love RSS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"We are the People's Front of Judea!","text":"\"We are the People's Front of Judea!\" \"What about the popular front?\" \"He's over there.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":44498425,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2025-07","story":"Where are the GenZ multi millionaires and billionaires?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Paul's subaltern comment reminded me strongly of \"Life of Brian","text":"Aha, I'd originally confused your D with the DISC D irectness. Paul's subaltern comment reminded me strongly of \"Life of Brian\": [VIDEO] Re: Rao, I found that I had to deliberately read [LINK] in small chunks with large breaks, because otherwise it all blurs together in terms of being a bunch of h saps being awful to each other. More specifically to his introduction, one can see big differences in the \"tournament\" between say 1250 and 1500: over that period they go from being a sort of open wargame with a racing-for-pinkslips feel played over the several km of hill and dale between villages, to a sport played in an enclosed pitch in front of an audience, limited to vetted entrants with the appropriate gear."},{"id":43137097,"author":"thomassmith65","month":"2025-02","story":"California Nominates Steve Jobs for Its American Innovation $1 Coin","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Left-leaning people famously tend to disagree with each other","text":"Left-leaning people famously tend to disagree with each other (eg: as lampooned in [VIDEO] ). If 'hippies' here refers to the original hippies back in the 1960s, they ran the whole range from far pipebombs-in-the-name-of-communism left, to centrist."},{"id":42803344,"author":"heresie-dabord","month":"2025-01","story":"The Tyranny of Structurelessness (1970)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"structureless\" groups which spontaneously organized around an opportunity and were initially effective [...] but succumbed to the dynamics Even spontaneous groups must filter for Competence. This is fundamental. From domain-specific skills to planning and managing the sustainability of the project, a spontaneous group must be more than superficial associates and barnacles. For a humorously timeless example, see the revolutionary movement in Monty Python and the Holy Grail. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41849772,"author":"carapace","month":"2024-10","story":"The Stallman Report","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The People's Front of Judea vs. Judean People's Front","text":"Yes, that's the obvious argument against this being a false flag operation: why would they bother, they already won. Is Bill Gates that petty? So that means that it's more likely that this is just backbiting on an old, sick man. E.g.: The People's Front of Judea vs. Judean People's Front [VIDEO] Let the old nerd die in peace with his friends. (I'm done with this thread and this subject. Have a better one.)"},{"id":28876420,"author":"1cvmask","month":"2021-10","story":"Fecal Transplants May Reverse Brain Aging in Mice","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That was the first thing that came to my mind when I saw the headline. Some of their shows are often seen as absurd and then they become mainstream reality years later. Sort of like some of the old Monty Python works. For example there is the skit where they talk about fractious Judean (insert Israeli) politics and men giving birth: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":21403357,"author":"notlukesky","month":"2019-10","story":"Spanish government orders GitHub to take down Tsunami Democràtic repository","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Ultimately reminds me of the Monty Python skit from the Life of Brian","text":"While currently Catalan separatism is a non-starter there are issues in the areas that they claim as theirs as the Valencians consider themselves separate to the Catalans. It is the nesting doll situation of splinter separatist groups. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Ultimately reminds me of the Monty Python skit from the Life of Brian - The People's Front of Judea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20764198,"author":"duxup","month":"2019-08","story":"No Son of Mine Is Going to Be a Benthamite","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">9.9% the same but it’s the 0.01% difference that gets all the attention Ah the good old: [LINK] Also: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17315012,"author":"ItsMe000001","month":"2018-06","story":"Dell XPS 13 Review from a lifelong Mac user","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It is like this Monty Python scene","text":"I found that all I need is git for Windows and its bash and Unix commands, plus ConEmu for terminals. I'm completely at ease with developing on Windows now, and I've \"done Linux\" since 1994, worked long for one of the major Linux vendors and even contributed a bit to the Linux kernel (networking and firewall), but if I had to do system administration I might have a stronger opinion (simply because I'M so sued to Unix ways in general). It is like this Monty Python scene [VIDEO] when people start arguing about their operating system. For the last couple of years I looked at how nature (biology) \"computes\", not just neurons, the entire bio-chemistry with its machinery. Now that is something different - while everything people put on their computers works on pretty much the exact same principles unless you zoom all the way in to nanometer scale (figuratively speaking)."},{"id":16574327,"author":"smsm42","month":"2018-03","story":"Reddit and the Struggle to Detoxify the Internet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> And yet we need to do it Who are \"we\"? And what is \"it\"? Do you just take on yourself the mantle of decider for the whole world who is worthy and who is not? Or how is it determined? Who is worthy to wear that mantle? I don't think any human is. > I'd claim that this is a problem Maybe human nature is a \"problem\". But what you're going to do, replace humanity with better species? Do you have one in mind? Beyond that, I don't see how declaring it \"a problem\" helps anything, unless there's a fix to this problem. History teaches me that all attempts to create a \"new, better human race\" didn't just end badly - they ended so awfully terrible that when people point at it, other people get offended that you dare to compare their ideas to that . So, we have to deal with what we have now - and had for millienia. Given what we did with it - well, not ideal, but certainly there have been some improvement. > it would be great to incentivize the kind that leads to a more stable, more just society How do you know what leads to more just society? Maybe rants would lead to more just society faster? As for stability, stability is only good when we're at the optimum. Are we? > I feel we need to try and walk that fine line, if we want to have a society that's better than just random. Which fine line? Everybody has their own fine line, that's the point. You could maybe find a bunch of people whose fine line is similar to your own, if you don't look too far into the future (and if you do, what "}]},"cgm3_jzcNm4":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a favorite example of how TV imagery can craft a misleading reality.","q":{"x":"this is my favorite example of imagery's ability to craft a reality","i":36803490,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36803490,"author":"munificent","month":"2023-07","story":"The past is not true","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"this is my favorite example of imagery's ability to craft a reality","text":"As someone who has experienced several serious floods, this is my favorite example of imagery's ability to craft a reality: [VIDEO] Floodwaters tend to stir up a lot of dirt and mud, so the water is usually totally opaque. That makes it very hard to tell how deep it is. A lot of overhead views of flooded neighborhoods look completely disastrous, but in reality it's just a foot or two of water that will just go away once the drains can handle it. But, sometimes, flooding really is severe and the damage is monumental. But it can be hard to distinguish those from imagery. Every picture of a flood looks biblical."},{"id":21887724,"author":"ishjoh","month":"2019-12","story":"How Disinformation Hacks Your Brain","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"a reporter in a canoe is describing the events when two public citizens walk through the shot","text":"One of my favourite examples of this is from \"the today show\" on NBC where they're discussing a flood and a reporter in a canoe is describing the events when two public citizens walk through the shot and the water seems to be less than 1/2 a foot deep. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12098971,"author":"exclusiv","month":"2016-07","story":"Is Facebook Ready to Be the World’s Live News Network?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Our news media is terrible.","text":"You're absolutely right. Our news media is terrible. We have reporters on \"respected\" shows sensationalizing flooding in canoes! [1] They love to divide us based on race, sex, whatever, but the data doesn't support their claims of crisis. And we gobble it up. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"iGOAQli7tGc":{"wy":"HN uses it as the primary source for PHP's origins as a templating system for C and for the story behind its inconsistent function design.","q":{"x":"have a look at this talk[1] by Rasmus Lerdorf about its creation.","i":22084560,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22221615,"author":"aspratley","month":"2020-02","story":"New in PHP 8","stance":"reference","why":"Lerdorf explains PHP function parameter order inconsistency","quote":"Rasmus Lerdorf has talked about it in other videos but this is one","text":"Rasmus Lerdorf has talked about it in other videos but this is one [VIDEO] Basically, groups of functions are consistent: string,array etc. Their parameter order was usually based on whatever the underlying C API was."},{"id":22084560,"author":"ptx","month":"2020-01","story":"State of Software Engineers 2019","stance":"reference","why":"Lerdorf on PHP's origin as a templating system for C","quote":"have a look at this talk[1] by Rasmus Lerdorf about its creation.","text":"PHP certainly works just fine when used well, but what in particular makes it a great introductory language? (As for what's (arguably) wrong with it, have a look at this talk[1] by Rasmus Lerdorf about its creation. He intended it as a quick and dirty templating system for C programs. Because it was never designed to be a programming language, but somehow organically grew into one, it lacks (in the eyes of some) the elegance of a purposefully designed language.) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20915050,"author":"qilo","month":"2019-09","story":"25 Years of PHP [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Original untrimmed upload of the PHP talk","quote":"The original untrimmed video of this talk by GrUSP","text":"The original untrimmed video of this talk by GrUSP (Italian PHP events organizer) can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20915282,"author":"blindseer","month":"2019-09","story":"25 Years of PHP [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Original source of the talk","quote":"here is the original source [1], that this time was NOT in their video description.","text":"This is tangential but this channel frustrates me to no end. They shamelessly rip other people's content, stripping their videos of author information, conference information etc, AND add their own title card so that it appears like they are the publishers of the video. Why is this acceptable! Anyway, here is the original source [1], that this time was NOT in their video description. [1] [VIDEO] And this is the last time I commented about this channel [2] [2] [LINK]"}]},"iYWzMvlj2RQ":{"wy":"Cited whenever Nvidia's relationship with open-source and Linux drivers comes up.","q":{"x":"Nvidia has been the single worst company we've ever dealt with.","i":13144989,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":46634840,"author":"worksonmine","month":"2026-01","story":"I spent a year on Linux and forgot to miss Windows","stance":"reference","why":"Clip showing Torvalds's view of Nvidia Linux support.","quote":"And before blaming Linux watch this short clip[0].","text":"> just picked up a 3080 with 32gb ram Prepare for some potential headaches, do some research and see what kind of problems you might encounter. And before blaming Linux watch this short clip[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42047643,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2024-11","story":"ReiserFS and the Art and Artist Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds's blunt remarks on Nvidia.","quote":"","text":"[LINK] > We don't merge kernel code just because user space was written by a retarded monkey on crack - Linus Torvalds Also: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38831757,"author":"LoveMortuus","month":"2024-01","story":"Bazzite – a SteamOS-like OCI image for desktop, living room, and handheld PCs","stance":"reference","why":"Short clip giving context on Torvalds and Nvidia.","quote":"This video (it's just a clip of the original) might give you a bit of context","text":"This video (it's just a clip of the original) might give you a bit of context: Title: Linus Torvalds: Nvidia, F** You! Duration: 0:39 [VIDEO]"},{"id":35930795,"author":"nebulousthree","month":"2023-05","story":"Intel Issues New CPU Microcode for New, Undisclosed Security Updates","stance":"reference","why":"Well-known Torvalds clip on Nvidia.","quote":"You already know this clip","text":"Given the Linux bent, it makes sense considering AMD's relationship with the open source community relative to Intel's, and to an even greater extent NVIDIA's. You already know this clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24466620,"author":"totorovirus","month":"2020-09","story":"Nvidia to Acquire Arm for $40B","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds on Nvidia's attitude to open source developers.","quote":"nvidia is notorious for being not nice to oss developers as Linus Torvalds claims","text":"nvidia is notorious for being not nice to oss developers as Linus Torvalds claims: [VIDEO] I wonder how Linux would react to this news."},{"id":23777110,"author":"blueblisters","month":"2020-07","story":"CUDA 11.0","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds telling Nvidia off.","quote":"Like Torvalds says [1]: Fuck You, Nvidia.","text":"The economic incentive is simple: open-sourcing the driver will allow an open-source API to interact with the hardware, allowing AMD/other competitors to support the same API. So instead of competing at the silicon level, Nvidia chooses to set up unnecessary barriers to entry at massive cost to developers/users. Like Torvalds says [1]: Fuck You, Nvidia. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24013163,"author":"vngzs","month":"2020-07","story":"Nvidia is reportedly in ‘advanced talks’ to buy ARM for more than $32B","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds quote on Nvidia as worst hardware vendor.","quote":"Nvidia is bad because it actively undermines open-source.","text":"> The claim that nvidia is bad at open source because it does not open source its Linux driver is also quite wrong [...] Hey! Wait a second, there. Nvidia isn't bad because it has a properietary Linux driver. Nvidia is bad because it actively undermines open-source. Quoting Linus Torvalds (2012) [0]: > I'm also happy to very publicly point out that Nvidia has been one of the worst trouble spots we've had with hardware manufacturers, and that is really sad because then Nvidia tries to sell chips - a lot of chips - into the Android Market. Nvidia has been the single worst company we've ever dealt with. > [Lifts middle finger] So Nvidia, fuck you. Nvidia managed to push some PR blurbs about how it was improving the open-source driver in 2014, but six years later, Nouveau is still crap compared to their proprietary driver [1]. Drew DeVault, on Nvidia support in Sway [2]: > Nvidia, on the other hand, have been fucking assholes and have treated Linux like utter shit for our entire relationship. About a year ago they announced “Wayland support” for their proprietary driver. This included KMS and DRM support (years late, I might add), but not GBM support. They shipped something called EGLStreams instead, a concept that had been discussed and shot down by the Linux graphics development community before. They did this because it makes it easier for them to keep their driver proprietary without having work with Linux developers on it. Without GBM, Nvidia does not support Wayland, and th"},{"id":13144989,"author":"StRoy","month":"2016-12","story":"AMD responds to Linux kernel maintainer's rejection of AMDGPU patch","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds's famous criticism of Nvidia.","quote":"Nvidia has been the single worst company we've ever dealt with.","text":"Without any conflict, uh? [VIDEO] > Nvidia has been the single worst company we've ever dealt with. > - Linus Kernel devs are antagonizing the only two GPU makers that matters. Besides the kernel, there has been some flame going between NVIDIA and Wayland devs too. Open source devs are immature men who do not understand the word compromise."},{"id":11176532,"author":"bryanlarsen","month":"2016-02","story":"GPL Violations Related to Combining ZFS and Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds on Nvidia's binary kernel module.","quote":"","text":"Exactly. They're arguing the combined work clause, which would automatically rule out all binary-only modules, such as nvidia.ko. Most previous arguments about zfs.ko were about the derived work clause, which is much more grey. I belive that nvidia.ko would be more likely to be ruled a derived work than ZFS.ko, but that's hard to tell. It does seem silly to argue about ZFS in this context. ZFS is open source and much less offensive to kernel developers than nvidia.ko.[1] If nvidia.ko is fine, then so is zfs.ko. If nvidia.ko isn't fine, zfs.ko may or may not be fine. Ubuntu has been shipping nvidia.ko for years. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8089541,"author":"Intermernet","month":"2014-07","story":"GCC 4.9 is doing “some seriously crazy shit” according to Linus Torvalds","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds criticizing Nvidia's anti-open-source stance.","quote":"","text":"Yes they're completely orthogonal. Linus (seemingly) doesn't care about a person's race, creed, gender, or sexual preference. He cares that they are breaking Linux ( [LINK] ), spreading misinformation about Linux ( [LINK] ), releasing software that breaks Linux (GCC 4.9.0), or being generally, and consistantly anti open-source ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":6358676,"author":"D9u","month":"2013-09","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Linus being abrasive toward Nvidia.","quote":"Linus has always been abrasive when confronted by what he sees as less than intelligent comments.","text":"How long have you been following the Linux Kernel Mailing List? Linus has always been abrasive when confronted by what he sees as less than intelligent comments. Then there was one of my favorites; When Linus made the following statement to Nvidia. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4471458,"author":"gagan2020","month":"2012-09","story":"Hybrid graphics support in Nvidia 's Linux driver","stance":"reference","why":"Linus Torvalds's remarks about Nvidia.","quote":"","text":"This effort of Nvidia is brand reconciliation for Linux users. After the talk where Linus mentioned Nvidia. [VIDEO]"}]},"icruGcSsPp0":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid demonstration of generation loss from repeated lossy re-encoding.","q":{"x":"Every time you render and recompress you're losing information, it's lossy compression after all.","i":35089967,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38403583,"author":"fnands","month":"2023-11","story":"I Am Sitting in a Room (1969)","stance":"reference","why":"YouTube-compression take on the repeated-degradation concept from 2010.","quote":"A take on it from 2010 but with YouTube compression","text":"A take on it from 2010 but with YouTube compression: [VIDEO] And MKBHD did a take on it in 2019 again with YouTube compression: [LINK]"},{"id":35089967,"author":"vel0city","month":"2023-03","story":"The Quest for Netflix on Asahi Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how repeated lossy re-encoding amplifies artifacts.","quote":"Every time you render and recompress you're losing information, it's lossy compression after all.","text":"You're not understanding how lossy compression encoders work. Try recompressing a JPEG a few dozen times. Or take an MP3 and export it from Audacity, open the export, export as MP3 again a dozen times and see what it sounds like. All those artifacts keep getting amplified every time you re-encode until it's practically just the artifacts. Every time you render and recompress you're losing information, it's lossy compression after all. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33567899,"author":"TonyTrapp","month":"2022-11","story":"Each visit to the page deteriorates the main image","stance":"reference","why":"Modern repeat of the lossy-degradation experiment with video and audio codecs.","quote":"uploading a video 1000 times to YouTube.","text":"The experiment has also been brought to the 21st century and its video and audio codecs: [VIDEO] - uploading a video 1000 times to YouTube."},{"id":23549601,"author":"majewsky","month":"2020-06","story":"No Paint","stance":"reference","why":"Short original version of the repeated-upload degradation experiment.","quote":"this is the first video that did this, and it's only 3 minutes total","text":"If you don't have 15 minutes to waste on exposition, this is the first video that did this, and it's only 3 minutes total: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16418600,"author":"esotericsean","month":"2018-02","story":"Depixelizing Pixel Art (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Video re-uploaded 1000 times showing compression degradation.","quote":"someone uploaded and ripped a YouTube video 1000 times and then edited the natural degradation of the compression","text":"I remember back in like 2010, someone uploaded and ripped a YouTube video 1000 times and then edited the natural degradation of the compression. Went from perfectly watchable video to horrible static-y mess. Here it is! [VIDEO]"}]},"jsLUidiYm0w":{"wy":"Used as shorthand for pointless corporate nagging and soulless office life.","q":{"x":"There is a famous scene, in the movie Office Space (1999)","i":43226907,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43226907,"author":"swat535","month":"2025-03","story":"I'm done with coding","stance":"reference","why":"Famous TPS report memo scene illustrating pointless corporate nagging","quote":"There is a famous scene, in the movie Office Space (1999)","text":"> It was three hours of nearly-continuous meetings, all with the same subject of how bad it was to email a higher-up without first emailing every single person in-between first. There is a famous scene, in the movie Office Space (1999), where the low level coder is constantly harassed about an idiotic memo because he forgot a cover sheet on his TPS reports.. he is reminded of this 3 times and then some by other people.. It's unreal how the \"modern\" corporate environment, remains the same as it was in early 90s.. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40210185,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-04","story":"RMS Lifestyle","stance":"reference","why":"Office Space cubicles as depressing, soulless workplace example","quote":"I remember the cubes in Office Space (1999) characterising IniTech as depressing","text":"> I remember people dressing what today would be quite formally in the office I remember the cubes in Office Space (1999) characterising IniTech as depressing (people dropping by without even needing to knock on the door) and soulless, but maybe today's open plan workers think they look like privacy incarnate? [VIDEO]"},{"id":38488653,"author":"FirmwareBurner","month":"2023-12","story":"The Persistent Myth That Most Americans Are Miserable at Work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Boy, are you out of sync with the cultural zeitgeist, or you're too young to have caught this masterpiece","text":">Filing TPS reports? Idk what that is Boy, are you out of sync with the cultural zeitgeist, or you're too young to have caught this masterpiece: [VIDEO] >but I'd imagine that feels a lot less satisfying than mowing a lawn or trimming some hedges. Satisfying it may be, if it's done as a hobby, but good luck supporting a family today by trimming hedges."},{"id":20562305,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2019-07","story":"The art of interrupting software engineers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory link to video (I can't help it, my name is Peter","text":"Obligatory link to video (I can't help it, my name is Peter, I love this movie and I work in tech lol) [VIDEO]"}]},"rKaEhBjt1ls":{"wy":"HN points to it as the real-world example for the garage orphan problem, meaning EV owners with no home charger.","q":{"x":"Fully Charged did an episode on this","i":20678997,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":45899537,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2025-11","story":"Using street lamps as EV chargers","stance":"reference","why":"Ubitricity system for charging EVs from lamp posts","quote":"Ubitricity is a German based company who've come up with a simple, cheap to install and well managed system","text":"Robert Llewellyn (previously of Red Dwarf fame) covered this idea eight years ago (July 2017) on his 'electrify' channel: > The simple and very commonplace lamp post will soon become a ubiquitous charge point for electric cars. They charge at about 5 kW, or 16 amps, not super fast but overnight charging is all most drivers need. Ubitricity is a German based company who've come up with a simple, cheap to install and well managed system for more people on more streets to adopt electric cars. * [VIDEO] See also pop-up chargers from six years ago: * [LINK]"},{"id":29239775,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-11","story":"Electric vehicles could fully recharge in 5 minutes with new cable design","stance":"reference","why":"Episodes on street lamp plugs and pop-up chargers","quote":"Fully Charged , based out of the UK, had episodes on street lamp plugs, and pop-up chargers","text":"Fully Charged , based out of the UK, had episodes on street lamp plugs, and pop-up chargers: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] Each area/street will probably have to examined individually to find the right solution for it."},{"id":25876630,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-01","story":"Electric vehicles close to ‘tipping point’ of mass adoption","stance":"reference","why":"Street lamp charging option for garage orphans","quote":"there are some options being developed. The YT channel Fully Charged featured two a while back","text":"> The number of people in the UK and Ireland that have to park overnight on the street is way too high to start banning ICE vehicles within the next decade. You're not wrong (and I too am a \"garage orphan\"—neither a garage or even a driveway), but there are some options being developed. The YT channel Fully Charged featured two a while back: * Street lamps: [VIDEO] * Pop-up charger: [LINK] I'm a bit skeptical."},{"id":25876671,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-01","story":"Electric vehicles close to ‘tipping point’ of mass adoption","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reasonable concern, though: 1. If every car has one, they become a commodity, so how much value will they actually end up having? 2. There are locking cables available: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":25102766,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-11","story":"UK to ban sale of new petrol and diesel cars from 2030: FT","stance":"reference","why":"Example of light pole charging","quote":"If anyone wants to see an example of light pole charging, the Fully Charged YT channel had an episode of one example","text":"> All new street lights (as of a couple of years ago) have to have charging points built-in […] If anyone wants to see an example of light pole charging, the Fully Charged YT channel had an episode of one example: * [VIDEO] See also pop-up chargers for streets without poles: * [LINK]"},{"id":20678997,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2019-08","story":"Where will ‘garage orphans’ charge electric cars?","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on charging from street lamp posts","quote":"Fully Charged did an episode on this","text":"Fully Charged did an episode on this: * [VIDEO] However, in many cities the posts are not in the sidewalk, but on people's properties, so as to allow clearer pedestrian paths. This is generally true in Toronto (where the story is written), especially on residential side-streets."},{"id":20679061,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2019-08","story":"Where will ‘garage orphans’ charge electric cars?","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on Ubitricity lamp post charging","quote":"Fully Charged had an episode on Ubitricity","text":"Fully Charged had an episode on Ubitricity: * [VIDEO] This mostly works if the posts are closer to the curb, but in some places they are on people's front lawns so the sidewalks are completely clear."},{"id":19363029,"author":"jasoncartwright","month":"2019-03","story":"Tesla to raise prices and keep more stores open","stance":"reference","why":"Ubitricity lamp post charging with council and CEO","quote":"Source: , from Fully Charged. Video features a council official (who own the lights) and Ubitricity CEO.","text":"This. I get more than enough juice out of an Ubitricity socket for an overnight charge. Here is a screenshot of the Tesla app showing the car charging on an Ubitricity plug.... [LINK] Source: [VIDEO] , from Fully Charged. Video features a council official (who own the lights) and Ubitricity CEO."}]},"sSF81yjVbJE":{"wy":"HN uses it as evidence of SpaceX's early full-reusability concept.","m":[{"id":26267760,"author":"bryanlarsen","month":"2021-02","story":"New Glenn’s Progress Towards Maiden Flight","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX's scrapped Falcon 9 second stage reuse mockup","quote":"SpaceX mocked up a plan to reuse the second stage of Falcon 9.","text":"I feel fairly certain that Blue Origin has plans for second stage reuse. Whether these plans have gone much past the napkin phase is another question. SpaceX mocked up a plan to reuse the second stage of Falcon 9. [VIDEO] These plans were scrapped to concentrate on Starship. If the Falcon 9 is big enough to have enough margin to support second stage reuse, New Glenn definitely does too."},{"id":10925097,"author":"zardo","month":"2016-01","story":"SpaceX Falcon rocket explodes on landing after delivering satellite to space","stance":"reference","why":"Early SpaceX full reusability concept","quote":"It certainly was at the start.","text":"It certainly was at the start. [VIDEO] Apparently they are no longer pursuing stage 2 reuse on the Falcon 9. The next gen superheavy lift rocket, the BFR, is intended to be fully reusable though."},{"id":8073369,"author":"ejr","month":"2014-07","story":"SpaceX Soft Lands Falcon 9 Rocket First Stage","stance":"reference","why":"Archived copy of old SpaceX reusability video","quote":"I found a copy uploaded to YouTube in 2011","text":"Their website has been changed quite a bit and old linked videos are hard to find. I found a copy uploaded to YouTube in 2011 : [VIDEO] Edit: Found the original copy on the Web Archive [LINK]"},{"id":4049050,"author":"john-n","month":"2012-05","story":"Dragon has successfully achieved splashdown","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX concept animation of reusable launch","quote":"Not done by spacex, they made this vid to demonstration the idea","text":"Not done by spacex, they made this vid to demonstration the idea - [VIDEO] I dont know of any ship that has the ability to land like that, except for craft in development like from blue origins - [LINK] I believe the Soyuz uses rockets to slow its decent before it hits the ground, but thats to assist the parachute rather than replace them."}]},"wSIWpFPkYrk":{"wy":"Posted as a joke whenever laser anti-mosquito devices come up, as an early internet gem.","m":[{"id":44478732,"author":"CyberThijs","month":"2025-07","story":"Laser-wielding device is like an anti-aircraft system for mosquitoes","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"one of those early-internet gems","text":"This product brings back fond memories to one of those early-internet gems: the \"Star Wars mosquito defence system\" gag infomercial that was launched 18 years (!) ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28796951,"author":"agent327","month":"2021-10","story":"Laser for control of mosquitos, weeds and pests","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Ah, someone finally implemented this","text":"Ah, someone finally implemented this: [VIDEO] How does it do against other 'annoying elements in your surroundings'? ;-)"},{"id":26377168,"author":"aequitas","month":"2021-03","story":"Raspberry Pi for Kill Mosquitoes by Laser","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Never thought I would even see the \"Starwars Musquito Defense System\" being actually developed :) [VIDEO]"}]},"ybSzoLCCX-Y":{"wy":"HN commenters post it as a punchline whenever a thread turns to some 'gap' or to surviving nuclear catastrophe.","m":[{"id":40292626,"author":"jasomill","month":"2024-05","story":"US ICBM Launch Center Virtual Tour","stance":"reference","why":"Scene from Dr. Strangelove about surviving near-total destruction.","quote":"this scene from Dr. Strangelove comes to mind","text":"Indefinitely, even in the case of near-total destruction. While not exactly historical fiction, this scene from Dr. Strangelove comes to mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40516645,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-05","story":"What would happen if a nuclear bomb went off in your backyard","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Wherefore I praised the dead which are already dead more than the living which are yet alive. —not President Merkin Muffley See [VIDEO] Lagniappe: [LINK]"},{"id":39906316,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-04","story":"World Population to fall for first time since the Black Death","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it's \"breed more prodigously\"","text":"> immediately breed more and faster than ever According to [Kubrick64] it's \"breed more prodigously\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":36371405,"author":"giardini","month":"2023-06","story":"Humans Have Pumped Enough Groundwater to Change the Tilt of the Earth","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Are we suffering a \"groundwater gap\"?8-))","text":"But can we use this as a weapon of war? Are we suffering a \"groundwater gap\"?8-)) mineshaft gap (in Dr.Strangelove): [VIDEO]"},{"id":34946598,"author":"giardini","month":"2023-02","story":"Lab Leak Most Likely Origin of Covid-19 Pandemic, U.S. Agency Now Says","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"We now have a \"BSL-4 lab gap","text":"So the only solution is to build my own BSL-4 lab and live inside it. We now have a \"BSL-4 lab gap\": \"We must not allow a mine-shaft gap!: [VIDEO] [I miss Peter Sellers!]"},{"id":33770018,"author":"zapataband1","month":"2022-11","story":"The AI War and How to Win It","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the real question is do we still have a mineshaft gap?","text":"[VIDEO] the real question is do we still have a mineshaft gap? hope we figure out how to get along in our only home."},{"id":23712008,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-07","story":"Studies in Intelligence [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Dr. Strangelove : \"we must not allow a mineshaft gap\"","text":"Dr. Strangelove : \"we must not allow a mineshaft gap\": [VIDEO] A Boy and His Dog would continue the trope. for the serious original, see p11 of [LINK]"}]}});
